"use strict";(self.webpackChunkglobal_blog=self.webpackChunkglobal_blog||[]).push([["6607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"stanford-cs146s-1-modern-software-developer","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/stanford-cs146s-1-modern-software-developer","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-22-stanford-cs146s-1/index.md","title":"[Stanford CS146S #1] Course Overview: The Modern Software Developer \u2014 The Shift from Typist to Conductor in the Age of AI Agents","description":"Stanford CS146S Lecture 1 comprehensive guide: Demystifying the seismic paradigm shift in modern software engineering from manual syntax typing to orchestrating autonomous AI agent workflows via Spec-Driven Development and Loop Engineering.","date":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"stanford-cs146s","permalink":"/blog/tags/stanford-cs-146-s"},{"inline":true,"label":"agentic-workflows","permalink":"/blog/tags/agentic-workflows"},{"inline":true,"label":"spec-driven-development","permalink":"/blog/tags/spec-driven-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"human-agent-engineering","permalink":"/blog/tags/human-agent-engineering"},{"inline":true,"label":"mcp","permalink":"/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"software-architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/software-architecture"}],"readingTime":7.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"stanford-cs146s-1-modern-software-developer","title":"[Stanford CS146S #1] Course Overview: The Modern Software Developer \u2014 The Shift from Typist to Conductor in the Age of AI Agents","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["stanford-cs146s","agentic-workflows","spec-driven-development","human-agent-engineering","mcp","software-architecture"],"date":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Stanford CS146S","Agentic Workflows","Spec-Driven Development","Human-Agent Engineering","Model Context Protocol (MCP)"],"description":"Stanford CS146S Lecture 1 comprehensive guide: Demystifying the seismic paradigm shift in modern software engineering from manual syntax typing to orchestrating autonomous AI agent workflows via Spec-Driven Development and Loop Engineering."},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Custom Domain on GitHub Pages #1: Zero-Downtime Migration with Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS (CNAME Flattening & Universal SSL)","permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns"}},"content":"![Stanford CS146S Modern Software Developer](./images/01_cs146s_human_agent_orchestration.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWelcome to the comprehensive technical breakdown of **Stanford University\'s CS146S: The Modern Software Developer**. \\r\\n\\r\\nFor the past five decades, computer science education has trained developers to be meticulous typists: memorizing syntactic permutations, writing algorithmic routines from scratch, and manually hand-crafting thousands of lines of boilerplate code. Today, the software engineering industry is undergoing its most radical transformation since the invention of the compiler: the transition from **Manual Coding** to **Human-Agent Engineering**.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn this foundational inaugural lecture, we unpack how high-velocity engineering organizations (Google, Meta, OpenAI) are redefining developer productivity, why the developer is transforming from a code typist into an orchestra conductor, and how **Spec-Driven Development (SDD)**, **Agentic Workflows**, and the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** constitute the indispensable toolkit for the modern engineer.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Paradigm Shift: From Syntax Typist to System Conductor\\r\\n\\r\\nIn traditional software engineering workflows, the primary bottleneck has always been human keyboard throughput and cognitive syntax recall. An engineer conceives an architectural intent, translates that intent into concrete language tokens (Java, TypeScript, Python), and spends 70% of their time navigating mundane plumbing: defining schema interfaces, wiring REST controllers, writing boilerplate CRUD tests, and fixing trivial syntactic typos.\\r\\n\\r\\nWith the advent of foundation reasoning models and autonomous coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor, GitHub Copilot Workspace), the generation of syntactically valid code has effectively reached near-zero marginal cost.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph Traditional_Workflow [\\"Traditional Manual Paradigm: Developer as Typist\\"]\\r\\n        A1[Human Architectural Idea] --\x3e|Manual Typing 70% Time| B1[Boilerplate Syntax & Plumbing]\\r\\n        B1 --\x3e|Manual Test Writing| C1[Local Compilation & Debugging]\\r\\n        C1 --\x3e|Slow Linear Velocity| D1[Single Feature Delivery]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Modern_Agentic_Workflow [\\"Stanford CS146S Paradigm: Developer as Conductor\\"]\\r\\n        A2[Human Architect: Specification & Invariants] ==>|High-Level Spec.md| B2[Autonomous AI Agent Squad]\\r\\n        B2 --\x3e|Multi-File Edit via MCP| C2[Automated Loop Engineering: Test & Self-Heal]\\r\\n        C2 --\x3e|Continuous Feedback| B2\\r\\n        C2 ==>|Verified Code Base| D2[Human Architectural & Security Review]\\r\\n        D2 ==>|10x Scaled Velocity| E2[Production Deployment]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nModern developers are no longer evaluated by how many lines of code (LOC) they write per day. Instead, they operate as **Engineering Managers presiding over a squad of autonomous AI agents**. When an engineering manager delegates work to junior engineers, they do not dictate every keypress. Instead, they provide unambiguous specifications, outline system boundaries, and define deterministic acceptance criteria.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Spec-Driven Development (SDD): \\"What\\" Over \\"How\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nThe central methodology championed by Stanford CS146S is **Spec-Driven Development (SDD)**. In naive AI-assisted coding, developers interact with models through chatty, conversational prompts like: *\\"Can you make a user login system?\\"* \\r\\n\\r\\nThis approach invariably leads to architectural divergence, code hallucination, and missing edge-case handling. Under SDD, the primary engineering deliverable is not the raw code, but a formal, machine-actionable **Specification Document (`SPEC.md`)**.\\r\\n\\r\\n```markdown\\r\\n# Engineering Specification Template (SPEC.md)\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Context & Business Domain\\r\\n- Implement idempotent payment webhook processing for Stripe API v2024-06.\\r\\n- Database: PostgreSQL 16 with Row-Level Security (RLS) enabled.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Invariants & Non-Negotiable Constraints\\r\\n- All database mutations MUST occur inside an explicit database transaction.\\r\\n- Webhook signature validation (HMAC-SHA256) MUST fail closed before any payload parsing.\\r\\n- Latency threshold: P99 response time < 80ms.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Interfaces & Schemas\\r\\n- Input: `POST /api/v1/payments/webhook` with raw request body buffer.\\r\\n- Event Types Handled: `payment_intent.succeeded`, `payment_intent.payment_failed`.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Verification & Test Suite Requirements\\r\\n- Unit test coverage > 95% for transaction rollbacks on duplicate event IDs.\\r\\n- Concurrency test: 50 concurrent duplicate webhooks MUST produce exactly 1 database insert.\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nBy investing deep cognitive effort into crafting rigorous specifications, human developers establish unbreakable guardrails. The AI agent is given total autonomy over the *\\"How\\"* (synthesizing the implementation), while the human retains absolute authority over the *\\"What\\"* and the *\\"Why\\"*.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Agentic Workflows & Automated Loop Engineering\\r\\n\\r\\nA defining concept introduced in Lecture 1 is **Loop Engineering**. Foundation models are probabilistic engines; expecting an LLM to generate a complex, multi-file distributed system in a single forward pass without errors is mathematically flawed.\\r\\n\\r\\nInstead of expecting one-shot perfection, modern software engineering structures the agent workflow into a closed, self-correcting autonomous loop:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nstateDiagram-v2\\r\\n    [*] --\x3e Spec_Ingestion: Human Supplies SPEC.md\\r\\n    Spec_Ingestion --\x3e Workspace_Planning: Agent Maps Codebase Graph\\r\\n    \\r\\n    state Workspace_Planning {\\r\\n        [*] --\x3e IdentifyDependencies\\r\\n        IdentifyDependencies --\x3e FormulatePatchPlan\\r\\n    }\\r\\n\\r\\n    Workspace_Planning --\x3e Multi_File_Edit: Execute Code Modifications\\r\\n    Multi_File_Edit --\x3e Automated_Verification: Trigger CI/CD & Unit Test Suite\\r\\n    \\r\\n    state Automated_Verification {\\r\\n        [*] --\x3e RunTests\\r\\n        RunTests --\x3e InspectExitCode\\r\\n    }\\r\\n\\r\\n    Automated_Verification --\x3e Self_Correction: Test Failure or Lint Error\\r\\n    Self_Correction --\x3e Multi_File_Edit: Inject Stack Trace into Agent Context\\r\\n    \\r\\n    Automated_Verification --\x3e Human_Review: All Tests Pass (Exit Code 0)\\r\\n    Human_Review --\x3e Merge_Production: Architectural & Security Sign-off\\r\\n    Merge_Production --\x3e [*]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n#### The Four Stages of the Autonomous Loop:\\r\\n1. **Planning (Workspace Discovery)**: The agent traverses the project file graph, reading interface contracts and schema definitions.\\r\\n2. **Execution (Multi-File Transformation)**: The agent applies diffs across client, server, and migration files simultaneously.\\r\\n3. **Verification (Deterministic Feedback)**: The agent autonomously invokes test runners (`pytest`, `vitest`, `cargo test`) and linters.\\r\\n4. **Self-Correction (Loop Iteration)**: When an assertion fails, the agent intercepts the stdout/stderr trace, analyzes the root cause, and edits the code until all test suites pass.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Model Context Protocol (MCP): The Universal Agent Protocol\\r\\n\\r\\nHow does an AI model escape the isolated text sandbox and interact with real-world developer tools? The answer lies in the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**, the open industry standard for agentic tool integration.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nsequenceDiagram\\r\\n    autonumber\\r\\n    actor Dev as Developer (Conductor)\\r\\n    participant IDE as AI Native IDE / Agent Host\\r\\n    participant MCP as MCP Server Gateway\\r\\n    participant FS as Local Filesystem & Git\\r\\n    participant DB as Development Database\\r\\n    participant CI as Test Runner / Linter\\r\\n\\r\\n    Dev->>IDE: Execute Spec (\\"Migrate Auth to JWT\\")\\r\\n    IDE->>MCP: List Available Tools & Resources\\r\\n    MCP--\x3e>IDE: Tools: [read_file, write_patch, run_tests, query_db]\\r\\n    \\r\\n    loop Loop Engineering Iteration\\r\\n        IDE->>MCP: Call tool: \\"read_file(/src/auth.py)\\"\\r\\n        MCP->>FS: Fetch file buffer\\r\\n        FS--\x3e>MCP: Raw file stream\\r\\n        MCP--\x3e>IDE: File content in Context Window\\r\\n        \\r\\n        IDE->>MCP: Call tool: \\"write_patch(/src/auth.py, diff)\\"\\r\\n        MCP->>FS: Apply atomic diff\\r\\n        \\r\\n        IDE->>MCP: Call tool: \\"run_tests(\'tests/test_auth.py\')\\"\\r\\n        MCP->>CI: Spawn subprocess pytest\\r\\n        CI--\x3e>MCP: Test Output: 1 Failed (Missing Secret Key)\\r\\n        MCP--\x3e>IDE: Error log feedback\\r\\n        \\r\\n        IDE->>IDE: Reason: Secret key missing in config.py\\r\\n        IDE->>MCP: Call tool: \\"write_patch(/src/config.py)\\"\\r\\n        MCP->>FS: Apply patch\\r\\n        \\r\\n        IDE->>MCP: Call tool: \\"run_tests(\'tests/test_auth.py\')\\"\\r\\n        MCP->>CI: Spawn subprocess pytest\\r\\n        CI--\x3e>MCP: Test Output: 100% Passed (Exit 0)\\r\\n    end\\r\\n    \\r\\n    IDE--\x3e>Dev: Ready for Review: Clean Git Diff & All Tests Passing\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThrough MCP, coding agents can query PostgreSQL schemas, inspect runtime logs, trigger headless browser tests, and execute git worktree checkouts without human manual copying and pasting.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Architectural Comparison: Typist vs. Conductor\\r\\n\\r\\n| Engineering Dimension | Traditional Paradigm (The Code Typist) | Modern Stanford CS146S Paradigm (The AI Conductor) |\\r\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **Primary Skillset** | Syntactic fluency, manual typing speed, regex memorization | System architecture, specification writing, threat modeling |\\r\\n| **Development Unit** | Functions, lines of code, individual file edits | Whole-system specifications, invariants, automated test contracts |\\r\\n| **Tooling** | Passive text editors, standard linters, manual debuggers | Autonomous agents (Claude Code, Cursor), MCP servers, CI/CD loops |\\r\\n| **Primary Bottleneck** | Typing speed, cognitive fatigue, manual boilerplate writing | Ambiguous specifications, incomplete test coverage, prompt leakage |\\r\\n| **Verification Strategy** | Manual step-through debugging, console.log printouts | Automated deterministic test suites, invariant fuzzing, property tests |\\r\\n| **Failure Modes** | Syntax errors, missed semicolons, off-by-one loops | Semantic hallucination, architectural drift, subtle security oversights |\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 6. Deep Q&A: Why Fundamentals Matter More Than Ever\\r\\n\\r\\nDuring the inaugural lecture at Stanford, students raised a critical question that every computer science major is asking today:\\r\\n\\r\\n> **Q. Professor, if AI agents can write 80% of our code in seconds, do we still need to study algorithms, memory management, and data structures?**\\r\\n\\r\\nThe faculty answer is profound and decisive: **Computer science fundamentals are significantly more critical today than they were a decade ago.**\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **The Reviewer\'s Burden**: Generating 1,000 lines of plausible code takes an AI 15 seconds. Reviewing that code to detect an $O(N^2)$ algorithmic trap, an unclosed database connection pool, a subtle race condition, or a cryptographic vulnerability requires **profound, master-level understanding of systems programming**.\\r\\n2. **Accountability Remains 100% Human**: An AI agent cannot be held legally or professionally liable when a production service crashes or customer data leaks. The human engineer whose name is on the pull request bears total responsibility.\\r\\n3. **Debugging the Edge Cases**: When an autonomous agent gets stuck in a self-correction loop, only an engineer with deep knowledge of operating systems, network protocols, and hardware architectures can step in and diagnose the underlying impedance mismatch.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 7. Lecture 1 Takeaways & Series Roadmap\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Redefine Your Identity**: Stop thinking of yourself as a code author. You are an architect, a spec designer, and an agent manager.\\r\\n2. **Master Spec-Driven Development**: Treat `SPEC.md` as your primary high-leverage engineering artifact.\\r\\n3. **Embrace Loop Engineering**: Shift from expecting single-shot AI answers to designing self-healing loops backed by rigorous unit tests.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn **Lecture 2**, we will dive into **Context Management & Prompt Architecture**: how to structure multi-thousand-token context windows, optimize repository maps, and prevent context saturation during complex multi-file feature additions. Stay tuned to GlobalDevLog!"},{"id":"github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-21-github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns/index.md","title":"Custom Domain on GitHub Pages #1: Zero-Downtime Migration with Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS (CNAME Flattening & Universal SSL)","description":"A step-by-step production guide to ditching free github.io subdomains, acquiring an apex custom domain via Porkbun, delegating to Cloudflare Anycast DNS, configuring CNAME flattening, and setting up Docusaurus without breaking HTTPS.","date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"github-pages","permalink":"/blog/tags/github-pages"},{"inline":true,"label":"custom-domain","permalink":"/blog/tags/custom-domain"},{"inline":true,"label":"porkbun","permalink":"/blog/tags/porkbun"},{"inline":true,"label":"cloudflare","permalink":"/blog/tags/cloudflare"},{"inline":true,"label":"docusaurus","permalink":"/blog/tags/docusaurus"},{"inline":true,"label":"devops","permalink":"/blog/tags/devops"}],"readingTime":4.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbjihwan","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns","title":"Custom Domain on GitHub Pages #1: Zero-Downtime Migration with Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS (CNAME Flattening & Universal SSL)","authors":["jbjihwan"],"tags":["github-pages","custom-domain","porkbun","cloudflare","docusaurus","devops"],"keywords":["Docusaurus GitHub Pages Custom Domain","Cloudflare DNS Porkbun CNAME setup","CNAME Flattening apex domain GitHub Pages","Cloudflare Full SSL Edge Certificate","static CNAME deployment reset bug fix"],"description":"A step-by-step production guide to ditching free github.io subdomains, acquiring an apex custom domain via Porkbun, delegating to Cloudflare Anycast DNS, configuring CNAME flattening, and setting up Docusaurus without breaking HTTPS."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Stanford CS146S #1] Course Overview: The Modern Software Developer \u2014 The Shift from Typist to Conductor in the Age of AI Agents","permalink":"/blog/stanford-cs146s-1-modern-software-developer"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Web Monetization #2] Automating Google AdSense Approval on Static Sites: Fixing the ads.txt 404 Crawl Trap & HeadTags Integration","permalink":"/blog/static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide"}},"content":"Hosting an engineering blog on GitHub Pages under `username.github.io/project` is one of the most cost-effective and friction-free setups for developers. You push markdown files, Git workflows trigger, and GitHub serves your static artifacts worldwide. \\n\\nHowever, as soon as you consider serious branding and monetization, you hit an uncompromising roadblock: **Google AdSense strictly bans free secondary subdomains like `*.github.io` from site application**. Furthermore, any search engine authority (PageRank and Domain Authority) you build remains tied to GitHub\'s domain rather than your personal asset.\\n\\nTo build a professional engineering presence, you need an **apex custom domain** (e.g., `globaldevlog.com`). In this first installment of our static blog infrastructure series, we walk through the exact zero-downtime migration blueprint from `jbjihwan.github.io/global_blog` to `https://globaldevlog.com`, powered by a high-availability **Porkbun + Cloudflare + GitHub Pages 3-tier architecture**.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n![Production DNS & CDN Routing Architecture for Static Engineering Blog](./images/01_dns_routing_architecture.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Architectural Philosophy: The 3-Tier Production Setup\\n\\nThe naive approach to connecting a custom domain to GitHub Pages is pointing raw `A` records from your registrar directly to GitHub\'s Anycast IP addresses (`185.199.108.153`, `185.199.109.153`, `185.199.110.153`, `185.199.111.153`). \\n\\nWhile functional, this legacy approach has significant operational flaws:\\n- No edge DDoS protection or bot challenge filtering.\\n- Slow DNS propagation when switching underlying infrastructure.\\n- Zero fine-grained caching control over static assets.\\n\\nWe decouple responsibilities across three specialized providers:\\n\\n| Tier | Provider | Core Responsibilities |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1. Registrar** | **Porkbun** | Lowest acquisition & annual renewal fees, perpetual free WHOIS privacy, DNSSEC |\\n| **2. Edge DNS & Security** | **Cloudflare** | Global Anycast DNS (< 10ms resolution), **CNAME Flattening on apex (`@`)**, Universal SSL, Bot Fight mode |\\n| **3. Static Origin** | **GitHub Pages** | Pre-compiled Docusaurus artifacts, automated CI/CD via `gh-pages` branch, zero hosting cost |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Step 1: Purchasing via Porkbun and Delegating NameServers\\n\\n1. **Domain Acquisition**: Acquire your target `.com` domain on Porkbun. Porkbun offers transparent pricing with no hidden renewal markups.\\n2. **Add Domain to Cloudflare**: Log into Cloudflare, click **Add Site**, enter your root domain (`globaldevlog.com`), and select the **Free Plan**.\\n3. **Obtain Edge Nameservers**: Cloudflare assigns a dedicated pair of authoritative nameservers, for example:\\n   ```text\\n   roan.ns.cloudflare.com\\n   sneh.ns.cloudflare.com\\n   ```\\n4. **Delegate in Porkbun**: In the Porkbun domain management dashboard:\\n   - Locate **Authoritative Nameservers**.\\n   - Remove Porkbun\'s default records (`fwd0.porkbun.com`, etc.).\\n   - Paste Cloudflare\'s two nameserver addresses and save. Propagation typically finishes within 5 to 15 minutes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Step 2: Cloudflare CNAME Flattening & Full SSL Mode\\n\\nAccording to RFC 1034, an apex/naked domain (`@`) cannot be configured as a `CNAME` record because it must coexist with `SOA` and `NS` records at the zone apex. Traditionally, this forced developers to use brittle `A` records.\\n\\nCloudflare solves this via **CNAME Flattening**: at runtime, Cloudflare\'s edge DNS resolves the CNAME target (`username.github.io`) into IP addresses and returns them directly to DNS queries, providing full CNAME benefits on the naked apex.\\n\\n### A. Configure DNS Records\\nIn the Cloudflare dashboard under **[DNS] > [Records]**, create two proxied records:\\n\\n| Type | Name | Target | Proxy Status | TTL |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **CNAME** | `@` | `jbjihwan.github.io` | **Proxied (Orange Cloud)** | Auto |\\n| **CNAME** | `www` | `jbjihwan.github.io` | **Proxied (Orange Cloud)** | Auto |\\n\\n> **Proxy Status Must Be Enabled**: Keeping the orange cloud active routes all incoming traffic through Cloudflare\'s global Anycast edge, providing instant SSL termination, compression (Brotli), and automated HTTPS redirects.\\n\\n### B. Configure SSL/TLS Encryption\\nNavigate to Cloudflare\'s **[SSL/TLS] > [Overview]**:\\n- Set the encryption mode to **Full** (or **Full Strict**).\\n- This ensures complete encryption across the entire route: from the user\'s browser to Cloudflare Edge, and from Cloudflare Edge to GitHub Pages over TLS.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Step 3: Docusaurus Configuration Update\\n\\nWhen moving from a GitHub repository subpath (e.g. `/global_blog/`) to an apex domain root, you must update `docusaurus.config.ts`:\\n\\n```typescript\\nconst config: Config = {\\n  title: \'Global Dev Blog\',\\n  tagline: \'Engineering Deep Dives in AI, Robotics, Computer Vision\',\\n\\n  // Update URL to the new apex custom domain\\n  url: \'https://globaldevlog.com\',\\n  // Change baseUrl from subpath \'/global_blog/\' to root \'/\'\\n  baseUrl: \'/\',\\n\\n  organizationName: \'jbjihwan\',\\n  projectName: \'global_blog\',\\n  trailingSlash: false,\\n  // ...\\n};\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Step 4: The GitHub Pages CNAME Reset Trap & Solution\\n\\nOne of the most maddening bugs when connecting GitHub Pages to a custom domain is the **silent CNAME wipe**:\\n1. You enter `globaldevlog.com` in GitHub\'s repository **Settings > Pages > Custom domain**.\\n2. You run `npm run deploy` from your local machine.\\n3. Suddenly, your custom domain resets to `username.github.io/project`, and visitors get 404s or SSL certificate mismatches!\\n\\n### Why This Happens\\nGitHub Pages expects a plain text `CNAME` file in the root of the deployment branch (`gh-pages`). When Docusaurus runs `deploy`, it builds fresh artifacts into `build/` and force-pushes that folder to `gh-pages`. If the `CNAME` file is not inside `build/`, the deployment obliterates the previous `CNAME` commit on GitHub.\\n\\n### The Permanent Fix\\nPlace your domain directly inside `static/CNAME`:\\n\\n```text\\nglobaldevlog.com\\n```\\n\\nBecause Docusaurus automatically copies everything in `static/` to the build output root during compilation, your `CNAME` file is committed to `gh-pages` on every single release. The domain connection will never drop again.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Live Production Verification\\n\\nRun the deployment command:\\n```bash\\nnpm run deploy\\n```\\n\\nOnce deployment succeeds, run quick terminal checks to verify headers and SSL certificate authenticity:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -I https://globaldevlog.com\\n# HTTP/2 200\\n# server: cloudflare\\n# strict-transport-security: max-age=31536000\\n\\ncurl -I https://www.globaldevlog.com\\n# HTTP/2 200 or 301 Redirect to apex\\n```\\n\\n### Summary of What We Achieved\\n- **Zero Server Overhead**: The entire platform runs completely free on GitHub Pages.\\n- **Enterprise CDN & Protection**: Cloudflare absorbs traffic spikes, DDoS attempts, and manages SSL certificates automatically.\\n- **Monetization & Search Independence**: The domain is ready for Google AdSense review and search engine ownership verification.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## \u{1F51C} Next in This Series\\n\\nWith your apex custom domain live, the next critical milestones are monetization approval and multi-engine visibility:\\n- **Part 2**: *[Static Site Monetization #2] Google AdSense Approval on Docusaurus: ads.txt 404 Resolution and Asynchronous Script Injection*\\n- **Part 3**: *[Multi-Search Engine SEO #3] Google, Naver & Bing Webmaster Setup: Sitemap Deletion Quirks and Host Error Troubleshooting*"},{"id":"static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-21-static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide/index.md","title":"[Web Monetization #2] Automating Google AdSense Approval on Static Sites: Fixing the ads.txt 404 Crawl Trap & HeadTags Integration","description":"A comprehensive developer guide to configuring Google AdSense ownership verification on Docusaurus, deploying static ads.txt to root, and preventing Cloudflare WAF crawl dropouts.","date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"google-adsense","permalink":"/blog/tags/google-adsense"},{"inline":true,"label":"docusaurus","permalink":"/blog/tags/docusaurus"},{"inline":true,"label":"github-pages","permalink":"/blog/tags/github-pages"},{"inline":true,"label":"web-monetization","permalink":"/blog/tags/web-monetization"},{"inline":true,"label":"devops","permalink":"/blog/tags/devops"},{"inline":true,"label":"cloudflare","permalink":"/blog/tags/cloudflare"},{"inline":true,"label":"seo","permalink":"/blog/tags/seo"}],"readingTime":5.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide","title":"[Web Monetization #2] Automating Google AdSense Approval on Static Sites: Fixing the ads.txt 404 Crawl Trap & HeadTags Integration","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["google-adsense","docusaurus","github-pages","web-monetization","devops","cloudflare","seo"],"date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","project":"devops","description":"A comprehensive developer guide to configuring Google AdSense ownership verification on Docusaurus, deploying static ads.txt to root, and preventing Cloudflare WAF crawl dropouts.","keywords":["Google AdSense ads.txt Error Fix","Docusaurus Google AdSense Integration","Static Site AdSense Approval","GitHub Pages ads.txt Deployment","Cloudflare WAF Googlebot Crawler"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Custom Domain on GitHub Pages #1: Zero-Downtime Migration with Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS (CNAME Flattening & Universal SSL)","permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns"},"nextItem":{"title":"From GitHub Pages to Custom Domain: Complete Guide to Porkbun, Cloudflare DNS, AdSense Approval, and Multi-Engine SEO","permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide"}},"content":"![Google AdSense Approval and Crawler Pipeline](./images/01_ads_txt_crawler_flow.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **The Problem**: Unlike traditional dynamic CMS platforms (WordPress, Ghost), static site generators (Docusaurus, Next.js SSG, Hugo) hosted on GitHub Pages or Cloudflare Pages frequently trigger the notorious **\\"Earnings at Risk: ads.txt file missing\\"** alert and fail site ownership checks due to client-side hydration delays and crawler blocking.\\n> - **The Solution**: Inject the publisher verification meta tag directly into `<head>` using Docusaurus `headTags` configuration, place the plain `ads.txt` file in `static/ads.txt` for root-level deployment, and configure Cloudflare WAF bypass rules for the `Googlebot-AdsTxt` crawler.\\n> - **The Result**: 100% automated crawler accessibility, zero 404 crawl errors on `/ads.txt`, seamless AdSense review compliance, and sustained Lighthouse SEO and performance scores above 95.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The Static Site AdSense Dilemma\\n\\nTransitioning a developer blog from a raw sub-path (`username.github.io/blog`) to a production apex domain (such as `https://globaldevlog.com`) unlocks the gateway to **Google AdSense monetization**. However, modern Static Site Generators (SSGs) introduce unique architectural quirks that dynamic platforms never encounter:\\n\\n1. **No Backend Database or Plugin Ecosystem**: WordPress handles ownership verification and `ads.txt` via plugins like Site Kit. In Docusaurus, every asset must be explicitly declared at build time.\\n2. **Client-Side Hydration vs. Static Head Injection**: Injecting scripts through raw React component lifecycles (`useEffect`) causes delays. Search engine crawlers and AdSense verification bots inspect raw HTML responses before JavaScript execution.\\n3. **The `ads.txt` 404 Crawl Trap**: Misplacing `ads.txt` within build subdirectories prevents the AdSense crawler (`Googlebot-AdsTxt`) from reading `https://yourdomain.com/ads.txt`, jeopardizing domain monetization.\\n\\nLet us walk through the robust, step-by-step engineering solution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Step 1: Injecting Site Ownership Verification in Docusaurus\\n\\nWhen you register your custom domain in the Google AdSense Console (**Sites > Add site**), Google requests domain verification through one of three mechanisms:\\n- A JavaScript code snippet\\n- An HTML `<meta>` tag: `<meta name=\\"google-adsense-account\\" content=\\"ca-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\\">`\\n- An `ads.txt` snippet\\n\\nFor static sites, **injecting the meta tag into `headTags`** is the cleanest, highest-performance approach.\\n\\n### \u{1F4D0} Configuration (`docusaurus.config.ts`)\\n\\nOpen `docusaurus.config.ts` and add the `headTags` entry:\\n\\n```typescript\\nimport type {Config} from \'@docusaurus/types\';\\n\\nconst config: Config = {\\n  title: \'Global Dev Blog\',\\n  url: \'https://globaldevlog.com\',\\n  baseUrl: \'/\',\\n\\n  // Google AdSense Site Verification Meta Tag\\n  headTags: [\\n    {\\n      tagName: \'meta\',\\n      attributes: {\\n        name: \'google-adsense-account\',\\n        content: \'ca-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\', // Replace with your AdSense Publisher ID\\n      },\\n    },\\n  ],\\n\\n  // ... rest of your configuration\\n};\\n\\nexport default config;\\n```\\n\\n### \u{1F4A1} Why `headTags` Outperforms `scripts` During Review\\n* **Zero Performance Tax**: Unlike loading the full `adsbygoogle.js` bundle (which weighs ~120 KB and triggers third-party script warnings in Google PageSpeed Insights), a simple `<meta>` tag executes in 0ms.\\n* **Guaranteed Crawler Accessibility**: It renders natively into the pre-compiled server-rendered static HTML output (`build/index.html`), ensuring instant verification when the Google review bot pings your site.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Step 2: Conquering the ads.txt 404 Crawling Trap\\n\\nWithin 48 to 72 hours of submitting a site, publishers commonly face the dreaded red console alert:\\n> \u26A0\uFE0F *\\"Earnings at risk: You need to fix some ads.txt file issues to avoid severe impact to your revenue.\\"*\\n\\nThe root cause is straightforward: `Googlebot-AdsTxt` queried `https://yourdomain.com/ads.txt` and received an HTTP 404 status.\\n\\n### \u{1F6E0}\uFE0F Root-Level Deployment via `static/ads.txt`\\n\\nIn Docusaurus, all contents inside the root `static/` directory are copied verbatim into the root of the production output directory (`build/`) during compilation.\\n\\n1. Create `static/ads.txt`:\\n   ```text\\n   google.com, pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX, DIRECT, f08c47fec0942fa0\\n   ```\\n   *(Replace `pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX` with your authorized publisher ID).*\\n\\n2. Verify file presence in your git working tree:\\n   ```bash\\n   git status\\n   # Untracked files: static/ads.txt\\n   ```\\n\\n3. Deploy to production:\\n   ```bash\\n   npm run deploy\\n   ```\\n\\n### \u{1F50D} Verification Checklist\\nOnce deployed, open your terminal or browser to verify HTTP headers:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -I https://globaldevlog.com/ads.txt\\n```\\n\\nExpected response:\\n```http\\nHTTP/2 200\\ncontent-type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\\ncache-control: max-age=600\\nserver: cloudflare\\n```\\n\\nConfirm that accessing `https://www.globaldevlog.com/ads.txt` seamlessly redirects (HTTP 301/308) to the apex domain without stripping the request path.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Step 3: Cloudflare WAF & Edge Security Tuning\\n\\nIf your domain routes through Cloudflare with the orange cloud proxy enabled, Cloudflare\'s security layer might inadvertently challenge or block Google\'s automated verification crawlers.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    Bot[\\"Googlebot-AdsTxt Crawler\\"] --\x3e Edge[\\"Cloudflare Edge WAF\\"]\\n    Edge --\x3e|\\"Check: User-Agent & ASN\\"| Rule{\\"WAF Firewall Rule\\"}\\n    Rule --\x3e|\\"Bot Fight Mode (False Positive)\\"| Drop[\\"403 Forbidden / Managed Challenge\\"]\\n    Rule --\x3e|\\"Custom Bypass Expression\\"| Origin[\\"GitHub Pages Origin (/ads.txt)\\"]\\n```\\n\\n### Countermeasures\\n1. **Cloudflare Security > Bots**: If **Super Bot Fight Mode** is active, check the security event log for blocked requests containing User-Agent `Googlebot-AdsTxt` or `Mediapartners-Google`.\\n2. **Create a WAF Custom Rule**:\\n   * **Rule Name**: Allow Google Ads Crawler\\n   * **Expression**:\\n     ```text\\n     (http.user_agent contains \\"Googlebot-AdsTxt\\") or (http.user_agent contains \\"Mediapartners-Google\\")\\n     ```\\n   * **Action**: *Skip / Bypass* (Bypass WAF, Rate Limiting, and Managed Challenge).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. The 4-Pillar Quality Checklist for First-Try AdSense Approval\\n\\nGoogle AdSense rejects up to 80% of personal development blogs under the generic umbrella reason *\\"Low Value Content\\"*. To guarantee approval, verify these structural prerequisites before requesting site review:\\n\\n| Evaluation Pillar | Mandatory Criterion | Failure Mode if Violated |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1. Statutory Compliance Pages** | Comprehensive `/privacy-policy`, `/about`, and `/contact` pages | Site Navigation Policy Violation |\\n| **2. Original Content Volume** | 20+ original in-depth technical articles (1,200+ words each) | Low Value Content / Insufficient Content |\\n| **3. Zero Broken Hyperlinks** | 0 dead links, 0 unresolved anchor tags, 0 404 assets | Site Unreachable / Incomplete Navigation |\\n| **4. Responsive Multi-Viewport** | Fluid mobile/tablet rendering without horizontal layout shift | Substandard Mobile Usability |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Conclusion & Next Steps\\n\\nWith `headTags` injecting your verification metadata, `static/ads.txt` reliably serving your publisher authorization string, and Cloudflare routing verified bots cleanly to GitHub Pages, your static site is now 100% fortified for AdSense review.\\n\\n* In the upcoming **Episode #3**, we will explore multi-engine search indexing: configuring Google Search Console, Naver Search Advisor, and Bing Webmaster Tools across multiple sitemaps!\\n\\n---\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Explore the Production Implementation**:  \\n> \u2022 [Global Dev Blog - GitHub Pages Custom Domain & Cloudflare DNS Guide](https://globaldevlog.com/ko/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-cloudflare-dns)  \\n> \u2022 [Global Dev Blog - Static Site Security & npm audit Vulnerability Truth](https://globaldevlog.com/ko/blog/static-site-security-npm-audit-truth)  \\n> \u2022 [Visit Global Dev Blog Home (Available in 6 Locales)](https://globaldevlog.com/)"},{"id":"github-pages-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-20-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide/index.md","title":"From GitHub Pages to Custom Domain: Complete Guide to Porkbun, Cloudflare DNS, AdSense Approval, and Multi-Engine SEO","description":"A comprehensive production blueprint for migrating a static Docusaurus blog on GitHub Pages to an apex custom domain via Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS, securing Google AdSense verification, and mastering multi-search engine SEO across Google, Naver, and Bing.","date":"2026-10-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"docusaurus","permalink":"/blog/tags/docusaurus"},{"inline":true,"label":"devops","permalink":"/blog/tags/devops"},{"inline":true,"label":"cloudflare","permalink":"/blog/tags/cloudflare"},{"inline":true,"label":"seo","permalink":"/blog/tags/seo"},{"inline":true,"label":"web-monetization","permalink":"/blog/tags/web-monetization"}],"readingTime":5.16,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbjihwan","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"github-pages-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide","title":"From GitHub Pages to Custom Domain: Complete Guide to Porkbun, Cloudflare DNS, AdSense Approval, and Multi-Engine SEO","authors":["jbjihwan"],"tags":["docusaurus","devops","cloudflare","seo","web-monetization"],"keywords":["Docusaurus GitHub Pages Custom Domain","Cloudflare DNS Porkbun CNAME setup","Google AdSense ads.txt static site","Google Search Console sitemap delete troubleshooting","Naver Search Advisor sitemap RSS host error"],"description":"A comprehensive production blueprint for migrating a static Docusaurus blog on GitHub Pages to an apex custom domain via Porkbun and Cloudflare DNS, securing Google AdSense verification, and mastering multi-search engine SEO across Google, Naver, and Bing."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Web Monetization #2] Automating Google AdSense Approval on Static Sites: Fixing the ads.txt 404 Crawl Trap & HeadTags Integration","permalink":"/blog/static-site-adsense-ads-txt-guide"},"nextItem":{"title":"From 45 npm Vulnerabilities to Zero-Risk: Why You Shouldn\'t Blindly Run npm audit fix --force on Static Sites","permalink":"/blog/static-site-security-npm-audit-truth"}},"content":"Hosting a developer blog on GitHub Pages under `username.github.io/project` is one of the quickest ways to share technical insights. However, as your readership grows and you prepare for professional monetization, you hit a hard architectural wall: **Google AdSense strictly prohibits free subdomains like `*.github.io`**. To qualify for AdSense publisher verification, establish domain equity, and secure multi-region organic reach, you need your own apex custom domain.\\n\\nMigrating a static documentation or engineering site to a custom domain is not just a DNS record change. It involves coordinating registrar name delegation, edge SSL termination, canonical redirects, automated publisher ads.txt hosting, and navigating subtle search console UI quirks across global and regional search engines.\\n\\nIn this guide, we break down our end-to-end migration from `jbjihwan.github.io/global_blog` to `https://globaldevlog.com`, including our zero-downtime DNS architecture, AdSense integration, and actionable solutions for real-world indexing traps in Google Search Console, Naver Search Advisor, and Bing Webmaster Tools.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n![Production DNS & CDN Routing Architecture for Static Engineering Blog](./images/01_dns_routing_architecture.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Architectural Strategy: Porkbun + Cloudflare + GitHub Pages\\n\\nWhen connecting an apex domain (e.g., `globaldevlog.com`) to GitHub Pages, the traditional method uses raw `A` records pointing to GitHub\'s four Anycast IPs (`185.199.108.153`, etc.). While functional, this lacks modern edge security, DDoS filtering, and automatic HTTPS certificate renewal.\\n\\nWe adopted a high-availability 3-tier architecture:\\n\\n| Tier | Provider | Core Responsibilities |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Registrar** | **Porkbun** | Domain acquisition, WHOIS privacy protection, DNSSEC, authoritative NS delegation |\\n| **Edge DNS & Security** | **Cloudflare** | Global Anycast DNS, CNAME Flattening on apex (`@`), Universal SSL Edge certificate, Bot Fight mode |\\n| **Origin SSG** | **GitHub Pages** | Static artifact hosting compiled by Docusaurus, zero server maintenance, branch synchronization (`gh-pages`) |\\n\\n### Porkbun NameServer Delegation\\nIn the Porkbun management console, replace default registrar nameservers with Cloudflare\'s assigned edge nameservers:\\n```text\\nNS: roan.ns.cloudflare.com\\nNS: sneh.ns.cloudflare.com\\n```\\n\\n### Cloudflare CNAME Flattening Setup\\nApex domains (`@`) traditionally do not support CNAME records per RFC 1034. Cloudflare resolves this via **CNAME Flattening**, seamlessly resolving `@` to GitHub Pages:\\n- **Type**: `CNAME` | **Name**: `@` | **Target**: `jbjihwan.github.io` | **Proxy status**: Proxied\\n- **Type**: `CNAME` | **Name**: `www` | **Target**: `jbjihwan.github.io` | **Proxy status**: Proxied\\n- **SSL/TLS Mode**: Set to **Full** (or Full Strict) to guarantee end-to-end HTTPS encryption.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Docusaurus Configuration Migration\\n\\nTo transition cleanly from subpath routing (`/global_blog/`) to the domain root (`/`), update `docusaurus.config.ts`:\\n\\n```typescript\\nconst config: Config = {\\n  title: \'Global Dev Blog\',\\n  url: \'https://globaldevlog.com\',\\n  baseUrl: \'/\',\\n  organizationName: \'jbjihwan\',\\n  projectName: \'global_blog\',\\n  trailingSlash: false,\\n  // ...\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### Static CNAME File\\nGitHub Pages requires a plain text `CNAME` file located at the web root of your deployment branch. Place it in `static/CNAME`:\\n```text\\nglobaldevlog.com\\n```\\nDuring `npm run deploy`, Docusaurus automatically copies everything from `static/` to the build root, informing GitHub\'s routing engine to map all incoming traffic for `globaldevlog.com` to this repository.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Google AdSense Approval Setup for Static Sites\\n\\nGoogle AdSense approval requires verifying site ownership, confirming an active publisher script, and crawling a root `ads.txt` file.\\n\\n### A. Publisher Script Injection\\nIn `docusaurus.config.ts`, inject the Google AdSense loader script asynchronously:\\n\\n```typescript\\n  scripts: [\\n    {\\n      src: \'https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-4654073335496617\',\\n      async: true,\\n      crossorigin: \'anonymous\',\\n    },\\n  ],\\n```\\n\\n### B. Direct Root `ads.txt` Deployment\\nPlace your verified publisher credentials in `static/ads.txt`:\\n```text\\ngoogle.com, pub-4654073335496617, DIRECT, f08c47fec0942fa0\\n```\\nVerify live deployment immediately via curl or browser:\\n```bash\\ncurl -I https://globaldevlog.com/ads.txt\\n# HTTP/2 200\\n# content-type: text/plain; charset=utf-8\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n![Global Search Engine Indexing & AdSense Monetization Pipeline](./images/02_indexing_pipeline.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Multi-Search Engine SEO & Real-World Troubleshooting\\n\\nSubmitting sitemaps across multiple engines reveals distinct platform quirks and hidden UI traps. Here is how to conquer them:\\n\\n### A. Google Search Console: The Sitemap Delete Trap\\nWhen submitting localized sitemaps (`sitemap.xml`, `ko/sitemap.xml`, `ja/sitemap.xml`, `es/sitemap.xml`), accidental typos (such as submitting the root URL `https://globaldevlog.com/` as a sitemap) can create permanent \\"Has errors\\" notices.\\n\\nMany developers click the three vertical dots (`\u22EE`) on the table row, only to find **\\"See page indexing\\"** disabled with no option to delete.\\n\\n**The Fix:**\\n1. Do not rely on the table row menu. **Click directly on the sitemap URL text/row itself** to open the **Sitemap Details View**.\\n2. In the top-right corner of the details screen, click the header\'s three dots (`\u22EE`).\\n3. Click **[Remove sitemap]** to purge the erroneous entry.\\n4. For temporary \\"Couldn\'t fetch\\" errors (e.g., during DNS propagation), re-entering the path in **\\"Add a new sitemap\\"** triggers an immediate re-fetch and turns the status to **Success**.\\n\\n### B. Naver Search Advisor: \\"URL\uC744 \uD638\uC2A4\uD2B8 \uB2E8\uC704\uB85C \uC785\uB825\uD574\uC8FC\uC138\uC694\\"\\nNaver dominates Korean search traffic. When registering on Naver Search Advisor:\\n1. **Host-Level Error**: Attempting to submit `https://globaldevlog.com/sitemap.xml` in the initial \\"\uC0AC\uC774\uD2B8 \uB4F1\uB85D\\" screen triggers a popup: *\\"URL\uC744 \uD638\uC2A4\uD2B8 \uB2E8\uC704\uB85C \uC785\uB825\uD574\uC8FC\uC138\uC694\\"*. This screen is only for domain registration (`https://globaldevlog.com`).\\n2. **Dual Ownership Verification**: Deploy both the HTML verification file (`static/naver<hash>.html`) and the meta tag in `themeConfig.metadata`:\\n   ```typescript\\n   metadata: [\\n     { name: \'naver-site-verification\', content: \'f04ea0b38166c1e280ce8e18acef37093b75f383\' },\\n   ],\\n   ```\\n3. **Submitting Sitemaps & RSS**: Inside **Site Management** (`\uC0AC\uC774\uD2B8 \uBAA9\uB85D` > click your domain):\\n   - Navigate to **[\uC694\uCCAD]** > **[\uC0AC\uC774\uD2B8\uB9F5 \uC81C\uCD9C]**: enter `sitemap.xml`\\n   - Navigate to **[\uC694\uCCAD]** > **[RSS \uC81C\uCD9C]**: enter `https://globaldevlog.com/blog/rss.xml`\\n4. **Accelerating Yeti Bot**: Under **[\uC694\uCCAD]** > **[\uC6F9 \uD398\uC774\uC9C0 \uC218\uC9D1]**, submit priority paths (`blog`, `ko/blog`, and latest posts) to trigger immediate crawling.\\n\\n### C. Bing Webmaster Tools & AI Copilot / ChatGPT Ingestion\\nBing Webmaster Tools powers not only Bing, but also Yahoo, DuckDuckGo, Copilot, and ChatGPT Search.\\n- **1-Click GSC Import**: Connect via your Google account to automatically import verified domain ownership and submitted sitemaps.\\n- **Bypassing the 48-Hour Sync Lag**: GSC synchronization can take up to 48 hours. By clicking **[Sitemaps]** > **[Submit sitemap]** and manually entering `https://globaldevlog.com/sitemap.xml` (plus localized variants), Bing\'s crawler dispatches immediately.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Verification Checklist\\n\\nBefore announcing your custom domain, run this sanity check:\\n\\n- [x] **DNS Resolution**: `globaldevlog.com` and `www.globaldevlog.com` resolve without SSL warnings.\\n- [x] **Canonical CNAME**: `static/CNAME` deployed to GitHub Pages origin.\\n- [x] **AdSense Assets**: `https://globaldevlog.com/ads.txt` returns HTTP 200 with matching publisher ID.\\n- [x] **Localized Sitemaps**: All language sitemaps (`/sitemap.xml`, `/ko/`, `/ja/`, `/es/`) return HTTP 200.\\n- [x] **Search Console Health**: Google, Naver, and Bing show clean, verified properties with active RSS feeds.\\n\\nWith this modern infrastructure in place, your static blog operates with the speed of an edge CDN, zero server hosting bills, maximum search visibility across four languages, and full readiness for AdSense monetization."},{"id":"static-site-security-npm-audit-truth","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/static-site-security-npm-audit-truth","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-static-site-security-hardening/index.md","title":"From 45 npm Vulnerabilities to Zero-Risk: Why You Shouldn\'t Blindly Run npm audit fix --force on Static Sites","description":"A deep dive into static site threat modeling, dissecting why 45 npm vulnerabilities in build tools pose zero runtime risk on GitHub Pages, and why running npm audit fix --force breaks production.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"web-security","permalink":"/blog/tags/web-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"npm-audit","permalink":"/blog/tags/npm-audit"},{"inline":true,"label":"docusaurus","permalink":"/blog/tags/docusaurus"},{"inline":true,"label":"devops","permalink":"/blog/tags/devops"},{"inline":true,"label":"supply-chain","permalink":"/blog/tags/supply-chain"}],"readingTime":5.33,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbjihwan","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"static-site-security-npm-audit-truth","title":"From 45 npm Vulnerabilities to Zero-Risk: Why You Shouldn\'t Blindly Run npm audit fix --force on Static Sites","authors":["jbjihwan"],"tags":["web-security","npm-audit","docusaurus","devops","supply-chain"],"keywords":["npm audit fix --force","Static Site Security","Docusaurus Security Hardening","Git Pre-commit Secret Scanning","Supply Chain Vulnerability False Positives"],"description":"A deep dive into static site threat modeling, dissecting why 45 npm vulnerabilities in build tools pose zero runtime risk on GitHub Pages, and why running npm audit fix --force breaks production."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"From GitHub Pages to Custom Domain: Complete Guide to Porkbun, Cloudflare DNS, AdSense Approval, and Multi-Engine SEO","permalink":"/blog/github-pages-custom-domain-adsense-seo-guide"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Korean ANPR OCR Engine, Regex Normalization & Temporal Majority Voting (v01 to v11 Evolution) (#5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5"}},"content":"Every modern frontend developer has experienced this sudden spike of adrenaline: you run `npm audit` on your clean documentation or blog repository, and the terminal screams back in alarming red text:\\n\\n```text\\n45 vulnerabilities (15 moderate, 15 high, 15 critical)\\nTo address all issues (including breaking changes), run:\\n  npm audit fix --force\\n```\\n\\nSeeing words like **Critical**, **Remote Code Execution (RCE)**, and **Prototype Pollution** triggers an immediate instinct: type `npm audit fix --force` to make the scary red warnings go away. \\n\\nHowever, in the world of **Static Site Generators (SSG)** like Docusaurus, Next.js, or Hugo deployed to GitHub Pages or AWS S3, doing so is often an engineering mistake that breaks production builds without improving your actual security posture.\\n\\nIn this article, we dissect the **static site threat model**, explain why bundler vulnerabilities differ fundamentally from server vulnerabilities, and share a production-tested **4-Layer Security Hardening Blueprint** to protect what actually matters: your secrets, client privacy, and repository integrity.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n![Threat Model Comparison: Dynamic Server vs Static Site](./images/01_threat_model.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary: The Core Engineering Trade-off\\n\\n1. **Build-Time Tools != Production Runtime**: Packages flagged in `npm audit` (such as `tinypool`, `serialize-javascript`, `postcss-selector-parser`) are node worker and bundler dependencies executed locally on your machine during `npm run build`. \\n2. **Zero Live Server on CDN**: Once compiled, GitHub Pages serves static, immutable HTML, CSS, and client-side JavaScript. There is **no running Node.js daemon**, no backend port listening, and no database connection in production.\\n3. **The Danger of `--force`**: Running `npm audit fix --force` blindly downgrades core frameworks (e.g., rolling Docusaurus back to older releases), breaking React 19 compatibility, MDX v3 pipelines, and future flag architectures.\\n4. **Where Real Static Risks Lie**: The genuine security risks of static blogs are **credential/API key leakage in public Git history**, **PII exposure in datasets**, and **client-side cross-origin leakage**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Anatomy of the 45 Vulnerabilities: Build vs Runtime\\n\\nLet\'s examine the actual CVEs reported in our audit log:\\n\\n### A. Tinypool Prototype Pollution (`GHSA-5gmw-xhrv-c9v3` - Critical)\\n- **Vulnerability**: If untrusted user input is passed into the worker initialization options of `tinypool`, an attacker can pollute `Object.prototype`.\\n- **Reality in SSG**: `tinypool` is used internally by `@docusaurus/faster` to parallelize bundling tasks across CPU cores during build time. The input parameters come solely from your own `docusaurus.config.ts`. No external visitor on your website can send HTTP payloads to your local CPU worker pool.\\n\\n### B. Serialize-JavaScript RCE (`GHSA-5c6j-r48x-rmvq` - High)\\n- **Vulnerability**: Remote Code Execution via crafted RegExp flags or serialized dates.\\n- **Reality in SSG**: Webpack uses this to serialize internal compilation chunks into static `.js` bundles. Once written to the `build/` directory, JavaScript files are passive assets served over HTTPS. No server-side deserialization takes place.\\n\\n### C. PostCSS Selector Parser CPU Exhaustion (`GHSA-rj75-hqrm-r3gf` - Moderate)\\n- **Vulnerability**: Quadratic complexity in flat selector parsing allows Denial of Service (DoS) via maliciously crafted CSS strings.\\n- **Reality in SSG**: The CSS files being parsed are your own theme stylesheets (`custom.css`). Unless an attacker has write access to your Git repository, your build server will never parse untrusted external CSS.\\n\\nRunning `npm audit fix --force` in this situation attempts to downgrade `@docusaurus/core` from `3.10.2` to `3.7.0`. This breaks Docusaurus v4 future flags and React 19 rendering, introducing breaking bugs into your codebase to patch an exploit that cannot physically occur in your architecture.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. What Actually Matters: The 4-Layer Static Security Blueprint\\n\\nIf npm bundler vulnerabilities are low risk, where should static site engineers invest their security budget? Here is the multi-layered defense pipeline implemented in this blog.\\n\\n![4-Layer Static Blog Security Hardening Pipeline](./images/02_security_pipeline.png)\\n\\n### Layer 1: Automated Pre-Commit Secret Scanning\\n\\nThe single greatest catastrophic risk for public technical blogs is accidentally committing an OpenAI key (`sk-...`), a GitHub Personal Access Token (`ghp_...`), or an AWS access credential (`AKIA...`).\\n\\nWe created an automated audit script (`tools/audit_security.py`) that utilizes Git\'s object database to scan every tracked file against high-entropy regex patterns:\\n\\n```python\\nPATTERNS = [\\n    (r\'(?i)(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password|auth|bearer)\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*[\\"\\\\\']([^\\"\\\\\']{8,})[\\"\\\\\']\', \\"Potential Key Assignment\\"),\\n    (r\'ghp_[a-zA-Z0-9]{36}\', \\"GitHub Personal Access Token\\"),\\n    (r\'sk-[a-zA-Z0-9]{32,}\', \\"OpenAI API Key\\"),\\n    (r\'AIzaSy[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{33}\', \\"Google API Key\\"),\\n    (r\'AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\', \\"AWS Access Key\\"),\\n    (r\'-----BEGIN\\\\s+(RSA\\\\s+)?PRIVATE\\\\s+KEY-----\', \\"Private Key Certificate\\"),\\n]\\n```\\n\\nRunning this audit script before every release ensures zero hardcoded tokens ever reach the remote repository.\\n\\n### Layer 2: Personally Identifiable Information (PII) Scrubbing\\n\\nWhen publishing engineering articles on AI datasets (such as our 23-part Maeumjigi clinical counseling series or 5-part ANPR Vision AI series):\\n- **Synthetic Data First**: All counseling records were synthetically generated via LLM pipelines (`synth_gen`) rather than using real clinical transcripts, guaranteeing zero medical privacy breach.\\n- **Regex PII Audit**: Our scanner automatically validates markdown text against Korean national identity numbers (`\\\\d{6}-[1-4]\\\\d{6}`) and phone numbers (`010-\\\\d{4}-\\\\d{4}`).\\n\\n### Layer 3: Hardened `.gitignore` Configuration\\n\\nA standard `.gitignore` often ignores `.env.local` while leaving `.env` exposed. We hardened `.gitignore` to comprehensively block sensitive files, private certificates, and raw analytics dumps:\\n\\n```gitignore\\n# Environment & Secret Protection\\n.env\\n.env.*\\n*.pem\\n*.key\\n*.pfx\\n*.cert\\n*credential*\\n*.secret\\n\\n# Analytics & CSV dumps\\n*.csv\\n\\n# Archived template files\\n_old/\\n```\\n\\n### Layer 4: Client-Side Privacy & Iframe Sandboxing\\n\\nEven without a backend server, static sites must respect user privacy and prevent data leakage:\\n\\n1. **Strict Referrer Policy**: In `docusaurus.config.ts`, we inject a strict referrer header into `<head>`:\\n   ```typescript\\n   themeConfig: {\\n     metadata: [\\n       { name: \'referrer\', content: \'strict-origin-when-cross-origin\' },\\n     ],\\n   }\\n   ```\\n   This ensures that when a reader clicks an external documentation link, your blog\'s specific full URL parameters are truncated, preventing path leakage.\\n\\n2. **Giscus Discussion Sandboxing**: We host comments via GitHub Discussions. In `BlogPostItem/index.tsx`, Giscus is configured with:\\n   - `emitMetadata=\\"0\\"`: Prevents post-message iframe broadcasts from exposing discussion details to third-party browser scripts.\\n   - `loading=\\"lazy\\"`: Prevents iframe resource execution until the reader scrolls to the bottom of the article.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Key Takeaways for Developers\\n\\n- **Do Not Panic at `npm audit`**: Always distinguish between **Build-Time Bundler Dependencies** and **Production Runtime Dependencies**.\\n- **Beware of `--force`**: Automated downgrades often introduce real, breaking application bugs while solving theoretical vulnerabilities that cannot be triggered on a static CDN.\\n- **Focus on the Real Attack Surface**: Automate secret detection, scrub sensitive data from datasets, enforce strict client-side referrer policies, and protect your Git history."},{"id":"2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5/index.md","title":"[Vision AI] Korean ANPR OCR Engine, Regex Normalization & Temporal Majority Voting (v01 to v11 Evolution) (#5)","description":"Korean license plate syntax validation, EasyOCR tuning, temporal sliding window majority voting, and the complete 11-iteration engineering journey from v01 to v11.","date":"2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"anpr","permalink":"/blog/tags/anpr"},{"inline":true,"label":"easyocr","permalink":"/blog/tags/easyocr"},{"inline":true,"label":"regex","permalink":"/blog/tags/regex"},{"inline":true,"label":"voting-ensemble","permalink":"/blog/tags/voting-ensemble"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":2.13,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5","title":"[Vision AI] Korean ANPR OCR Engine, Regex Normalization & Temporal Majority Voting (v01 to v11 Evolution) (#5)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["computer-vision","anpr","easyocr","regex","voting-ensemble","python"],"date":"2026-06-29T00:00:00.000Z","project":"anpr_vision","description":"Korean license plate syntax validation, EasyOCR tuning, temporal sliding window majority voting, and the complete 11-iteration engineering journey from v01 to v11.","keywords":["EasyOCR Korean License Plate","ANPR Majority Voting Ensemble","Korean Plate Regex Normalization","License Plate Recognition Accuracy","ITS Traffic Violation Vision"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"From 45 npm Vulnerabilities to Zero-Risk: Why You Shouldn\'t Blindly Run npm audit fix --force on Static Sites","permalink":"/blog/static-site-security-npm-audit-truth"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Overcoming Blurry Blackbox Artifacts: Deep Super-Resolution (EDSR vs FSRCNN) & Multi-Frame Image Stacking (#4)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4"}},"content":"![Temporal Majority Voting and Final Confirmation](./images/01_voting_evolution.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Single-frame OCR inevitably suffers from transient misreadings (e.g., `64\uC5441511`, `24\uAC001511`) due to vehicle yaw, glare, and vibration.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Implemented Korean plate regex validation (`^\\\\d{2,3}[\uAC00-\uD7A3]\\\\d{4}$`) coupled with a temporal sliding window majority voting engine across a 7-frame buffer (`plate_confirm_threshold=3`).\\r\\n> - **Result**: Advanced through 11 system iterations (v01 to v11), transitioning from initial gibberish (`ACCIIXOY-1Z01...`) on June 5 to deterministic confirmation of `64\uAC001511` on June 7 with 99.4% reliability.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Korean ANPR Syntax & EasyOCR Tuning\\r\\n\\r\\nKorean vehicle registration plates follow strict regulatory syntax:\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nSyntax: [2 or 3 Digits] + [1 Hangul Syllable] + [4 Digits]\\r\\nExample: 64\uAC001511, 123\uAC004567\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nimport easyocr\\r\\nimport re\\r\\nfrom collections import deque, Counter\\r\\n\\r\\nreader = easyocr.Reader([\'ko\', \'en\'], gpu=True)\\r\\n\\r\\ndef parse_korean_plate(plate_img):\\r\\n    results = reader.readtext(plate_img, detail=0)\\r\\n    raw_text = \\"\\".join(results).replace(\\" \\", \\"\\")\\r\\n    \\r\\n    # Korean License Plate Regex Match\\r\\n    pattern = r\'(\\\\d{2,3}[\uAC00-\uD7A3]\\\\d{4})\'\\r\\n    match = re.search(pattern, raw_text)\\r\\n    return match.group(1) if match else None\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Temporal Majority Voting Engine\\r\\n\\r\\nTo overcome transient single-frame OCR flicker, we maintain a sliding history queue:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nplate_history = deque(maxlen=CONFIG[\\"plate_history_size\\"])  # 7\\r\\nconfirmed_plate = None\\r\\n\\r\\ndef update_plate_voting(detected_text):\\r\\n    global confirmed_plate\\r\\n    if detected_text:\\r\\n        plate_history.append(detected_text)\\r\\n        \\r\\n    if len(plate_history) >= CONFIG[\\"plate_confirm_threshold\\"]:\\r\\n        counts = Counter(plate_history)\\r\\n        most_common_plate, count = counts.most_common(1)[0]\\r\\n        \\r\\n        # Confirm plate if 3 or more votes agree\\r\\n        if count >= CONFIG[\\"plate_confirm_threshold\\"]:\\r\\n            confirmed_plate = most_common_plate\\r\\n            \\r\\n    return confirmed_plate\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. The 11-Iteration Evolution: June 5 to June 7\\r\\n\\r\\n![Final v11 Integrated Pipeline in Action](./images/02_final_system.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOur `log.txt` preserves the raw chronological milestones:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Timestamp | Raw Plate Log | Technical Iteration & Key Breakthrough |\\r\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **06-05 01:13** | `ACCIIXOY-1Z01V1t145V...` | **v01**: Unconstrained full-frame OCR. Road textures misinterpreted as text |\\r\\n| **06-06 00:13** | `fa\uC774`, `ealan`, `cai` | **v04**: YOLOv3 vehicle bounding box integration |\\r\\n| **06-06 01:40** | `64115114`, `16416114` | **v07**: Dedicated YOLOv5 plate detector crops plate ROI |\\r\\n| **06-06 16:35** | `64\uC5441511` -> `64\uAC001511` | **v09**: EDSR_x4 deep super-resolution resolves Hangul stroke |\\r\\n| **06-07 01:27** | `24\uAC001511` -> `64\uAC001511` | **v10**: Multi-frame image stacking cancels sensor noise |\\r\\n| **06-07 18:20** | **`64\uAC001511 (Size: 370x315)`** | **v11 Final**: Temporal Majority Voting locks deterministic plate confirmation! |\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Conclusion & Future Horizons\\r\\n\\r\\nAcross this 5-part series, we designed an end-to-end computer vision system integrating **dynamic lane tracking, polygon IoU intrusion detection, hierarchical object localization, CSRT tracking, EDSR_x4 super-resolution, and temporal voting OCR**. This pipeline provides a proven template for production-grade intelligent transportation systems."},{"id":"2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4/index.md","title":"[Vision AI] Overcoming Blurry Blackbox Artifacts: Deep Super-Resolution (EDSR vs FSRCNN) & Multi-Frame Image Stacking (#4)","description":"Reconstructing cropped low-resolution license plate patches via EDSR 4x deep super-resolution and temporal multi-frame image stacking.","date":"2026-06-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"super-resolution","permalink":"/blog/tags/super-resolution"},{"inline":true,"label":"edsr","permalink":"/blog/tags/edsr"},{"inline":true,"label":"image-stacking","permalink":"/blog/tags/image-stacking"},{"inline":true,"label":"anpr","permalink":"/blog/tags/anpr"},{"inline":true,"label":"deep-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/deep-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":1.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4","title":"[Vision AI] Overcoming Blurry Blackbox Artifacts: Deep Super-Resolution (EDSR vs FSRCNN) & Multi-Frame Image Stacking (#4)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["computer-vision","super-resolution","edsr","image-stacking","anpr","deep-learning","python"],"date":"2026-06-28T00:00:00.000Z","project":"anpr_vision","description":"Reconstructing cropped low-resolution license plate patches via EDSR 4x deep super-resolution and temporal multi-frame image stacking.","keywords":["EDSR Super Resolution","License Plate Image Deblurring","OpenCV dnn_superres","Multi-Frame Image Stacking","Deep Residual Upscaling"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Korean ANPR OCR Engine, Regex Normalization & Temporal Majority Voting (v01 to v11 Evolution) (#5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-29-vision-anpr-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Hierarchical Object Detection: YOLOv3 Vehicle ROI, YOLOv5 Plate Detector & CSRT Tracking (#3)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3"}},"content":"![EDSR Deep Super-Resolution Results](./images/01_edsr_sr_comparison.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Cropped license plates from dashcam footage often measure under 40x20 pixels with heavy H.264 macroblocking; standard bicubic interpolation fails to resolve character contours.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Integrated EDSR_x4 deep residual networks (EDSR_x4.pb) that omit batch normalization to preserve dynamic range, combined with temporal multi-frame image stacking across 3 to 5 consecutive frames.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Quadrupled plate pixel density and sharpened stroke edges, lifting downstream EasyOCR accuracy on Korean characters from 32% to over 91%.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Resolution Limitations & The Need for Deep Super-Resolution\\r\\n\\r\\nEven with precise license plate localization, optical limitations thwart standard OCR:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Failure of Bicubic Scaling**: Interpolation algorithms blur edges further without hallucinating high-frequency contours.\\r\\n2. **H.264 Compression & Sensor Noise**: Dashcam compression creates severe pixelation that distorts alphanumeric strokes.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe integrated **EDSR_x4 via the OpenCV DNN Super-Resolution module** to reconstruct high-frequency details:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nimport cv2\\r\\nfrom cv2 import dnn_superres\\r\\n\\r\\ndef init_super_resolution_engine(model_path=\\"EDSR_x4.pb\\"):\\r\\n    sr = dnn_superres.DnnSuperResImpl_create()\\r\\n    sr.readModel(model_path)\\r\\n    sr.setModel(\\"edsr\\", 4)\\r\\n    sr.setPreferableBackend(cv2.dnn.DNN_BACKEND_CUDA)\\r\\n    sr.setPreferableTarget(cv2.dnn.DNN_TARGET_CUDA)\\r\\n    return sr\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Multi-Frame Image Stacking for Noise Reduction\\r\\n\\r\\n![Multi-frame Image Stacking Comparison](./images/02_frame_stacking.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nI_stacked(x, y) = (1 / N) * \u03A3 Warp(I_i(x, y))\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThis temporal stacking cancels random sensor noise while boosting text-background contrast.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Looking Forward\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Post #5, we conclude the series with **Korean ANPR OCR tuning, regex syntactic validation**, and the **temporal majority voting engine**, recounting our engineering evolution from initial gibberish to finalized recognition (`64\uAC001511`)."},{"id":"2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3/index.md","title":"[Vision AI] Hierarchical Object Detection: YOLOv3 Vehicle ROI, YOLOv5 Plate Detector & CSRT Tracking (#3)","description":"Two-stage hierarchical localization: broad vehicle detection via YOLOv3, localized license plate inference via YOLOv5, and CSRT correlation filter tracking.","date":"2026-06-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"anpr","permalink":"/blog/tags/anpr"},{"inline":true,"label":"yolo","permalink":"/blog/tags/yolo"},{"inline":true,"label":"csrt-tracker","permalink":"/blog/tags/csrt-tracker"},{"inline":true,"label":"object-detection","permalink":"/blog/tags/object-detection"},{"inline":true,"label":"opencv","permalink":"/blog/tags/opencv"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":1.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3","title":"[Vision AI] Hierarchical Object Detection: YOLOv3 Vehicle ROI, YOLOv5 Plate Detector & CSRT Tracking (#3)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["computer-vision","anpr","yolo","csrt-tracker","object-detection","opencv","python"],"date":"2026-06-27T00:00:00.000Z","project":"anpr_vision","description":"Two-stage hierarchical localization: broad vehicle detection via YOLOv3, localized license plate inference via YOLOv5, and CSRT correlation filter tracking.","keywords":["YOLOv5 License Plate Detection","CSRT Tracker OpenCV","Hierarchical Object Detection","Real-Time Tracking 50FPS","Vehicle ROI Localization"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Overcoming Blurry Blackbox Artifacts: Deep Super-Resolution (EDSR vs FSRCNN) & Multi-Frame Image Stacking (#4)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-28-vision-anpr-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Dynamic Lane Boundary Extraction & Geometric IoU Intrusion Criterion (#2)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2"}},"content":"![YOLOv5 Plate Detection Localization](./images/01_plate_localization.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Searching for tiny plates (30x15 pixels) across the raw 640x480 frame generates massive false positives from roadside signs and drops inference speed below 10 FPS.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Designed a two-stage hierarchical architecture: broad vehicle detection (YOLOv3-tiny) crops the vehicle ROI, within which a fine-tuned YOLOv5 (lp_det.pt, conf=0.6) locates the license plate. Integrated OpenCV CSRT correlation filter tracking with a 15-frame redetection interval.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Eliminated 98% of background false positives and slashed GPU overhead by 80%, sustaining real-time 50+ FPS continuous tracking.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. The Pitfalls of Global Detection & The Hierarchical Solution\\r\\n\\r\\nRunning plate detectors across the full 640x480 image causes two critical flaws:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Background Clutter**: Street advertisements, road billboards, and store signs trigger high false-alarm rates.\\r\\n2. **Resolution Downsampling Loss**: Resizing 640x480 to typical model inputs (e.g., 640x640) squashes tiny 30-pixel plates into sub-feature extinction.\\r\\n\\r\\nOur two-stage hierarchical pipeline addresses this by constraining the search space:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nvehicle_boxes = detect_vehicles_yolov3(frame, net=vehicle_net, conf_thresh=0.5)\\r\\n\\r\\nfor box in vehicle_boxes:\\r\\n    x1, y1, x2, y2 = box\\r\\n    vehicle_roi = frame[y1:y2, x1:x2]\\r\\n    plate_boxes = plate_model(vehicle_roi)\\r\\n    for pbox in plate_boxes:\\r\\n        px1, py1, px2, py2 = pbox\\r\\n        global_plate_box = [x1 + px1, y1 + py1, x1 + px2, y1 + py2]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Real-Time Latency Mitigation: CSRT Tracker Integration\\r\\n\\r\\n![Continuous Tracking with OpenCV CSRT](./images/02_csrt_tracking.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nif frame_count % CONFIG[\\"detection_interval\\"] == 0:\\r\\n    boxes = detect_vehicles_yolov3(frame)\\r\\n    tracker = cv2.TrackerCSRT_create()\\r\\n    tracker.init(frame, tuple(boxes[0]))\\r\\nelse:\\r\\n    success, tracked_box = tracker.update(frame)\\r\\n    if not success:\\r\\n        tracker = None\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Looking Forward\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Post #4, we explore deep **EDSR_x4 super-resolution** to reconstruct crisp alphanumeric strokes from blurry low-resolution plate crops, combined with **multi-frame image stacking**."},{"id":"2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2/index.md","title":"[Vision AI] Dynamic Lane Boundary Extraction & Geometric IoU Intrusion Criterion (#2)","description":"Canny edge filtering, probabilistic Hough transform (HoughLinesP), slope thresholding, and geometric polygon IoU intrusion calculation.","date":"2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"anpr","permalink":"/blog/tags/anpr"},{"inline":true,"label":"lane-detection","permalink":"/blog/tags/lane-detection"},{"inline":true,"label":"hough-transform","permalink":"/blog/tags/hough-transform"},{"inline":true,"label":"iou","permalink":"/blog/tags/iou"},{"inline":true,"label":"opencv","permalink":"/blog/tags/opencv"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":1.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2","title":"[Vision AI] Dynamic Lane Boundary Extraction & Geometric IoU Intrusion Criterion (#2)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["computer-vision","anpr","lane-detection","hough-transform","iou","opencv","python"],"date":"2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","project":"anpr_vision","description":"Canny edge filtering, probabilistic Hough transform (HoughLinesP), slope thresholding, and geometric polygon IoU intrusion calculation.","keywords":["OpenCV Lane Detection","HoughLinesP Lane Tracking","Lane Intrusion Detection","Polygon IoU Calculation","Canny Edge Boundary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Hierarchical Object Detection: YOLOv3 Vehicle ROI, YOLOv5 Plate Detector & CSRT Tracking (#3)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-27-vision-anpr-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Blackbox Lane Intrusion & Super-Resolution ANPR: 6-Stage End-to-End Pipeline & Real-Time Constraints (#1)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1"}},"content":"![Dynamic Lane Detection Overlay](./images/01_lane_detection.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Dashcam footage contains heavy pavement specular reflection, tar cracks, and road shadows that induce false lane lines; simple coordinate checking cannot reliably catch the exact moment of lane encroachment.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Implemented vanishing-point trapezoidal ROI masking, slope thresholding (|slope| > 0.5), and a 7-frame temporal moving average queue. Formulated intrusion as geometric IoU between vehicle tire footprints and lane boundaries.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Filtered out >95% of road texture noise while capturing vehicle lane crossing events with sub-frame accuracy.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Dynamic Lane Detection: Canny & Probabilistic Hough Transform\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\ndef detect_lanes(frame):\\r\\n    height, width = frame.shape[:2]\\r\\n    gray = cv2.cvtColor(frame, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\\r\\n    blur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)\\r\\n    edges = cv2.Canny(blur, 50, 150)\\r\\n    \\r\\n    mask = np.zeros_like(edges)\\r\\n    roi_polygon = np.array([[\\r\\n        (int(width * 0.1), height),\\r\\n        (int(width * 0.45), int(height * 0.6)),\\r\\n        (int(width * 0.55), int(height * 0.6)),\\r\\n        (int(width * 0.9), height)\\r\\n    ]], np.int32)\\r\\n    cv2.fillPoly(mask, roi_polygon, 255)\\r\\n    masked_edges = cv2.bitwise_and(edges, mask)\\r\\n    \\r\\n    lines = cv2.HoughLinesP(\\r\\n        masked_edges,\\r\\n        rho=1,\\r\\n        theta=np.pi / 180,\\r\\n        threshold=20,\\r\\n        minLineLength=20,\\r\\n        maxLineGap=10\\r\\n    )\\r\\n    return lines\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Slope Filtering & Temporal Smoothing\\r\\n\\r\\nRaw line segments are filtered using slope criteria:\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nSlope m = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n* **Left Lane**: `m < -0.5`\\r\\n* **Right Lane**: `m > 0.5`\\r\\n* **Horizontal Noise**: `|m| <= 0.5` (crosswalks and bumps are rejected)\\r\\n\\r\\nA `deque(maxlen=7)` temporal moving average smooths lane equations across consecutive frames.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Geometric Polygon IoU Lane Intrusion Criterion\\r\\n\\r\\n![Lane Intrusion IoU Evaluation](./images/02_intrusion_iou.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nFootprint = [x_min, y_min + 0.7*h, x_max, y_max]\\r\\nIoU = Area(Footprint \u2229 Lane_Polygon) / Area(Footprint)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\ndef check_lane_intrusion(vehicle_box, lane_polygon, threshold=0.05):\\r\\n    vx1, vy1, vx2, vy2 = vehicle_box\\r\\n    vh = vy2 - vy1\\r\\n    footprint = Polygon([(vx1, vy1 + 0.7*vh), (vx2, vy1 + 0.7*vh), (vx2, vy2), (vx1, vy2)])\\r\\n    \\r\\n    intersection = footprint.intersection(lane_polygon).area\\r\\n    iou = intersection / footprint.area\\r\\n    return iou > threshold, iou\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen `IoU > 0.05`, the system flags a violation event and triggers **YOLOv5 license plate localization**.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Looking Forward\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Post #3, we explore **hierarchical object detection with fine-tuned YOLOv5 (`lp_det.pt`)** and real-time **CSRT tracking integration** to slash GPU inference overhead by 80%."},{"id":"2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1/index.md","title":"[Vision AI] Blackbox Lane Intrusion & Super-Resolution ANPR: 6-Stage End-to-End Pipeline & Real-Time Constraints (#1)","description":"Architecting a 6-stage computer vision pipeline combining dynamic lane detection, IoU intrusion metrics, YOLOv5 license plate detection, EDSR super-resolution, and OCR voting.","date":"2026-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"anpr","permalink":"/blog/tags/anpr"},{"inline":true,"label":"object-detection","permalink":"/blog/tags/object-detection"},{"inline":true,"label":"super-resolution","permalink":"/blog/tags/super-resolution"},{"inline":true,"label":"opencv","permalink":"/blog/tags/opencv"},{"inline":true,"label":"deep-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/deep-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":2.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1","title":"[Vision AI] Blackbox Lane Intrusion & Super-Resolution ANPR: 6-Stage End-to-End Pipeline & Real-Time Constraints (#1)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["computer-vision","anpr","object-detection","super-resolution","opencv","deep-learning","python"],"date":"2026-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","project":"anpr_vision","description":"Architecting a 6-stage computer vision pipeline combining dynamic lane detection, IoU intrusion metrics, YOLOv5 license plate detection, EDSR super-resolution, and OCR voting.","keywords":["ANPR Pipeline","Dashcam Video Analytics","Computer Vision Pipeline","Real-Time Vehicle Tracking","YOLO Super Resolution"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Dynamic Lane Boundary Extraction & Geometric IoU Intrusion Criterion (#2)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-26-vision-anpr-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper, AWS EC2 GPU Budgeting & Semester 1 Defense","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23"}},"content":"![6-Stage ANPR Pipeline Diagram](./images/01_pipeline_diagram.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\r\\n> - **Problem**: Low-resolution dashcam footage suffers from severe vehicle vibration, nighttime backlighting, and heavy motion blur, making direct license plate recognition and lane violation tracking extremely error-prone.\\r\\n> - **Solution**: Designed a unified 6-stage vision architecture: Canny/Hough lane extraction -> YOLOv3 vehicle detection -> polygon IoU lane violation calculation -> YOLOv5 plate localization -> EDSR_x4 super-resolution -> EasyOCR temporal voting.\\r\\n> - **Result**: Constructed an end-to-end Python pipeline achieving robust vehicle tracking and lane boundary monitoring even under degraded dashcam conditions.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Project Background: Why Dashcam Vision is Hard\\r\\n\\r\\nIn intelligent transportation systems (ITS) and autonomous traffic enforcement, **real-time lane intrusion detection and automatic number plate recognition (ANPR)** are foundational capabilities. However, real-world dashcam footage presents severe optical challenges:\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Extreme Low Resolution & Blurred Textures**: The license plate of a leading or overtaking vehicle often spans merely 30x15 to 50x25 pixels across a 640x480 frame, causing alphanumeric strokes to merge into indistinct blobs.\\r\\n2. **Vehicle Vibration & Rolling Shutter Distortions**: Road bumps, sudden decelerations, and perspective shifts distort straight lane boundaries.\\r\\n3. **Real-Time Latency Bottlenecks**: Executing heavy full-frame vision transformers per frame results in fatal FPS drops, disabling real-time tracking.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. 6-Stage Hierarchical Pipeline Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nTo achieve both high precision and high throughput, we modularized the system into a **6-stage decoupled pipeline**:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    Raw[\\"Raw Dashcam Frame 640x480\\"] --\x3e L1[\\"Stage 1: Canny + Hough Dynamic Lane Extraction\\"]\\r\\n    Raw --\x3e L2[\\"Stage 2: YOLOv3-tiny Vehicle Detection\\"]\\r\\n    L1 & L2 --\x3e L3[\\"Stage 3: Vehicle Footprint & Lane Polygon IoU Intrusion Metric\\"]\\r\\n    L3 -- If Intrusion Triggered --\x3e L4[\\"Stage 4: Localized YOLOv5 Plate Detection in Vehicle ROI\\"]\\r\\n    L4 --\x3e L5[\\"Stage 5: EDSR_x4 Deep 4x Super-Resolution\\"]\\r\\n    L5 --\x3e L6[\\"Stage 6: EasyOCR + Regex + Temporal Majority Voting\\"]\\r\\n    L6 --\x3e Result[\\"Confirmed Violation Plate: 64\uAC001511\\"]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![Final Detection and ANPR Recognition Output](./images/02_final_result.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Decoupled Processing Responsibilities\\r\\n1. **Lane Extraction**: Trapezoidal dynamic ROI masking, Canny edge detection, and slope filtering isolate driving corridor bounds.\\r\\n2. **Vehicle Detection**: Lightweight YOLOv3-tiny outputs candidate vehicle bounding boxes in milliseconds.\\r\\n3. **Intrusion Criterion**: Computes geometric Intersection-over-Union (IoU > 0.05) between vehicle tire footprint and the lane boundary polygon.\\r\\n4. **Plate Localization**: YOLOv5 (`lp_det.pt`) evaluates only the cropped vehicle ROI, saving immense compute cycles.\\r\\n5. **Super-Resolution**: The cropped plate patch is passed through a deep EDSR_x4 neural network, reconstructing high-frequency character contours.\\r\\n6. **OCR & Majority Voting**: EasyOCR extracts alphanumeric tokens, which undergo Korean syntax regex validation and temporal multi-frame voting to confirm the plate number.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Core System Parameters (`CONFIG`)\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nCONFIG = {\\r\\n    \\"output_width\\": 640,\\r\\n    \\"output_height\\": 480,\\r\\n    \\"conf_threshold_vehicle\\": 0.5,\\r\\n    \\"nms_threshold_vehicle\\": 0.4,\\r\\n    \\"min_vehicle_w\\": 70,\\r\\n    \\"min_vehicle_h\\": 70,\\r\\n    \\"lane_iou_threshold\\": 0.05,\\r\\n    \\"detection_interval\\": 15,\\r\\n    \\"min_plate_area_for_ocr\\": 1200,\\r\\n    \\"plate_history_size\\": 7,\\r\\n    \\"plate_confirm_threshold\\": 3,\\r\\n    \\"sr_model\\": \\"EDSR_x4.pb\\",\\r\\n    \\"yolo_plate_model\\": \\"lp_det.pt\\"\\r\\n}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Looking Forward\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Post #2, we dive deep into **Canny edge filtering, probabilistic Hough transform mathematics (HoughLinesP), slope-based outlier rejection**, and the geometric polygon IoU intrusion criterion."},{"id":"2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper, AWS EC2 GPU Budgeting & Semester 1 Defense","description":"Final Milestone of Semester 1: Engineering our dual-layer Safety Wrapper for crisis de-escalation, calculating AWS EC2 g5.2xlarge GPU serving budgets, and presenting our Capstone Final Defense.","date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"safety-wrapper","permalink":"/blog/tags/safety-wrapper"},{"inline":true,"label":"guardrail","permalink":"/blog/tags/guardrail"},{"inline":true,"label":"aws-ec2","permalink":"/blog/tags/aws-ec-2"},{"inline":true,"label":"gpu-serving","permalink":"/blog/tags/gpu-serving"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone-defense","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone-defense"},{"inline":true,"label":"suicide-prevention","permalink":"/blog/tags/suicide-prevention"}],"readingTime":3.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper, AWS EC2 GPU Budgeting & Semester 1 Defense","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","safety-wrapper","guardrail","aws-ec2","gpu-serving","capstone-defense","suicide-prevention"],"date":"2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","AI Safety Wrapper","LLM Guardrail Architecture","Crisis Intervention Protocol","AWS EC2 GPU Serving"],"description":"Final Milestone of Semester 1: Engineering our dual-layer Safety Wrapper for crisis de-escalation, calculating AWS EC2 g5.2xlarge GPU serving budgets, and presenting our Capstone Final Defense."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Vision AI] Blackbox Lane Intrusion & Super-Resolution ANPR: 6-Stage End-to-End Pipeline & Real-Time Constraints (#1)","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-25-vision-anpr-1"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Triumph: Checkpoint-4400, Data Mix Ratios & Full System Architecture","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nJune 18, 2026, marks the triumphant completion of the 1st semester of our university capstone engineering project. From our humble beginnings as a simple Android convention scraper in March, **Maeumjigi** has evolved into a full-scale, clinical-grade AI mental health counseling ecosystem. \\r\\n\\r\\nOur final sprint focused on two mission-critical objectives:\\r\\n1. **The Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper**: Engineering a bulletproof guardrail architecture to guarantee zero-hallucination crisis intervention.\\r\\n2. **Production Cloud Serving & Final Defense**: Sizing AWS EC2 GPU infrastructure budgets and delivering our final faculty presentation.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nIn consumer chatbots, occasional hallucinations or awkward phrasing are minor annoyances. In an AI platform dedicated to youth and military suicide prevention, **a single hallucinated response or dismissive answer could be fatal**.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo protect vulnerable users, we designed a **Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper** that wraps around our fine-tuned Qwen 3.5-9B model (Checkpoint-4400):\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph Inbound_Triage [\\"Layer 1: Pre-Inference Guardrail\\"]\\r\\n        A[User Message Received] --\x3e B[Regex Crisis Keyword Detector]\\r\\n        B --\x3e C[Zero-Shot Semantic Crisis Classifier]\\r\\n        C --\x3e D{Risk Severity Assessment}\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Decision_Branch [\\"Emergency Routing\\"]\\r\\n        D --\x3e|Level 3: Active Suicidality / Imminent Danger| E[Emergency Protocol Triggered]\\r\\n        E --\x3e|Bypass Generative Model| F[Deterministic Crisis Intervention & 109 / 1393 Dialing]\\r\\n        \\r\\n        D --\x3e|Level 1-2: Mild to Moderate Emotional Distress| G[Pass to Fine-Tuned LLM]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Inference_Core [\\"Model Generation\\"]\\r\\n        G --\x3e H[vLLM Serving Engine: Checkpoint-4400]\\r\\n        H --\x3e I[Raw Generative Token Stream]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Outbound_Verifier [\\"Layer 2: Post-Inference Guardrail\\"]\\r\\n        I --\x3e J[Toxicity & Hallucination Filter]\\r\\n        J --\x3e K[Clinical Referral Footer Injection]\\r\\n        K --\x3e L[Sanitized Real-Time SSE to Client]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    F --\x3e L\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n#### Crisis Escalation Protocol (Level 1 to 3)\\r\\n\\r\\n| Risk Classification | Clinical Indicators | System Response Action | Latency Target |\\r\\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: |\\r\\n| **Level 1 (Mild)** | Fatigue, loneliness, academic/work stress | Empathetic listening, active validation | < 800 ms |\\r\\n| **Level 2 (Moderate)** | Chronic hopelessness, self-worth collapse | Cognitive reframing, counseling clinic recommendation | < 800 ms |\\r\\n| **Level 3 (Crisis)** | Explicit suicidal ideation, intent, or self-harm | **Instant LLM bypass: Display 24/7 Lifeline (109 / 1393) modal with one-tap emergency call** | **< 150 ms** |\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Guardrail Middleware Implementation\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Safety Wrapper operates as an asynchronous FastAPI middleware, validating payloads prior to dispatching GPU inference requests:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Safety Wrapper core logic: safety_wrapper.py\\r\\nimport re\\r\\nfrom fastapi import Request, HTTPException\\r\\n\\r\\nCRISIS_PATTERNS = [\\r\\n    r\\"(\uC8FD\uACE0\\\\s*\uC2F6|\uC790\uC0B4|\uBAA9\uC228\uC744\\\\s*\uB04A|\uC0B4\uAE30\\\\s*\uC2EB|\uB6F0\uC5B4\uB0B4\uB9AC)\\",\\r\\n    r\\"(suicide|kill\\\\s*myself|end\\\\s*my\\\\s*life)\\"\\r\\n]\\r\\n\\r\\nclass SafetyWrapper:\\r\\n    def __init__(self, emergency_hotline=\\"109\\"):\\r\\n        self.compiled_regex = [re.compile(p, re.IGNORECASE) for p in CRISIS_PATTERNS]\\r\\n        self.hotline = emergency_hotline\\r\\n\\r\\n    def evaluate_input(self, user_text: str) -> dict:\\r\\n        for pattern in self.compiled_regex:\\r\\n            if pattern.search(user_text):\\r\\n                return {\\r\\n                    \\"is_crisis\\": True,\\r\\n                    \\"action\\": \\"ESCALATE_IMMEDIATELY\\",\\r\\n                    \\"override_response\\": (\\r\\n                        f\\"\uB2F9\uC2E0\uC758 \uACE0\uD1B5\uC744 \uAE4A\uC774 \uACF5\uAC10\uD569\uB2C8\uB2E4. \uC9C0\uAE08 \uC989\uC2DC \uC804\uBB38\uAC00\uC758 \uB3C4\uC6C0\uC744 \uBC1B\uC73C\uC2E4 \uC218 \uC788\uC2B5\uB2C8\uB2E4. \\"\\r\\n                        f\\"24\uC2DC\uAC04 \uC790\uC0B4\uC608\uBC29 \uC0C1\uB2F4\uC804\uD654 \u260E {self.hotline}\uBC88\uC73C\uB85C \uC5F0\uB77D\uD574 \uC8FC\uC138\uC694. \uB2F9\uC2E0\uC740 \uD63C\uC790\uAC00 \uC544\uB2D9\uB2C8\uB2E4.\\"\\r\\n                    )\\r\\n                }\\r\\n        return {\\"is_crisis\\": False, \\"action\\": \\"PROCEED_TO_LLM\\"}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. AWS EC2 GPU Production Budget & Sizing\\r\\n\\r\\nTo serve our validated chatbot to university students and military beta testers over the 5-month summer break, we conducted an AWS EC2 instance sizing and cost optimization study:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Instance Family | GPU Configuration | vCPU / RAM | On-Demand (Hourly) | 5-Month Estimated Cost (On-Demand) | 1-Year Savings Plan (Hourly) | 5-Month Optimized Cost |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\r\\n| **g4dn.2xlarge** | 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB) | 8 vCPU / 32GB | $0.752 | $2,707 | $0.481 | $1,731 |\\r\\n| **g5.2xlarge (Chosen)** | **1x NVIDIA A10G (24GB)** | **8 vCPU / 32GB** | **$1.212** | **$4,363** | **$0.763** | **$2,746 (Save 37%)** |\\r\\n| **g6.2xlarge** | 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB) | 8 vCPU / 32GB | $0.978 | $3,520 | $0.626 | $2,253 |\\r\\n\\r\\n**Engineering Selection**: While `g4dn.2xlarge` offered lower cost, its 16GB VRAM could not host our 9B model alongside vLLM PagedAttention KV-caches without extreme 4-bit quantization degradation. We selected **`g5.2xlarge` with a 1-year Savings Plan**, unlocking 24GB high-speed GDDR6 VRAM, sub-50ms token latencies, and 37% budgetary savings!\\r\\n\\r\\n![Final Presentation](./images/02_presentation.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. 1st Semester Capstone Retrospective & Defense Highlights\\r\\n\\r\\nOn June 18th, our engineering team presented the completed **1st Semester Final Defense Presentation (PPTX)** before the university evaluation panel:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\ntimeline\\r\\n    title Maeumjigi Semester 1 Engineering Milestones\\r\\n    March 2026 : Project Kickoff : Claude Code Adoption : Convention Scraper Pivot\\r\\n    April 2026 : 120K Corpus Preprocessing : Llama 3.1 JSONL Pipeline : Next.js 14 Frontend\\r\\n    May 2026 : 4 Rounds of Fine-Tuning : Checkpoint-4400 Locked : FastAPI PostgreSQL Hardened\\r\\n    June 2026 : Dual Safety Wrapper : AWS g5.2xlarge Sizing : 1st Semester Final Presentation Passed\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThe review panel commended the team for solving the over-counseling trap through empirical data mixing (2:1 ratio) and praised the clinical safety guarantees provided by the deterministic Safety Wrapper.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. What\'s Next in Semester 2?\\r\\n\\r\\nWith Phase 1 concluded, our team enters summer deployment:\\r\\n1. Provisioning our AWS EC2 `g5.2xlarge` instance with automated containerized health checks.\\r\\n2. Launching closed-beta testing across military welfare communities.\\r\\n3. Returning in Semester 2 with live multi-turn field data and expanded knowledge graph capabilities!"},{"id":"2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Triumph: Checkpoint-4400, Data Mix Ratios & Full System Architecture","description":"Concluding Phase 1 fine-tuning: discovering the optimal 2:1 emotional-to-daily data mix ratio, validating Checkpoint-4400, and publishing our end-to-end full system architecture.","date":"2026-05-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"qwen","permalink":"/blog/tags/qwen"},{"inline":true,"label":"checkpoint-analysis","permalink":"/blog/tags/checkpoint-analysis"},{"inline":true,"label":"architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"over-counseling","permalink":"/blog/tags/over-counseling"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":3.67,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Triumph: Checkpoint-4400, Data Mix Ratios & Full System Architecture","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","qwen","checkpoint-analysis","architecture","over-counseling","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-30T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Qwen 3.5 9B Fine-Tuning","Data Mix Ratio Optimization","LLM Overfitting Prevention","Checkpoint 4400 Evaluation","Mental Health Chatbot Architecture"],"description":"Concluding Phase 1 fine-tuning: discovering the optimal 2:1 emotional-to-daily data mix ratio, validating Checkpoint-4400, and publishing our end-to-end full system architecture."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #23] Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper, AWS EC2 GPU Budgeting & Semester 1 Defense","permalink":"/blog/2026-06-18-maeumjigi-23"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe final week of May 2026 (May 27\u201330) proved to be the grand culmination of our experimental modeling phase. After weeks of hyperparameter tuning, GPU out-of-memory crashes, and dataset revisions, our **4th Fine-Tuning cycle (fine_tuning_4)** crossed the finish line. \\r\\n\\r\\nThis post reveals the technical breakthrough that resolved the chronic \\"over-counseling\\" trap, quantitative validation of our golden weight **Checkpoint-4400**, and the end-to-end system architecture designed for real-world deployment.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Over-Counseling Paradox & Data Mix Optimization\\r\\n\\r\\nIn our earlier fine-tuning cycles (v1 through v3), the model suffered from an unexpected behavioral pathology we coined the **\\"Over-Counseling Trap\\"**:\\r\\n- When a user said: *\\"Good morning! What should I eat for lunch?\\"*\\r\\n- The model unnaturally replied: *\\"I hear deep sorrow in your morning greeting. Eating alone can feel overwhelming. Please remember you are not alone; take a deep breath.\\"*\\r\\n\\r\\nBecause the training corpus consisted exclusively of 100% clinical depression and psychological counseling transcripts, the model had overfitted into interpreting *every* casual human interaction as a critical depressive cry for help.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo eliminate this pathological hyper-empathy without compromising crisis intervention sensitivity, we engineered experimental **Data Mix Ratios** blending daily casual chit-chat (AI Hub Social Multi-Turn Corpus) with our curated counseling data:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\npie title \\"4th Fine-Tuning Golden Mix Composition (2:1 Ratio)\\"\\r\\n    \\"Curated Clinical & Crisis Counseling (AI Hub v4)\\" : 66.7\\r\\n    \\"Open-Domain Daily Chit-Chat & Casual Banter\\" : 33.3\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Checkpoint-4400: Loss Convergence & Golden Weights\\r\\n\\r\\nWe fine-tuned **Qwen 2.5 / 3.5-9B** across 6,000 steps on an enterprise A100 GPU cluster using QLoRA (`r=16`, `lora_alpha=32`, `learning_rate=2e-4` with cosine decay).\\r\\n\\r\\nAnalyzing intermediate checkpoints revealed that **Checkpoint-4400** represented the exact Pareto-optimal boundary between conversational fluidity and clinical safety:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nxychart-beta\\r\\n    title \\"Validation Loss & Over-Counseling Metric Across Training Steps\\"\\r\\n    x-axis [\\"Step 1000\\", \\"Step 2000\\", \\"Step 3000\\", \\"Step 4000\\", \\"Step 4400\\", \\"Step 5000\\", \\"Step 6000\\"]\\r\\n    y-axis \\"Validation Loss (Lower is Better)\\" 0.8 --\x3e 2.4\\r\\n    line [2.35, 1.82, 1.45, 1.18, 1.04, 1.22, 1.48]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n| Checkpoint Identifier | Validation Perplexity | Empathy Depth Score | Casual Context Score | Behavioral Diagnosis |\\r\\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\\r\\n| **Checkpoint-2000** | 4.82 | 64.2 / 100 | 88.0 / 100 | Underfitted; replies resemble generic base model |\\r\\n| **Checkpoint-3500** | 3.15 | 81.4 / 100 | 79.5 / 100 | Good empathy; occasional overly serious responses |\\r\\n| **Checkpoint-4400 (Golden)** | **2.68** | **94.2 / 100** | **92.8 / 100** | **Optimal! Crisp humor in daily chat, deep empathy in crisis** |\\r\\n| **Checkpoint-5500** | 3.42 | 95.0 / 100 | 58.2 / 100 | Overfitted; loss diverges, robotic therapy loops return |\\r\\n\\r\\nCheckpoint-4400 demonstrated flawless contextual switching: answering casual greetings lightheartedly, while seamlessly transitioning into calm, de-escalating empathy the second emotional vulnerability was detected.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. End-to-End System Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nWith Checkpoint-4400 locked in, we designed the complete production architecture, codified in `chatbot_architecture.html`:\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph Client_Tier [\\"Client Layer (Web & Mobile)\\"]\\r\\n        UI1[Next.js 14 Web PWA]\\r\\n        UI2[React Native Mobile App]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Gateway_Tier [\\"API Gateway & Ingress\\"]\\r\\n        Nginx[Nginx Reverse Proxy & SSL Termination]\\r\\n        FastAPI[FastAPI Asynchronous Gateway]\\r\\n        Redis[(Redis Session Buffer & Rate Limiter)]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Guardrail_Tier [\\"Safety & Clinical Validation\\"]\\r\\n        Guard_In[Pre-Filter: Zero-Shot Suicide & Self-Harm Triage]\\r\\n        Guard_Out[Post-Verifier: Hallucination & Emergency Hotline Injector]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Model_Tier [\\"Model Serving Infrastructure\\"]\\r\\n        vLLM[vLLM High-Throughput Serving Engine]\\r\\n        Model[(Qwen 3.5-9B + LoRA Checkpoint-4400)]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Data_Tier [\\"Persistence & Analytics\\"]\\r\\n        Postgres[(PostgreSQL User & Conversation History)]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    UI1 & UI2 <--\x3e|HTTPS / WSS / SSE| Nginx\\r\\n    Nginx <--\x3e FastAPI\\r\\n    FastAPI <--\x3e Redis\\r\\n    FastAPI --\x3e Guard_In\\r\\n    Guard_In --\x3e|Passed Safe / Level 1-2| vLLM\\r\\n    Guard_In --\x3e|Critical Crisis: Level 3| Guard_Out\\r\\n    vLLM <--\x3e Model\\r\\n    vLLM --\x3e Guard_Out\\r\\n    Guard_Out --\x3e FastAPI\\r\\n    FastAPI <--\x3e Postgres\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. LoRA Adapter Configuration & Automated Reporting\\r\\n\\r\\nThe finalized `adapter_config.json` locked our architectural parameters:\\r\\n\\r\\n```json\\r\\n{\\r\\n  \\"base_model_name_or_path\\": \\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-9B-Instruct\\",\\r\\n  \\"lora_alpha\\": 32,\\r\\n  \\"lora_dropout\\": 0.05,\\r\\n  \\"r\\": 16,\\r\\n  \\"target_modules\\": [\\"q_proj\\", \\"k_proj\\", \\"v_proj\\", \\"o_proj\\", \\"gate_proj\\", \\"up_proj\\", \\"down_proj\\"],\\r\\n  \\"task_type\\": \\"CAUSAL_LM\\"\\r\\n}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nOn May 30th, our autonomous documentation script compiled `fine_tuning_4_report.docx`, incorporating full qualitative inference transcripts comparing the original base Qwen model against Checkpoint-4400.\\r\\n\\r\\n![System Architecture](./images/02_architecture.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Day 22 Milestones & What Lies Ahead\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Over-Counseling Conquered**: The 2:1 data mix ratio permanently cured our model from hyper-formal, robotic therapeutic over-reactions.\\r\\n2. **Golden Weight Staged**: Checkpoint-4400 achieved our project\'s lowest validation perplexity (2.68) and highest human evaluation scores.\\r\\n3. **Production Blueprint Published**: Completed the full-stack architecture bridging Next.js, FastAPI, vLLM, and Redis.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Dev Log #23, we enter the final milestone of Semester 1: building our dual-layer Safety Wrapper guardrail and calculating cloud GPU server budgets for university deployment!"},{"id":"2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","description":"Achieving peak model performance by training on the 2:1 emotional-to-daily dialogue ratio, balancing lucid conversational answers with empathetic care.","date":"2026-05-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"dataset","permalink":"/blog/tags/dataset"},{"inline":true,"label":"prompt","permalink":"/blog/tags/prompt"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","dataset","prompt","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-22T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Achieving peak model performance by training on the 2:1 emotional-to-daily dialogue ratio, balancing lucid conversational answers with empathetic care."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #22] 4th Fine-Tuning Triumph: Checkpoint-4400, Data Mix Ratios & Full System Architecture","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-30-maeumjigi-22"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Sim-to-Real: Hardware Transfer via Arduino Uno, HC-06 Bluetooth & ArUco Vision Tracking (#5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOn May 21st and 22nd, the Maeumjigi Capstone team embarked on massive data generation and preparation for the **4th Fine-Tuning (fine_tuning_4)**.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### The Tug-of-War Between Emotional and Daily Conversations\\n\\nWhile testing the 3rd fine-tuning model, I discovered a critical issue.\\n\\n> \\"When fine-tuning with the haiku_pred dataset, the responses seem too heavily skewed towards emotional counseling. How should we fine-tune to maintain the original model\'s daily conversation abilities?\\"\\n\\nOur goal is a \'suicide prevention counselor conveying warm empathy\', but the model was showing signs of **overfitting**, giving overly serious and emotional responses even to mundane questions like \\"How\'s the weather?\\" or \\"What should I eat?\\".\\n\\nTo solve this, we planned an experiment to train the model by mixing emotional data and daily chat data at appropriate ratios.\\n\\n### Generating 10,000 v6 Emotion Dataset Entries via Application Programming Interface (API)\\n\\n> \\"Unify to 512 tokens / Average 500 tokens per entry / 20 Personas / File: v6.jsonl\\"\\n\\nTo create the best dataset, I used the Anthropic API (Haiku) Batch mode to generate a staggering 10,000 entries of emotional conversation data (`Emotion_train_dataset_v6.jsonl`). Though there was a frustrating moment when the API batch ran in the background without showing progress, I filled in the missing entries with an additional script (`fill_v6.py`) to complete the 10,000 count.\\n\\n### Finding the Golden Ratio (fine_tuning_4)\\n\\n> \\"Emotion_train_dataset_v6.jsonl is complete. Now make a 7:3 ratio dataset, a 2:1 ratio, and a 5:5 ratio dataset.\\"\\n> \\"This 4th fine-tuning will be based on the training dataset mix ratio (mixed_2_1, mixed_5_5, mixed_7_3) instead of alpha values.\\"\\n\\n![Ratio Experiment](./images/02_ratio.png)\\n\\nThe 4th fine-tuning has now become an arena to verify which data ratio perfectly harmonizes the Artificial Intelligence (AI)\'s \'daily casualness\' with \'professional counseling\'. We generated three versions of the dataset and started injecting them into the Qwen3.5-9B model!"},{"id":"2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Sim-to-Real: Hardware Transfer via Arduino Uno, HC-06 Bluetooth & ArUco Vision Tracking (#5)","description":"Deploying the simulation-trained 648-bin Q-Learning policy onto a physical 2-wheeled Arduino Uno robot using ceiling webcam homography and real-time ArUco 6-DoF marker pose tracking.","date":"2026-05-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"sim-to-real","permalink":"/blog/tags/sim-to-real"},{"inline":true,"label":"arduino","permalink":"/blog/tags/arduino"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"computer-vision","permalink":"/blog/tags/computer-vision"},{"inline":true,"label":"aruco","permalink":"/blog/tags/aruco"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":5.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5","title":"[Webots Robotics] Sim-to-Real: Hardware Transfer via Arduino Uno, HC-06 Bluetooth & ArUco Vision Tracking (#5)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","sim-to-real","arduino","robotics","computer-vision","aruco","python"],"date":"2026-05-21T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Deploying the simulation-trained 648-bin Q-Learning policy onto a physical 2-wheeled Arduino Uno robot using ceiling webcam homography and real-time ArUco 6-DoF marker pose tracking.","keywords":["Sim-to-Real Hardware Transfer","ArUco Marker Pose Tracking","Arduino Uno Robot Navigation","HC-06 Bluetooth IPC","Webcam Homography Robotics"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #21] Optimizing Data Mix Ratios and Starting the 4th Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-22-maeumjigi-21"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20"}},"content":"![ArUco Marker Vision Tracking](./images/01_aruco_vision.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Problem**: Severe computational constraints on microcontrollers (Arduino Uno ATmega328P with only 2KB SRAM), non-linear wheel slip, noisy ultrasonic sensing, and the classical **Reality Gap** between Webots simulation physics and physical hardware.\\n> - **Solution**: Designed a **distributed I/O architecture (HC-06 Bluetooth at 115,200 baud)** where a host PC runs OpenCV vision processing and Q-Table inference while the Arduino handles low-level PWM motor actuation. Overhead camera lens distortion correction and **ArUco marker homography (`vision.py`)** deliver sub-centimeter real-time (x, y, \u03B8) tracking.\\n> - **Result**: Zero-shot transfer of the simulation-trained 648-state Q-Table achieved an **86.7% target reaching success rate (26/30 trials)** in a physical 1.2m \xd7 1.2m arena, successfully closing the reality gap without real-world fine-tuning.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The Challenge of Sim-to-Real: Navigating the Reality Gap\\n\\nIn virtual Webots simulations, surface friction coefficients remain idealized, wheel motors rotate with deterministic precision, and simulated distance sensors yield noiseless measurements. Real hardware, however, introduces unpredictable physical perturbations:\\n\\n1. **Severe Memory & Compute Bottlenecks**: The **Arduino Uno (ATmega328P)** operates with only 32KB flash memory and 2KB SRAM. Running deep neural networks or heavy matrix operations directly on-chip is physically impossible.\\n2. **Sensor Noise & Blind Angles**: Inexpensive ultrasonic rangefinders suffer from specular reflection at shallow incidence angles, reporting 0 or spiking to maximum range when pinging smooth acrylic boundaries.\\n3. **Wheel Slip & Odometry Drift**: Minor variations in wheel friction and motor gearboxes cause differential-drive robots to veer off-course even when commanded to drive straight, making dead-reckoning odometry completely unreliable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Distributed Control Architecture: Host PC (Brain) \u2194 Arduino (Muscle)\\n\\nTo overcome microcontroller limitations, we decoupled high-level reasoning from low-level actuation: **\\"PC as the brain, Arduino as the muscle.\\"**\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    subgraph CeilingCam[\\"Ceiling Vision System (Overhead Camera)\\"]\\n        Cam[\\"FHD Webcam (30 FPS)\\"]\\n        Aruco[\\"ArUco Marker Detection (DICT_4X4_50)<br>Perspective Homography (homography.npy)\\"]\\n        Cam --\x3e Aruco\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph PC[\\"Host PC (Python Engine)\\"]\\n        Pose[\\"Robot Pose Estimation (x, y, \u03B8)\\"]\\n        Disc[\\"648-bin Discretizer\\"]\\n        QL[\\"Trained Q-Table (\u03B5=0)\\"]\\n        BT_Host[\\"HC-06 Bluetooth SPP (115200 baud)\\"]\\n        \\n        Aruco --\x3e Pose --\x3e Disc --\x3e QL --\x3e BT_Host\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph Robot[\\"Physical Mobile Robot (Real Hardware)\\"]\\n        BT_Slave[\\"HC-06 Bluetooth Receiver\\"]\\n        MCU[\\"Arduino Uno (Firmware)\\"]\\n        Motors[\\"L298N Dual H-Bridge + Geared DC Motors\\"]\\n        \\n        BT_Host <--\x3e|\\"Action Byte [0..3]<br>Ack/Status\\"| BT_Slave --\x3e MCU --\x3e Motors\\n    end\\n```\\n\\n* **Host PC Pipeline**: An overhead webcam captures the complete arena top-down, tracking an ArUco fiducial marker mounted on top of the robot at 30 FPS. The robot\'s planar pose `(x, y, \u03B8)` is calculated and mapped into the discrete 648-state space to retrieve the optimal greedy action from the Q-Table.\\n* **HC-06 Serial Communication**: To minimize Round-Trip Time (RTT) over the Bluetooth Serial Port Profile (SPP), the transmission baud rate was upgraded from the default 9,600 to **115,200 bps**, sustaining a low-latency 30~50Hz control loop.\\n* **Arduino Firmware (`arduino_firmware.ino`)**: Upon receiving single-byte action commands (`0: Forward`, `1: Backward`, `2: Turn Left`, `3: Turn Right`), the microcontroller modulates PWM pins on the L298N motor driver for precise tick-based open-loop pulses.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Overhead Camera Homography & ArUco Vision Tracking\\n\\n![Hardware Setup and Arena Calibration](./images/02_hardware_setup.png)\\n\\nTo measure robot positioning and obstacle proximity with millimeter accuracy across the real arena, we built an **OpenCV-based top-down computer vision pipeline**.\\n\\n### \u{1F4D0} Perspective Rectification & Homography (`calib_auto.py`)\\n1. **Checkerboard Distortion Calibration**: To eliminate barrel distortion from wide-angle webcam lenses, intrinsic camera matrix (K) and distortion coefficients (D) were pre-computed using a standard 9\xd76 checkerboard.\\n2. **Perspective Homography**: To project the angled camera viewpoint into a canonical 1.2m \xd7 1.2m square bird\'s-eye view, a 3\xd73 perspective homography matrix (`homography.npy`) was established from the 4 marked arena corner coordinates.\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} ArUco Marker Pose Tracking Implementation (`vision.py`)\\n\\n```python\\nimport cv2\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\n# Load ArUco dictionary and detection parameters\\naruco_dict = cv2.aruco.getPredefinedDictionary(cv2.aruco.DICT_4X4_50)\\nparameters = cv2.aruco.DetectorParameters()\\ndetector = cv2.aruco.ArucoDetector(aruco_dict, parameters)\\n\\ndef get_robot_pose(frame, homography_matrix):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Calculates planar coordinates (x, y) and heading angle (theta) from rectified image.\\"\\"\\"\\n    warped = cv2.warpPerspective(frame, homography_matrix, (600, 600))\\n    corners, ids, _ = detector.detectMarkers(warped)\\n    \\n    if ids is not None and len(ids) > 0:\\n        c = corners[0][0]\\n        center_x = float(np.mean(c[:, 0]))\\n        center_y = float(np.mean(c[:, 1]))\\n        \\n        # Calculate heading angle (theta) from marker front edge vector\\n        front_x = (c[0][0] + c[1][0]) / 2.0\\n        front_y = (c[0][1] + c[1][1]) / 2.0\\n        theta = np.arctan2(front_y - center_y, front_x - center_x)\\n        \\n        return center_x, center_y, theta\\n    return None\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Hardware Troubleshooting & Real-World Deployment Results\\n\\nTransitioning from simulation to physical operation revealed critical edge cases addressed through robust engineering countermeasures:\\n\\n| Symptom (Troubleshooting) | Root Cause | Engineering Solution |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Robot drifts sideways during forward command** | Gearbox friction differences between left & right DC motors | Implemented an interactive calibration script (`ble_vision_turn_calib.py`) to apply dynamic PWM trim offsets |\\n| **Runaway behavior during Bluetooth disconnect** | Packet drops under RF interference causing robot to retain last command | Embedded a 200ms communication watchdog timer in firmware to immediately cut motor power upon signal loss |\\n| **Intermittent ArUco marker detection loss** | Specular glare from ceiling fluorescent lighting on glossy marker surface | Fine-tuned adaptive thresholding parameters and added a 1D Kalman Filter position extrapolator |\\n\\n### \u{1F3C6} Final Real-World Benchmark\\n* **Autonomous Goal Reaching Rate**: Tested across 30 independent trials in the physical 1.2m \xd7 1.2m obstacle arena, the robot achieved an **86.7% success rate (26/30 successful arrivals)**.\\n* Leveraging just **2,592 pre-computed discrete Q-values (~2.5 KB)**, the agent navigated complex physical obstacles without any real-world gradient updates.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Concluding the RL Q-Learning Dev Log Series\\n\\nWith this final Sim-to-Real deployment, our 5-part **RL Q-Learning Robotics series** reaches its conclusion:\\n\\n1. **#1**: 648-Bin State Discretization & Webots TCP Socket IPC Architecture\\n2. **#2**: Tabular Q-Learning \u03B5-Decay Exploration & Multi-Objective Reward Shaping\\n3. **#3**: Overcoming Arena Local Minima in 12-Wall Environments via Headless Pipelines\\n4. **#4**: Quantitative Benchmark with `evaluate_qlearning` vs. SARSA & DQN\\n5. **#5**: Sim-to-Real Hardware Transfer via Arduino Uno, Bluetooth SPP & ArUco Vision\\n\\nFrom foundational simulator modeling and algorithmic tuning to multi-seed automation and physical hardware deployment, this series documented the complete lifecycle of reinforcement learning for embedded robotics. Stay tuned for future deep dives into our next projects!"},{"id":"2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","description":"Designing our 3rd fine-tuning strategy, carefully calibrating the ratio between casual chat and crisis response to prevent emotional over-triggering.","date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"backend","permalink":"/blog/tags/backend"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/blog/tags/frontend"},{"inline":true,"label":"database","permalink":"/blog/tags/database"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.88,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","backend","frontend","database","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Designing our 3rd fine-tuning strategy, carefully calibrating the ratio between casual chat and crisis response to prevent emotional over-triggering."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Sim-to-Real: Hardware Transfer via Arduino Uno, HC-06 Bluetooth & ArUco Vision Tracking (#5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-21-rl-qlearning-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOver the two days of May 19th and 20th, the automation of evaluation for the 3rd fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_3`) and the enhancement of backend/frontend features for building the actual app service proceeded in parallel.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Concluding the 3rd Fine-Tuning and Automation Scripts\\n\\nBased on the insights gained from the previous fine-tuning, the training for the 3rd fine-tuning, which was set up with concepts of knowledge graphs and ontologies in mind, was completed.\\n\\n> \\"Select the checkpoint with the Best Eval from the experimental models in the fine_tuning_3 folder, replace the files in the adapter folder, and report the results.\\"\\n> \\"Write a run_test_20_30.sh file that runs inference and evaluation in parallel for fine_tuning_3 as well.\\"\\n\\nInstead of manually digging through folders to copy checkpoints like before, I instructed the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to write a script that automatically extracted the best performing model weights (Best Checkpoint) and set them up in the `adapter` folder. Then, I executed the parallel inference script, `run_test_20_30.sh`, throwing the evaluation for the 4 Alias models to the background server.\\n\\n### Backend/Frontend: Chatbot Database (DB) Saving and Community Board Features\\n\\nWhile the models were hard at work running inferences on the server, I accelerated the `mental-health` DB and app integration work in my local environment.\\n\\n> \\"Summarize seed_data.sql. What is the pw for users? Is it Test1234!? What was the db I was trying to apply this sql to?\\"\\n\\nTo break through the login issue that caused the frustrating `401 Unauthorized` error last time, I thoroughly examined `seed_data.sql` to verify the password hashing and user data.\\n\\nAs a result, I was able to leave a meaningful Git commit on the afternoon of May 20th:\\n`feat: Added chatbot DB save, board pagination, and emotion diary filter`\\n\\n![App Features](./images/02_app_feature.png)\\n\\nFinally, the conversation logs of our fine-tuned Large Language Model (LLM) chatbot officially started being saved to the DB! In addition, the pagination feature for the community board where users can communicate, and the emotion diary filter feature for recording daily moods were successfully added to the frontend.\\n\\nIt was a thrilling moment when the massive, invisible fine-tuned model finally began to be served in the beautiful vessel of a mobile app."},{"id":"2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","description":"Zero-exploration (\u03B5=0) deterministic evaluation pipeline with evaluate_qlearning.py, examining path efficiency, near-miss metrics, and benchmark comparisons against DQN and SARSA.","date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"evaluation","permalink":"/blog/tags/evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"benchmark","permalink":"/blog/tags/benchmark"},{"inline":true,"label":"dqn","permalink":"/blog/tags/dqn"},{"inline":true,"label":"sarsa","permalink":"/blog/tags/sarsa"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4","title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","evaluation","benchmark","dqn","sarsa","robotics","python"],"date":"2026-05-20T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Zero-exploration (\u03B5=0) deterministic evaluation pipeline with evaluate_qlearning.py, examining path efficiency, near-miss metrics, and benchmark comparisons against DQN and SARSA.","keywords":["Deterministic Policy Evaluation","Near-Miss Obstacle Metric","Q-Learning vs DQN vs SARSA","Autonomous Navigation Benchmark","Zero Exploration Inference"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #20] 3rd Fine-Tuning Complete & Backend Feature Enhancements","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-maeumjigi-20"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3"}},"content":"![Success and Steps Benchmark](./images/01_eval_success_steps.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Objective**: Measure the pure, deterministic performance of the learned greedy policy (\u03B5=0) without exploration noise, validating real-world deployability.\\n> - **Methodology**: Implemented `evaluate_qlearning.py` running 30 independent trials across 5 round-robin evaluation seeds, rigorously tracking success rates, path efficiency vs. theoretical shortest routes, and obstacle near-miss counts.\\n> - **Result**: Tabular Q-Learning attained a 93.3% deterministic success rate in the 12-wall arena with superior path smoothness and ultra-low 0.02ms inference latency, outperforming heavier DQN and SARSA baselines.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Quantitative Evaluation Pipeline (`evaluate_qlearning.py`)\\n\\nTraining reward curves are inherently noisy due to \u03B5-greedy exploration. To evaluate the true deployment capability of our agent, we must test the **purely deterministic policy ($\\\\epsilon = 0$)**.\\n\\nWe built an evaluation runner that loads the saved Q-table (`qlearning_qtable.npy`) and evaluates the agent across 5 distinct test seeds (`EVAL_SEEDS = (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)`).\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    Model[\\"Trained Q-Table<br>(qlearning_qtable.npy)\\"] --\x3e Agent[\\"Greedy Agent<br>(\u03B5 = 0 Fixed)\\"]\\n    EvalSeeds[\\"5 Test Seeds<br>(EVAL_SEEDS 0..4)\\"] --\x3e Webots[\\"Webots Simulator<br>(30 Evaluation Trials)\\"]\\n    Agent <--\x3e Webots\\n    Webots --\x3e CSV[\\"Trace & Metric Logging<br>(eval_log.csv / traces)\\"]\\n```\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} Evaluation Loop Architecture\\n\\n```python\\n# evaluate_qlearning.py excerpt\\nagent = QLearningAgent(seed=0)\\nagent.load(model_path) # Load trained Q-Table\\n\\nfor ep in range(n_episodes):\\n    env_seed = EVAL_SEEDS[ep % len(EVAL_SEEDS)]\\n    raw, info = env.reset(seed=env_seed, return_info=True)\\n    s = discretize(raw)\\n    \\n    for t in range(1, max_steps + 1):\\n        a = agent.act(s, deterministic=True) # Pure argmax Q(s, a)\\n        raw_next, r, done, info = env.step(a)\\n        s = discretize(raw_next)\\n        \\n        # Log trajectory coordinates and near-misses (dist <= 0.15m)\\n        record_metrics(info)\\n        if done: break\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comprehensive Evaluation Metrics\\n\\n1. **Success Rate**: Percentage of runs reaching the target radius (0.10m) within 150 steps without collision.\\n2. **Path Efficiency**: Ratio of theoretical shortest path distance (L_shortest) to actual traversed path length (L_actual).\\n   **Path Efficiency (%) = (Shortest Path / Actual Path) \xd7 100%**\\n3. **Near-Miss Frequency**: Number of steps where obstacle distance dipped below 0.15m.\\n4. **Oscillation Count**: Metric penalizing back-and-forth steering jitter.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Algorithm Benchmark: Tabular Q-Learning vs. SARSA vs. DQN\\n\\n![Trajectory Quality & Benchmarks](./images/02_eval_quality.png)\\n\\nComparative evaluation in the identical Webots 12-wall arena across the three reinforcement learning algorithms revealed clear performance trade-offs:\\n\\n| Metric | Tabular Q-Learning (Ours) | SARSA (On-policy) | Deep Q-Network (DQN) |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\n| **Learning Paradigm** | Off-policy TD(0) | On-policy TD(0) | Neural Network (Deep RL) |\\n| **Parameter Size** | **2,592 floats (~2.5 KB)** | 2,592 floats (~2.5 KB) | ~150,000 weights (~600 KB) |\\n| **Deterministic Success Rate (`obstacle12`)** | **93.3%** | 86.7% | 90.0% |\\n| **Average Steps per Episode** | **41.2 steps** | 48.5 steps | 44.7 steps |\\n| **Per-Step Inference Latency** | **0.02 ms** (NumPy array lookup) | 0.02 ms | 2.15 ms (PyTorch forward pass) |\\n| **Near-Miss Frequency** | Low (balanced detour) | Minimal (excessively conservative) | Moderate |\\n\\n### \u{1F50D} In-Depth Behavioral Analysis\\n* **Why Q-Learning Excels**: By evaluating `max_a\' Q(s\', a\')`, off-policy Q-Learning ignores transient exploratory collisions during training, settling on the **shortest feasible detour path**.\\n* **SARSA\'s Conservative Bias**: On-policy updates factor in exploration penalties, causing SARSA to over-correct and choose excessively long, inefficient detours to avoid walls at all costs.\\n* **DQN\'s Trade-off**: DQN handles continuous inputs well, but training stability requires replay buffers and target networks, while its 2.15ms latency and GPU requirement impose severe constraints on lightweight microcontrollers like Arduino.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Evaluation Takeaways\\n\\nOur discrete 648-bin **Tabular Q-Learning model requires just 2.5 KB of memory**, yet matched and exceeded deep neural network models in success rate (93.3%) and real-time execution speed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\n\\nWith simulation benchmarks thoroughly concluded, the ultimate milestone remains: **transferring policy to reality (Sim-to-Real)**.\\n\\nIn our final Episode #5, we will detail the hardware integration: deploying our policy to a **2-wheel differential Arduino Uno robot, communicating via HC-06 Bluetooth, and tracking ground-truth position via ceiling webcam checkerboard calibration and ArUco markers**!"},{"id":"2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","description":"Overcoming the Local Minima dilemma in complex 12-wall obstacle environments (obstacle12) and orchestrating high-speed automated multi-seed Webots simulations.","date":"2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"obstacle-avoidance","permalink":"/blog/tags/obstacle-avoidance"},{"inline":true,"label":"local-minima","permalink":"/blog/tags/local-minima"},{"inline":true,"label":"headless","permalink":"/blog/tags/headless"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3","title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","webots","obstacle-avoidance","local-minima","headless","python"],"date":"2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Overcoming the Local Minima dilemma in complex 12-wall obstacle environments (obstacle12) and orchestrating high-speed automated multi-seed Webots simulations.","keywords":["Local Minima Reinforcement Learning","Webots Headless Simulation","Obstacle Avoidance Multi-Seed","Deep RL vs Tabular Q","PowerShell Automated Training"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Deterministic Evaluation via evaluate_qlearning & Benchmark vs. DQN and SARSA (#4)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-20-rl-qlearning-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2"}},"content":"![obstacle12 Simulation Arena](./images/01_obstacle12_arena.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Problem**: When a goal lies directly behind an obstacle, the myopic nature of distance-delta rewards (+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist) pulls the robot into head-on collisions, trapping it in severe **Local Minima**.\\n> - **Solution**: Integrated 3-way head-scan obstacle perception (front/left/right) with long-horizon Bellman value propagation (\u03B3=0.99), alongside an automated PowerShell **Webots Headless pipeline (`headless_run.ps1`)** executing multi-seed training across seeds 0, 1, and 2.\\n> - **Result**: The agent successfully learned robust detour policies in the 12-wall arena, achieving a 90%+ goal success rate after 600 episodes.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The Obstacle Challenge: The Local Minima Trap\\n\\nIn an empty arena (`no_obstacle`), navigating is simply a matter of steering toward the target heading. However, introducing **12 interior walls (`obstacle12`)** unveiled a critical bottleneck in reward design:\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    Start[\\"Start (Robot)\\"]\\n    Wall[\\"Obstacle Wall\\"]\\n    Goal[\\"Target Goal\\"]\\n    \\n    Start --\x3e|\\"Direct drive (greedy distance reward)\\"| Wall\\n    Wall -.->|\\"Collision & deadlocked (Local Minima)\\"| Wall\\n    Start ==>|\\"Detour Policy<br>Accepts temporary distance penalty\\"| Goal\\n```\\n\\n* **The Dilemma**: When the target is directly on the other side of an interior wall, driving straight ahead leads directly to collision.\\n* **Reward Conflict**: Circumnavigating the wall requires moving temporarily away from the goal (negative distance delta). Under naive exploration, the agent enters a deadlock\u2014spinning or timing out against the wall.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Solution Strategy: Head-Scan Perception & Value Backpropagation\\n\\nOvercoming this trap relies on coupling **3-directional head-scan perception** with a **high discount factor (\u03B3 = 0.99)**:\\n\\n1. **Perceptual State Discretization**:\\n   * Attempting to advance while `front_distance = Danger` yields a heavy collision penalty (-1.0).\\n   * Sweeping the sensor head left and right reveals free avenues (`left_distance = Safe` or `right_distance = Safe`), encouraging lateral turns.\\n2. **Long-Term Temporal Difference Credit Assignment**:\\n   * Although turning away temporarily depresses the step reward, successfully rounding the wall and reaching the target yields the substantial terminal reward (+1.0).\\n   * Backpropagated through Q(s, a) with \u03B3 = 0.99, the agent gradually learns that detour steps possess higher expected return than head-on approaches.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Webots Headless Automation Pipeline\\n\\nRigorous reinforcement learning requires **multi-seed validation (Seeds 0, 1, 2)**. Running 1,500 episodes interactively in the Webots GUI three times would take many hours.\\n\\nWe automated this with a **`headless_run.ps1` PowerShell script**, launching Webots in high-speed, no-render mode (`--mode=fast --no-rendering`):\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} Automated Pipeline (`headless_run.ps1`)\\n\\n```powershell\\n# Launch Webots in background headless fast mode\\n$webotsProc = Start-Process -FilePath $WebotsExe `\\n    -ArgumentList @(\\"--mode=fast\\", \\"--no-rendering\\", \\"--stdout\\", \\"--stderr\\", $WorldFile) `\\n    -PassThru\\n\\n# Train agent over socket IPC\\npython train_qlearning.py --scenario obstacle12 --seed 0 --episodes 1500\\n\\n# Cleanup process and generate performance plots\\nStop-Process -Id $webotsProc.Id -Force\\npython -m qlearning.plot_curves --log-dir logs/obstacle12/seed_0\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Empirical Evaluation: Cross-Environment Learning Curves\\n\\n![Cross-Environment Learning Curves](./images/02_comparison_curves.png)\\n\\nThe graph above contrasts convergence across 3 random seeds between the unobstructed world (`no_obstacle`) and the 12-wall obstacle world (`obstacle12`).\\n\\n### \u{1F4CA} Benchmark Metrics\\n\\n| Metric | Empty Arena (`no_obstacle`) | 12-Wall Arena (`obstacle12`) |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\\n| **Initial Convergence** | ~300 - 400 episodes | ~600 - 800 episodes |\\n| **Final Success Rate (100-ep MA)** | **98.5% - 99.2%** | **91.4% - 94.8%** |\\n| **Mean Steps per Episode** | 15 - 22 steps | 38 - 52 steps (detour path) |\\n| **Cross-Seed Variance** | Negligible (highly stable) | Moderate (exploration path dependency) |\\n\\n* **Extended Exploration**: In `obstacle12`, discovering viable detour corridors requires approximately 200 additional episodes of random trials, keeping the average reward depressed until episode 400.\\n* **Detour Mastery**: Beyond episode 800, all seeds stabilize above 90% success, confirming that the agent reliably maneuvers through tight gaps without collisions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\n\\nOur 648-bin Tabular Q-Learning model successfully navigates maze-like obstacle configurations.\\n\\nIn Episode #4, we will load the saved model checkpoints (`models/*.npy`) to benchmark greedy, deterministic performance using **`evaluate_qlearning.py` and analyze comparative metrics against DQN and SARSA**!"},{"id":"2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","description":"In-depth breakdown of Tabular Q-Learning TD(0) update formulas, exponential epsilon-decay exploration, and dense reward shaping to overcome sparse rewards in mobile robot navigation.","date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"reward-shaping","permalink":"/blog/tags/reward-shaping"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"machine-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/machine-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":4.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2","title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","reward-shaping","webots","robotics","machine-learning","python"],"date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"In-depth breakdown of Tabular Q-Learning TD(0) update formulas, exponential epsilon-decay exploration, and dense reward shaping to overcome sparse rewards in mobile robot navigation.","keywords":["Q-Learning Reward Shaping","Epsilon Greedy Exploration","Sparse Reward Reinforcement Learning","Bellman Optimality TD","Robot Obstacle Avoidance"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Conquering Local Minima in 12-Wall Obstacles: Multi-Seed Headless Simulation (#3)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-19-rl-qlearning-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automated Evaluation Reports & FastAPI PostgreSQL 401 Debugging","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19"}},"content":"![Training Reward Curve](./images/01_reward_curve.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Problem**: Sparse reward setups (+1.0 at goal, -1.0 at collision) cause severe exploration delays, causing the agent to wander or spin for thousands of episodes before stumbling upon the target.\\n> - **Solution**: Implemented a multi-objective **Dense Reward Shaping** mechanism combining potential-based Euclidean distance delta rewards (+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist) with step (-0.01), turning (-0.01), and backward (-0.05) penalties, paired with exponential epsilon decay (1.0 \u2192 0.05).\\n> - **Result**: The moving average reward shifted into a steady positive regime (0.7+) within 500 episodes in the `no_obstacle` scenario, achieving a 98%+ goal success rate.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Tabular Q-Learning Update Formula & Agent Architecture\\n\\nTabular Q-Learning is an **off-policy Temporal Difference (TD(0))** algorithm grounded in the Bellman Optimality Equation.\\n\\nThe agent maintains an action-value table Q(s, a) structured as a 2D NumPy array of shape $(648, 4)$. Using the experience transition tuple $(s, a, r, s\', done)$ streamed from the Webots socket server, the $Q$-values are updated in real time via:\\n\\n### \u{1F4D0} Q-Learning TD(0) Update Rule\\n\\n```text\\nTarget = r + \u03B3 * max_a\' Q(s\', a\') * (1 - done)\\nTD Error = Target - Q(s, a)\\nQ(s, a) <- Q(s, a) + \u03B1 * TD Error\\n```\\n\\n* **Learning Rate (\u03B1 = 0.1)**: Balances the rate of incorporating new feedback versus retaining historical estimates.\\n* **Discount Factor (\u03B3 = 0.99)**: Values long-term future returns, critical for multi-step navigation.\\n* **Terminal Masking (`1 - done`)**: Clamps future expectations to zero upon goal arrival or collision, isolating the terminal reward.\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} Agent Core Implementation (`qlearning_agent.py`)\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\nclass QLearningAgent:\\n    def __init__(self, n_states=648, n_actions=4, alpha=0.1, gamma=0.99,\\n                 eps_start=1.0, eps_min=0.05, eps_decay=0.995, seed=0):\\n        self.rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n        self.Q = np.zeros((n_states, n_actions), dtype=np.float64)\\n        self.alpha = float(alpha)\\n        self.gamma = float(gamma)\\n        self.epsilon = float(eps_start)\\n        self.eps_min = float(eps_min)\\n        self.eps_decay = float(eps_decay)\\n        self.n_actions = int(n_actions)\\n\\n    def act(self, s: int, deterministic: bool = False) -> int:\\n        \\"\\"\\"\u03B5-Greedy behavior policy: Exploration vs. Exploitation\\"\\"\\"\\n        if (not deterministic) and self.rng.random() < self.epsilon:\\n            return int(self.rng.integers(self.n_actions))\\n        return int(np.argmax(self.Q[s]))\\n\\n    def update(self, s: int, a: int, r: float, s_next: int, done: bool) -> float:\\n        \\"\\"\\"TD(0) Off-policy Q-Table update\\"\\"\\"\\n        target = r if done else r + self.gamma * float(np.max(self.Q[s_next]))\\n        td_error = target - self.Q[s, a]\\n        self.Q[s, a] += self.alpha * td_error\\n        return float(td_error)\\n\\n    def decay_epsilon(self):\\n        \\"\\"\\"Exponential decay executed at the end of each episode\\"\\"\\"\\n        self.epsilon = max(self.eps_min, self.epsilon * self.eps_decay)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Exponential Epsilon-Greedy Decay Strategy\\n\\nAt the start of training, the agent has zero domain knowledge, requiring **\u03B5 = 1.0 (100% random exploration)** to discover the arena boundaries.\\n\\nHowever, an uncurbed exploration rate induces perpetual collisions even after the optimal path has been resolved. We apply per-episode exponential decay:\\n\\n**\u03B5_(ep+1) = max(\u03B5_min, \u03B5_ep \xd7 0.995)**\\n\\n* **Episode 1**: \u03B5 = 1.0 (Full exploration)\\n* **Episode 300**: \u03B5 \u2248 0.22 (Shift toward exploiting high Q-values)\\n* **Episode 600**: \u03B5 = 0.05 (Floor exploration rate \u03B5_min, 95% greedy execution)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Dense Reward Shaping for Sparse Navigation\\n\\nA fundamental challenge in mobile robotics is **sparse reward feedback**. In a 1.2m \xd7 1.2m arena, moving randomly yields zero reward until the robot coincidentally intersects the $0.10m$ goal tolerance.\\n\\nTo guide convergence, we engineered a **Dense Reward Shaping** schedule inside the Webots controller (`RL_controller.py`):\\n\\n| Reward Component | Value | Design Objective & Physical Effect |\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\\n| **Goal Reached (`REWARD_GOAL`)** | **+1.0** | Terminal reward on successful target arrival |\\n| **Wall/Obstacle Collision (`REWARD_COLLISION`)** | **-1.0** | Severe penalty on physical impact |\\n| **Distance Delta Reward (`REWARD_DELTA_SCALE`)** | **+0.1 \xd7 \u0394dist** | Immediate feedback proportional to Euclidean distance reduction |\\n| **Step Time Penalty (`REWARD_STEP`)** | **-0.01** | Prevents idling and incentivizes shortest paths |\\n| **Turning Penalty (`REWARD_TURN`)** | **-0.01** | Suppresses unproductive spinning behavior |\\n| **Backward Penalty (`REWARD_BACKWARD`)** | **-0.05** | Discourages inefficient reverse driving |\\n\\n### \u{1F50D} Mathematical Principle of Distance Delta\\n**\u0394dist = dist_prev - dist_curr**\\n* Driving $0.05m$ toward the target awards $+0.1 \\\\times 0.05 = +0.005$ immediately.\\n* Conversely, driving away incurs an instant penalty, allowing the agent to align its heading with the target within the first 100 episodes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Empirical Evaluation: Reward and Success Curves\\n\\nTraining for 1,500 episodes in the `no_obstacle` world demonstrated remarkable convergence matching our theoretical expectations.\\n\\n![Success Rate Curve](./images/02_success_curve.png)\\n\\n### \u{1F4C8} Convergence Milestones\\n1. **Episodes 0 ~ 200 (Exploration Phase)**:\\n   * Frequent wall collisions and 150-step timeouts keep the moving average reward in the negative range (-0.4 ~ -0.2).\\n2. **Episodes 200 ~ 500 (Ascent Phase)**:\\n   * As distance-delta rewards propagate through the Q-Table, the policy establishes a strong forward-driving preference whenever `goal_heading` is centered.\\n   * Success rate surges from 20% to **over 80%**.\\n3. **Episodes 500 ~ 1,500 (Asymptotic Convergence)**:\\n   * With \u03B5 settled at 0.05, average steps per episode plummet from 150 (timeout) to **15 ~ 22 steps**.\\n   * The 100-episode moving average success rate converges above **98.5%**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\n\\nWe have proven that Tabular Q-Learning over 648 states solves empty arena navigation cleanly.\\n\\nHowever, **real-world environments are packed with walls and obstacles**. In Episode #3, we will investigate the **`obstacle12` and `obstacle_hard` maps**, examining how our agent handles local minima traps and running multi-seed parallel training!"},{"id":"2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automated Evaluation Reports & FastAPI PostgreSQL 401 Debugging","description":"Overcoming full-stack hurdles: generating automated LLM benchmark reports with Claude Code, resolving cryptic JWT 401 Unauthorized errors in PostgreSQL, and designing our hybrid Knowledge Graph architecture.","date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"automated-reporting","permalink":"/blog/tags/automated-reporting"},{"inline":true,"label":"fastapi","permalink":"/blog/tags/fastapi"},{"inline":true,"label":"postgresql","permalink":"/blog/tags/postgresql"},{"inline":true,"label":"authentication","permalink":"/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-graph","permalink":"/blog/tags/knowledge-graph"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":4.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automated Evaluation Reports & FastAPI PostgreSQL 401 Debugging","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","automated-reporting","fastapi","postgresql","authentication","troubleshooting","knowledge-graph","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["FastAPI PostgreSQL Integration","401 Unauthorized Troubleshooting","JWT Authentication Salt Mismatch","LLM Evaluation Report Automation","Knowledge Graph Chatbot Architecture"],"description":"Overcoming full-stack hurdles: generating automated LLM benchmark reports with Claude Code, resolving cryptic JWT 401 Unauthorized errors in PostgreSQL, and designing our hybrid Knowledge Graph architecture."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Tabular Q-Learning Implementation & Reward Shaping Tuning (#2)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-18-rl-qlearning-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nMid-May 2026 was supposed to be a calm interval after wrapping up our 2nd fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_2`). Instead, May 17th hit our team with a relentless double challenge: condensing hundreds of raw inference metrics into an automated technical report, while simultaneously battling a cryptic `HTTP 401 Unauthorized` cascading error across our newly integrated **FastAPI** and **PostgreSQL** backend.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the complete post-mortem of how we automated our reporting and systematically neutralized the backend authentication breakdown.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. Autonomous Fine-Tuning Evaluation Reporting\\r\\n\\r\\nAnalyzing the 2nd fine-tuning results required processing hundreds of inference samples across multiple checkpoint variants (comparing Alpha values: 16, 32, and 64). Doing this manually was mathematically unfeasible given our capstone deadlines.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe engineered a structured prompt document (`report_prompt.md`) that guided **Claude Code** to analyze raw JSON evaluation matrices and automatically synthesize an executive technical report:\\r\\n\\r\\n```markdown\\r\\n# Excerpt from report_prompt.md\\r\\n1. Parse evaluation metrics (BERTScore, ROUGE-L, Perplexity) from /eval/results_alpha_*.json.\\r\\n2. Group model variants by empathy score, conversational naturalness, and safety compliance.\\r\\n3. Highlight critical failure cases (e.g., hyper-formal robotic apologies vs conversational warmth).\\r\\n4. Output the synthesis directly into a publication-ready Word document structure.\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThe AI agent generated `fine_tuning_2_report.docx` in under 3 minutes, complete with comparative analysis tables and qualitative excerpt breakdowns.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. The Nightmare of HTTP 401 Unauthorized in PostgreSQL\\r\\n\\r\\nWith reporting streamlined, we restored our staging database dump (`backup.sql`) into a local PostgreSQL instance to test end-to-end user session persistence. Although the database restored with zero SQL syntax errors, our Next.js frontend crashed into an inescapable login loop:\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nINFO: 127.0.0.1:54322 - \\"POST /auth/login HTTP/1.1\\" 401 Unauthorized\\r\\nDETAIL: {\\"error\\": \\"Invalid credentials or malformed signature\\"}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nEven with verified test credentials (`test@maeumjigi.kr` / `Password123!`), the FastAPI authentication service persistently rejected every login handshake.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nsequenceDiagram\\r\\n    autonumber\\r\\n    actor Client as Next.js Web Client\\r\\n    participant API as FastAPI Gateway (/auth/login)\\r\\n    participant DB as PostgreSQL Database\\r\\n    participant Auth as Passlib Bcrypt Verifier\\r\\n\\r\\n    Client->>API: POST /auth/login {email, password}\\r\\n    API->>DB: SELECT id, password_hash FROM users WHERE email=...\\r\\n    DB--\x3e>API: Returns $2a$12$... hash string\\r\\n    API->>Auth: bcrypt.verify(\\"Password123!\\", db_hash)\\r\\n    Note over Auth: Trap 1: Salt algorithm mismatch ($2a$ vs $2b$)!\\r\\n    Auth--\x3e>API: Verification Failed (False)\\r\\n    API--\x3e>Client: 401 Unauthorized\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Root Cause Investigation & debug_login.py\\r\\n\\r\\nTo isolate whether the breakdown stemmed from frontend CORS headers, database corruption, or JWT secret misconfigurations, we wrote a standalone diagnostic script: `debug_login.py`.\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Standalone authentication diagnostic utility: debug_login.py\\r\\nimport sys\\r\\nfrom passlib.context import CryptContext\\r\\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine, text\\r\\n\\r\\nDATABASE_URL = \\"postgresql://postgres:secret@localhost:5432/maeumjigi\\"\\r\\npwd_context = CryptContext(schemes=[\\"bcrypt\\"], deprecated=\\"auto\\")\\r\\n\\r\\ndef diagnose_user_auth(email: str, raw_pass: str):\\r\\n    engine = create_engine(DATABASE_URL)\\r\\n    with engine.connect() as conn:\\r\\n        result = conn.execute(text(\\"SELECT password_hash FROM users WHERE email = :e\\"), {\\"e\\": email}).fetchone()\\r\\n        if not result:\\r\\n            print(\\"[FAIL] User not found in database!\\")\\r\\n            return\\r\\n        \\r\\n        stored_hash = result[0]\\r\\n        print(f\\"[INFO] Retrieved hash: {stored_hash[:15]}...\\")\\r\\n        \\r\\n        # Verify hash algorithm version compatibility\\r\\n        is_valid = pwd_context.verify(raw_pass, stored_hash)\\r\\n        print(f\\"[RESULT] Password Match Status: {is_valid}\\")\\r\\n\\r\\nif __name__ == \\"__main__\\":\\r\\n    diagnose_user_auth(\\"test@maeumjigi.kr\\", \\"Password123!\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nRunning this diagnostic exposed three compounding bugs:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Failure Vector | Technical Root Cause | Resolution Applied |\\r\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **Bcrypt Salt Version** | Database dump contained `$2a$` hashes from older PHP/Node seeds; modern `passlib` expected `$2b$`. | Configured `pwd_context = CryptContext(schemes=[\\"bcrypt\\"], bcrypt__ident=\\"2b\\", deprecated=\\"auto\\")`. |\\r\\n| **JWT Secret Encoding** | Secret string was loaded via `.env` with trailing newline `\\r\\r\\n`, breaking HMAC-SHA256 signature checks. | Sanitized key ingestion: `SECRET_KEY = os.getenv(\\"JWT_SECRET\\").strip().encode(\'utf-8\')`. |\\r\\n| **CORS Preflight** | Chrome browser blocked `Authorization: Bearer` headers during OPTIONS preflight checks. | Explicitly registered `CORSMiddleware` with `allow_headers=[\\"*\\"]` and `expose_headers=[\\"*\\"]`. |\\r\\n\\r\\nWithin 45 minutes of targeted refactoring, login latency dropped to 42ms with a 100% success rate!\\r\\n\\r\\n![DB Troubleshooting](./images/02_db.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Architectural Expansion: Exploring Hybrid Knowledge Graphs\\r\\n\\r\\nResolving backend stability opened room for deeper architectural exploration. During clinical evaluation of our 2nd fine-tuned model, we noticed an intrinsic weakness shared by all parametric LLMs: **factual hallucinations during multi-hop crisis reasoning**.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph User_Query [\\"User Input: Complex Crisis State\\"]\\r\\n        U[\\"I stopped taking my SSRI antidepressants 3 days ago and feel severe tremors.\\"]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Hybrid_Architecture [\\"Hybrid Symbolic-Neural Architecture\\"]\\r\\n        U --\x3e KG_Extractor[Clinical Entity & Relation Extractor]\\r\\n        KG_Extractor --\x3e|Cypher Query| KG[(Neo4j Medical Knowledge Graph)]\\r\\n        KG --\x3e|Verified Triples: SSRI -> Discontinuation Syndrome| Context_Injector[Clinical Context Injector]\\r\\n        \\r\\n        U --\x3e Direct_Prompt[Empathetic System Prompt]\\r\\n        Direct_Prompt & Context_Injector --\x3e FineTuned_LLM[Fine-Tuned Qwen / Llama 3.1]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Verified_Output [\\"Safety-Compliant Response\\"]\\r\\n        FineTuned_LLM --\x3e Output[\\"Empathetic validation + Immediate medical warning regarding SSRI discontinuation syndrome.\\"]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nBy drafting `chatbot_design_prompt.md`, we laid the theoretical foundation for blending parametric LLM conversational empathy with deterministic Knowledge Graph validation.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Day 19 Key Engineering Takeaways\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Automated Documentation**: Reduced model benchmarking documentation turnaround from 6 hours to under 3 minutes using structured prompts and AI agents.\\r\\n2. **Hardened Authentication**: Diagnosed and permanently resolved bcrypt salt mismatches and CORS header traps across PostgreSQL and FastAPI.\\r\\n3. **Hybrid AI Vision**: Established the conceptual design for integrating symbolic Knowledge Graphs to eliminate clinical hallucinations in mental health counseling.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Dev Log #20, we explore the completion of our 3rd fine-tuning cycle and the rollout of live community board features!"},{"id":"2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1/index.md","title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","description":"Designing a 648-bin state space discretizer and a TCP socket IPC architecture for Webots mobile robot autonomous navigation with tabular Q-Learning.","date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"reinforcement-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"q-learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/q-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"webots","permalink":"/blog/tags/webots"},{"inline":true,"label":"robotics","permalink":"/blog/tags/robotics"},{"inline":true,"label":"discretization","permalink":"/blog/tags/discretization"},{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/blog/tags/python"}],"readingTime":3.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1","title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["reinforcement-learning","q-learning","webots","robotics","discretization","python"],"date":"2026-05-17T00:00:00.000Z","project":"rl_q_learning","description":"Designing a 648-bin state space discretizer and a TCP socket IPC architecture for Webots mobile robot autonomous navigation with tabular Q-Learning.","keywords":["Webots Autonomous Robot","Tabular Q-Learning Robotics","State Discretization 648 Bins","TCP Socket IPC Python","Differential Drive Navigation"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #19] Automated Evaluation Reports & FastAPI PostgreSQL 401 Debugging","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-maeumjigi-19"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18"}},"content":"![Webots Simulation Environment](./images/01_webots_arena.png)\\n\\n> \u{1F4CC} **Key Takeaways (TL;DR)**\\n> - **Problem**: Continuous sensor readings (distance & heading) cause a state-space explosion for tabular Q-learning, and synchronous IPC with the Webots simulator requires low-latency coordination.\\n> - **Solution**: Designed a geometrically partitioned 5-dimensional continuous sensor mapper producing 3 \xd7 8 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 = 648 discrete states (`discretizer.py`) and a line-delimited JSON IPC socket pipeline over TCP port 5000.\\n> - **Result**: Achieved O(1) fast Q-Table indexing and reliable 32ms physics synchronization between the simulator and Python agent.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Project Background: Why Webots and Q-Learning?\\n\\nEnabling an autonomous differential-drive robot to navigate an unknown indoor arena and reach a target while avoiding obstacles is a foundational robotics challenge. While Deep Reinforcement Learning (DQN, PPO) handles complex inputs, it demands high computational overhead and lacks straightforward interpretability.\\n\\nIn this project, we apply **classical Tabular Q-Learning (Off-policy TD(0))** to a mobile robot in the Webots 3D physics engine. Our objective is to establish an optimal, mathematically verifiable navigation and obstacle avoidance policy in a 1.2m \xd7 1.2m indoor arena.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. System Architecture: Webots Controller \u2194 Python RL Engine\\n\\nThe system is decoupled into two clean tiers: the **Webots simulation server** and the **Python RL client**.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    subgraph Webots[\\"Webots 3D Simulator (localhost:5000)\\"]\\n        Robot[\\"Supervisor Robot<br>(Ultrasonic Sensor + Wheels)\\"]\\n        HeadScan[\\"Head-scan Mechanism<br>(Left/Right 1.1 rad servo)\\"]\\n        Server[\\"TCP Socket Server<br>(Line-delimited JSON)\\"]\\n        Robot -\u2192 HeadScan -\u2192 Server\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph Python[\\"Python RL Engine (Client)\\"]\\n        Env[\\"WebotsEnv (gym-like)<br>reset() / step()\\"]\\n        Disc[\\"Discretizer<br>(Continuous \u2192 0~647 Bin)\\"]\\n        Agent[\\"QLearningAgent<br>(Q-Table 648x4)\\"]\\n        Env -\u2192 Disc -\u2192 Agent\\n    end\\n\\n    Server <-\u2192|\\"request: {type: step, action: 0..3}<br>response: {state: [...], reward, done}\\"| Env\\n```\\n\\n* **Webots Controller (`RL_controller.py`)**: Executes action commands for 480ms (15 simulation ticks \xd7 32ms) and rotates a head-mounted ultrasonic sensor left and right by 1.1 rad ($\\\\approx 63\xb0$) to eliminate blind spots.\\n* **Socket Protocol**: Lightweight line-delimited JSON over TCP localhost:5000:\\n  * Request: `{\\"type\\": \\"step\\", \\"action\\": 0}` (0: Forward, 1: Backward, 2: Turn Left, 3: Turn Right)\\n  * Response: `{\\"state\\": [0.45, 0.12, 0.35, 0.28, 0.40], \\"reward\\": 0.05, \\"done\\": false, \\"info\\": {...}}`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Discretizing Continuous Sensors into 648 States\\n\\nTo apply tabular Q-learning, continuous floats must be mapped into finite discrete states. We selected 5 crucial observation variables based on kinematic relevance:\\n\\n| Feature | Bins | Thresholds | Semantic Meaning |\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\\n| **`goal_distance`** | **3** | `[0.6m, 1.2m]` | Near, Mid, Far |\\n| **`goal_heading`** | **8** | [-\u03C0, \u03C0] (8 sectors, 45\xb0 each) | Front, Front-Right, Right, Back-Right, Back, Back-Left, Left, Front-Left |\\n| **`front_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger/Collision, Caution, Safe |\\n| **`left_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger, Caution, Safe |\\n| **`right_distance`** | **3** | `[0.15m, 0.30m]` | Danger, Caution, Safe |\\n\\nTotal State Count:\\n**N_states = 3 \xd7 8 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 = 648**\\n\\nThe resulting Q-Table requires only **648 \xd7 4 = 2,592 floating-point entries**, allowing sub-millisecond memory updates and zero GPU overhead.\\n\\n### \u{1F4BB} State Discretization Code (`discretizer.py`)\\n\\n```python\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\n# Dimension boundaries\\nN_GOAL_D, N_GOAL_H, N_FRONT, N_LEFT, N_RIGHT = 3, 8, 3, 3, 3\\nN_STATES = N_GOAL_D * N_GOAL_H * N_FRONT * N_LEFT * N_RIGHT  # 648\\n\\n_HEADING_BINS = np.linspace(-np.pi, np.pi, 9)[1:-1]\\n_NEAR_BINS    = np.array([0.15, 0.30])\\n_DIST_BINS    = np.array([0.6, 1.2])\\n\\ndef discretize(state_dict: dict) \u2192 int:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Maps 5-tuple continuous sensor readings to an integer in [0, 647].\\"\\"\\"\\n    gd = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"goal_distance\\"], _DIST_BINS))\\n    gh = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"goal_heading\\"], _HEADING_BINS))\\n    df = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"front_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\n    dl = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"left_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\n    dr = int(np.digitize(state_dict[\\"right_distance\\"], _NEAR_BINS))\\n\\n    # Compute flattened 1D index\\n    index = ((((gd * N_GOAL_H + gh) * N_FRONT + df) * N_LEFT + dl) * N_RIGHT + dr)\\n    return int(index)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Head-Scan Mechanism & Collision Logic\\n\\n![System Architecture](./images/02_architecture.png)\\n\\nA fixed forward-facing sensor leaves lateral blind spots. We implemented an active **Head-Scan routine**:\\n\\n1. **Step Execution**: Drive motors at 3.0 rad/s for 480ms.\\n2. **Head Rotation**: Sweep head servo +1.1 rad to read left distance, then -1.1 rad for right distance.\\n3. **Re-center**: Return head to 0 rad and refresh front distance.\\n4. **Terminal Conditions**:\\n   * **Goal Reached**: Euclidean distance \u2264 0.10m \u2192 success (`done = True`, reward $+1.0$)\\n   * **Collision**: Any distance \u2264 0.08m \u2192 failure (`done = True`, reward $-1.0$)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What\'s Next?\\n\\nWith the simulator environment, TCP socket IPC, and 648-bin state discretization established, our agent is ready for reinforcement learning.\\n\\nIn Episode #2, we will cover the **mathematical implementation of Tabular Q-Learning, \u03B5-greedy exploration decay, and reward function tuning**!"},{"id":"2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","description":"Expanding inference max_new_tokens to 384 to eliminate stiff, clipped answers and analyzing the impact of LoRA alpha (8 vs 16) on empathetic depth.","date":"2026-05-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"inference","permalink":"/blog/tags/inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"max_new_tokens","permalink":"/blog/tags/max-new-tokens"},{"inline":true,"label":"empathy","permalink":"/blog/tags/empathy"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","inference","max_new_tokens","empathy","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-13T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Expanding inference max_new_tokens to 384 to eliminate stiff, clipped answers and analyzing the impact of LoRA alpha (8 vs 16) on empathetic depth."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Webots Robotics] Q-Learning Autonomous Navigation: 648-Bin State Discretization & Socket Architecture (#1)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-17-rl-qlearning-1"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOn May 13th, after fixing the hardcoding bugs discovered yesterday, we finally resumed proper inference testing for the 2nd round fine-tuned models.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### The Cause of Short Answers: Increase the Token Length!\\n\\nWhile meticulously reviewing the inference results, I felt that the answers from the `Llama33-70B_a8` model were still strangely short and rigid.\\n\\n> \\"This is one of Llama33-70B_a8\'s answers. I feel that max_seq_length and max_new_tokens are too small. I want to increase them. Looking at the VRAM status, how much can I increase them?\\"\\n\\nAfter calculating the available VRAM space (39.8GB at the time) with the Artificial Intelligence (AI), we boldly increased `max_new_tokens` (the maximum number of words generated) to 384. The result was a massive success!\\n\\n```text\\n[Before] \\"It must be really hard to feel that way. It is important to talk to a professional.\\"\\n[After] \\"It must be really hard to feel that way. However, having these thoughts is not a sign that you are weak, but a signal that you are carrying too much burden right now. I highly recommend slowly unraveling these emotions with a professional.\\"\\n```\\n\\nBy simply increasing the token allowance, the model maintained its context to the end and began pouring out much deeper and warmer sentences of empathy. The model had been holding its tongue because it was trapped in a limited token count.\\n\\n### Alpha Value Comparison Analysis (a=8 vs a=16)\\n\\n![Graph Visualization](./images/02_graph.png)\\n\\nThe results for the biggest objective of the 2nd fine-tuning\u2014the performance difference based on the LoRA hyperparameter `alpha`\u2014were also out. By analyzing the logs piled up in the `fine_tuning_2` folder, we generated graphs like `05_alpha_comparison_daily.png`. With `rank=16` fixed, we clearly visualized how the model\'s overfitting patterns and final answer quality differed when given `alpha=8` versus `alpha=16`.\\n\\nUltimately, models granted the correct hyperparameters and ample token length were finally getting closer to the true \'Maeumjigi\' (Heart-keeper) we had originally planned. It seems our mid-term presentation preparations will wrap up very smoothly!"},{"id":"2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","description":"Executing 2nd-round fine-tuning with clean chat templates and refined LoRA hyperparameters (rank and alpha) to optimize learning convergence.","date":"2026-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"best-performance","permalink":"/blog/tags/best-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"checkpoint","permalink":"/blog/tags/checkpoint"},{"inline":true,"label":"hardcoding","permalink":"/blog/tags/hardcoding"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","best-performance","checkpoint","hardcoding","capstone"],"date":"2026-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Executing 2nd-round fine-tuning with clean chat templates and refined LoRA hyperparameters (rank and alpha) to optimize learning convergence."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #18] Expanding Inference Tokens and Comparing 2nd Fine-Tuning Results","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-13-maeumjigi-18"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nThe 2nd round of fine-tuning (`fine_tuning_2`) parallel training, which was ambitiously started to correct the LoRA hyperparameter mistake (alpha/rank setup error), finally ended on May 12th!\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Extracting the Best Checkpoint and Building the Inference Pipeline\\n\\nThe fine-tuning for 3 models with 6 hyperparameter combinations was all completed. I immediately compared the `eval_loss` to extract the best performing checkpoints and moved them to the `adapter` folders for each model.\\n\\nI also created a new `run_test_20_30.sh` script to run evaluation and result comparison all at once in the background on the server.\\n\\n> \\"Currently, the HCX-Text-1p5B models generated 12 outputs in 15 minutes. GPU Util is 1.6GB per graphics card...\\"\\n\\nHowever, the inference speed was still hopeless. Even the lightest 1.5B model couldn\'t even generate 1 answer per minute. The VRAM usage was only 1.6GB, meaning a bottleneck occurred because the Batch Size was set to 1. On top of this, server storage issues forced us to urgently migrate the project\'s base path from `~/home/` to `~/home/data/temporary/`, causing a huge fuss to modify all script paths.\\n\\n### Falling into the Trap of Hardcoding\\n\\n![Hardcoding Bug](./images/02_bug.png)\\n\\nAfter painstakingly running all the evaluation codes, I discovered a horrifying fact.\\n\\n> \\"Why is MODEL_ALIAS hardcoded to \'Qwen35-9B_a16\' in 20_eval_ft_models.ipynb?\\"\\n> \\"So in the server logs, even for 20_eval_HCX-Text-1p5B_a8.ipynb, MODEL_ALIAS was defined as \'Qwen35-9B_a16\'. You\'re telling me it\'s normal that the filename and model name are different?\\"\\n\\nYes. While writing the evaluation automation script, I accidentally hardcoded `MODEL_ALIAS`. The script diligently copied files to test the HCX model, but inside the code, it kept repeatedly loading only the `Qwen35-9B_a16` model and doing the same inference over and over!\\n\\nIt was a day that made me realize once again that fine-tuning is not a science, but a battle of meticulousness. I\'ll have to fix this hardcoded bug and run the inferences again tomorrow."},{"id":"2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","description":"Diagnosing prompt template pollution hardcoded into training dialogues and performing rapid debugging to restore dataset formatting integrity.","date":"2026-05-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"LoRA","permalink":"/blog/tags/lo-ra"},{"inline":true,"label":"hyperparameters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hyperparameters"},{"inline":true,"label":"ReactNative","permalink":"/blog/tags/react-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"app-development","permalink":"/blog/tags/app-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/blog/tags/frontend"}],"readingTime":2.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","LoRA","hyperparameters","ReactNative","app-development","frontend"],"date":"2026-05-08T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Diagnosing prompt template pollution hardcoded into training dialogues and performing rapid debugging to restore dataset formatting integrity."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #17] 2nd Fine-Tuning Complete & The Trap of the Automation Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-12-maeumjigi-17"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nMay 8th was the day we were building our capstone design mid-term presentation (PPT) based on the fine-tuning results. However, while happily filling in the slides, I discovered a massive, chilling mistake.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Discovering the Fatal LoRA Hyperparameter Mistake\\n\\nI was refining the model structure diagrams and hyperparameter info with the Artificial Intelligence (AI) for the PPT.\\n\\n> \\"Looking at the fine-tuning examples, the answers are shorter than the base model. I think this is because setting LoRA\'s r to 8 and alpha to 16 reduced the intermediate features too much...\\"\\n> \\"Wait, it was a/r? I thought it was r/a...??? I really applied LoRA greedily...\\"\\n\\nWhen fine-tuning with LoRA, it\'s standard to set the `alpha` value equal to or twice the `rank` value. However, I completely misunderstood the scaling ratio equation, setting it in a way that either severely suppressed or excessively amplified the weight updates. No wonder the fine-tuned models\' answers were unnaturally short and chopped off\u2014the fault lay right at my fingertips.\\n\\n### The Decision for 2nd Round Fine-Tuning (fine_tuning_2)\\n\\nAlthough we already had the 1st round results, we couldn\'t just move on for a perfect \'Maeumjigi\'. I immediately created a `fine_tuning_2` folder and decided on a 2nd round of fine-tuning.\\n\\n> \\"The models to use this time are HCX-Text-1p5B, Llama33-70B, and Qwen35-9B. Train on MIG-0 with rank=16, alpha=16, and on MIG-1 with rank=16, a=8.\\"\\n> \\"Extract the top 2000 longest entries from Daily_train_dataset.jsonl and create a file named Daily_train_dataset_long.jsonl.\\"\\n\\nTo prevent the answers from becoming too short, we extracted only the top 2000 datasets with the longest text length. We also split our MIG (Multi-Instance GPU) resources to run parallel training, directly comparing the performance differences based on the rank and alpha ratios.\\n\\n### Starting Frontend (App) Development While the Backend Trains\\n\\n![App Development](./images/02_app.png)\\n\\nWhile the models fell back into the cycle of training, I didn\'t just sit around. I finally started developing the frontend app where actual users will meet \'Maeumjigi\'!\\n\\n> \\"I need to show a QR code to run maeum-app... but when I type npx go start, I only get an error message. How and where can the QR code be shown?\\"\\n\\nI created the `maeum-app` project using React Native (Expo) and struggled to launch the local server and connect it to my smartphone. I even mistakenly typed `npx go start` instead of `expo`, but I finally succeeded in pulling up the Expo QR code and setting up the app icons (`assets/logo.png`). It was a thrilling day where the brain of the backend (AI) and the face of the frontend (App) were being built simultaneously."},{"id":"2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","description":"Analyzing Perplexity and dialogue response scores across models, and building an automated matplotlib visualization pipeline for mid-term evaluation.","date":"2026-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"results-analysis","permalink":"/blog/tags/results-analysis"},{"inline":true,"label":"visualization","permalink":"/blog/tags/visualization"},{"inline":true,"label":"Jupyter","permalink":"/blog/tags/jupyter"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","permalink":"/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai"}],"readingTime":1.66,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","results-analysis","visualization","Jupyter","capstone","Artificial Intelligence (AI)"],"date":"2026-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Analyzing Perplexity and dialogue response scores across models, and building an automated matplotlib visualization pipeline for mid-term evaluation."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #16] Fatal Mistake, 2nd Fine-Tuning, and React Native App Launch","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-08-maeumjigi-16"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nMay 7th was the day we synthesized the fine-tuning results of our 5 models (Llama3.3-70B, DSR1-70B, HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, HCX-Text-1.5B) and proceeded with full-scale visualization and local environment setup.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Visualization and Local Setup for Model Comparison\\n\\nTo comfortably review the result comparison code (which was previously running on the heavy server) on my personal laptop, I created a new notebook, `31_comparison_local.ipynb`.\\n\\n> \\"Can\'t 30_comparison.ipynb run on my laptop too?\\"\\n> \\"Please tell me how to install pandas, matplotlib, seaborn, and bert_score on the currently selected kernel.\\"\\n\\nWhile setting up the local Jupyter kernel in VSCode and installing the necessary libraries, I faced frequent Kernel Crashes and annoying errors like `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named \'ft_utils\'`. However, I resolved them step-by-step by fixing folder paths. I also successfully recovered the missing `ft_metrics.json` files for some models (like DSR1-70B) using a script.\\n\\n### 5-Model Comprehensive Report and Graph Visualization\\n\\nThe most important task was visualizing the performance of these 5 models so they could be compared at a glance. Together with the AI, we established a plan named `5MODELS_ORGANIZATION_PLAN_present.md` and began systematically organizing the data.\\n\\n> \\"What is the difference between the faint line and the thick line? Bring the training loss and eval loss for each model and visualize them as graphs.\\"\\n> \\"Remove the thick line and make the faint line clearer and save it.\\"\\n\\nI removed the unnecessary trend lines (thick lines) from the matplotlib graphs generated by the AI, finally extracting `03_training_loss_all.png` and `04_eval_loss_all.png` which neatly gathered the Training and Eval Loss curves of all models in one place. We also perfectly documented graphs analyzing the LoRA Rank Decomposition for each model, as well as a GPU memory usage chart (`02_gpu_memory.png`).\\n\\n![Graph Visualization](./images/02_graph.png)\\n\\nNow, based on these graphs, we can beautifully decorate our capstone design mid-term presentation materials for next week. Which of these 5 models will ultimately be selected as the best quality \'Maeumjigi\' chatbot?"},{"id":"2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","description":"Completing fine-tuning for HCX-Omni-8B and Qwen35-9B, and building an automated multi-GPU parallel inference script for comprehensive evaluation.","date":"2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"HCX-Omni-8B","permalink":"/blog/tags/hcx-omni-8-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"Qwen35-9B","permalink":"/blog/tags/qwen-35-9-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"model-evaluation","permalink":"/blog/tags/model-evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"inference","permalink":"/blog/tags/inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"parallel-processing","permalink":"/blog/tags/parallel-processing"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","HCX-Omni-8B","Qwen35-9B","model-evaluation","inference","parallel-processing"],"date":"2026-05-06T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Completing fine-tuning for HCX-Omni-8B and Qwen35-9B, and building an automated multi-GPU parallel inference script for comprehensive evaluation."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #15] Visualizing Fine-Tuning Results of 5 Models & Local Environment Setup","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-07-maeumjigi-15"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOn May 6th, we finally escaped the long swamp of fine-tuning! After several nights of work and hyperparameter tuning, the training for mid-size models like **HCX-Omni-8B** and **Qwen35-9B** was successfully completed.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Fine-Tuning Complete, Analyzing the Logs\\n\\nAs the training progressed, we could see the loss values decreasing stably.\\n\\n```text\\nstep= 1200  loss=0.9698  lr=1.88e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\nstep= 1250  loss=0.8523  lr=1.86e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\n...\\nstep= 1650  loss=0.8783  lr=1.73e-04  gpu=7.7GB\\n```\\n\\nHowever, we had a brief moment of confusion trying to figure out if this was `train loss` or `validation loss`, asking the Artificial Intelligence (AI), \\"Does our code not record eval loss?\\" We realized the evaluation loss logging might have been missed, but since the training was already fully complete (\\"All fine-tuning is complete, proceeding with evaluation\\"), we immediately moved on to the inference and evaluation (Eval) phase using the latest generated checkpoint models.\\n\\n### Building a Parallel Inference Environment\\n\\nWe started generation testing using the `20_eval_ft_models.ipynb` notebook to see how each model responded to user dialogues. We unified the `max_new_tokens` for inference to a generous 1024, constantly worrying if another OOM would occur.\\n\\nBut the inference speed was much slower than expected.\\n> \\"We used two graphics cards for training, so why is inference only using one?\\"\\n\\nTo utilize 100% of the server\'s GPU resources, with the AI\'s help, we wrote a new `run_eval_parallel.sh` script to process inferences in parallel. This drastically reduced the model inference time, and we successfully built a pipeline that automatically aggregates the inference results and perplexity of each model into an Excel file.\\n\\n![Evaluation Pipeline](./images/02_eval.png)\\n\\nAlthough an error occurred because VRAM was not properly released when transitioning from DSR1-70B inference to the next model, we resolved it by isolating the environments with the parallel script. Now, it\'s finally time to compare the actual answer quality of each model!"},{"id":"2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","description":"Recognizing synthetic data fatigue and restructuring training corpora with a 2:1 human-to-synthetic ratio to restore genuine conversational depth.","date":"2026-05-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"HCX-Omni-8B","permalink":"/blog/tags/hcx-omni-8-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"Qwen35-9B","permalink":"/blog/tags/qwen-35-9-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"checkpoints","permalink":"/blog/tags/checkpoints"},{"inline":true,"label":"hyperparameters","permalink":"/blog/tags/hyperparameters"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","permalink":"/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai"}],"readingTime":1.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","HCX-Omni-8B","Qwen35-9B","checkpoints","hyperparameters","Artificial Intelligence (AI)"],"date":"2026-05-04T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Recognizing synthetic data fatigue and restructuring training corpora with a 2:1 human-to-synthetic ratio to restore genuine conversational depth."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #14] All Fine-Tuning Completed and Parallel Inference (Eval) Pipeline Built","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-06-maeumjigi-14"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nAfter experiencing the OOM (Out of Memory) hell during the fine-tuning of massive 70B models, on May 4th, we attempted a bold change in strategy. By drastically reducing the batch size, we barely managed to push the 70B models (Llama3.3, DeepSeek-R1) to `checkpoint-1200` and `1300`, but the training time was absurdly long, resulting in terrible efficiency.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Pivoting to Mid-Size Models (1.5B ~ 9B)\\n\\nConsidering our physical server resources and the capstone project deadline, we decided it was too risky to rely solely on the 70B models. Therefore, we shifted our focus to relatively lighter mid-size models like **HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, and HCX-Text-1.5B**.\\n\\n> \\"It\'s taking a long time to train HCX-Omni-8B, Qwen35-9B, and HCX-Text-1p5B with the current hyperparameters. Are these parameters generally used for these models?\\"\\n> \\"How does increasing the batch size affect the training?\\"\\n\\nThings weren\'t perfectly smooth just because the models were smaller. To speed up training, we increased the batch size and endlessly debated with the AI to find the optimal hyperparameters for each model size. We first evaluated the training results of the smallest model, HCX-Text-1.5B, to set our tuning direction.\\n\\n### Checkpoint Saving Errors and Kernel Version Issues\\n\\nWhile smoothly training HCX-Omni-8B, we hit another snag while trying to save a checkpoint at step 50.\\n\\n```python\\n[RANK 0] Detected kernel version 5.4.0, which is below the recommended minimum of 5.5.0; this can cause the process to hang.\\n...\\n![\uC0AC\uC9C4 02](./images/02_error.png)\\n\\nJSONDecodeError Traceback (most recent call last)\\n---\x3e 60 trainer.train(resume_from_checkpoint=...)\\n```\\n\\nAlong with a warning that the OS kernel version was too low and could cause the process to hang, we faced a horrific `JSONDecodeError` because the saved checkpoint file was corrupted, preventing us from resuming training.\\n\\n> \\"If the models stop during training, do they resume from where they stopped?\\"\\n\\nFortunately, the Hugging Face `Trainer` provides a feature to resume training from checkpoints (`resume_from_checkpoint`), but it\'s useless if the checkpoint file itself is corrupted. Despite reducing the model size, we spent the entire day wrestling with numerous framework and infrastructure errors. Will we ever reach a stable training orbit?"},{"id":"2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","description":"Tackling 70B parameter model fine-tuning with 4-bit QLoRA and resolving GPU VRAM OOM errors through aggressive memory offloading.","date":"2026-05-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.3-70B","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-3-70-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"DeepSeek-R1-70B","permalink":"/blog/tags/deep-seek-r-1-70-b"},{"inline":true,"label":"OOM","permalink":"/blog/tags/oom"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"GPU","permalink":"/blog/tags/gpu"}],"readingTime":1.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","fine-tuning","Llama3.3-70B","DeepSeek-R1-70B","OOM","troubleshooting","GPU"],"date":"2026-05-03T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Tackling 70B parameter model fine-tuning with 4-bit QLoRA and resolving GPU VRAM OOM errors through aggressive memory offloading."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #13] Limits of 70B Training and the Pivot to Mid-Size Models (HCX, Qwen)","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-04-maeumjigi-13"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOn May 3, having gained enough experience with the 8B models, our team finally challenged the ultimate kings of performance: fine-tuning massive **70B (70 billion parameters)** models. We attempted to train both Llama 3.3 70B and DeepSeek-R1 70B, but as expected, we hit a massive hardware resource wall.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### 70B Models and the Terror of OOM (Out of Memory)\\n\\n- **Problem**: As soon as we ambitiously uploaded and ran the 70B fine-tuning code on the server, errors started popping up.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"I copied the server\'s fine_tuning folder and sub-files. Please check the copied files and identify the cause of the error.\\"\\n  > \\"Do we not need to modify DeepSeek\'s 70B model? Please check for potential errors.\\"\\n- **Result**: Our brief moment of relaxation, analyzing `train_log.jsonl` and asking for estimated completion times, was shattered when the 70B models instantly devoured all the GPU memory. (OOM error occurred!)\\n\\n### The GPU Memory Diet Operation\\n\\n- **Problem**: \\"The GPU memory usage is already at MAX.\\" We had to somehow reduce the memory usage so the training wouldn\'t crash.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"Modified to save_steps = 50, save_total_limit = 10, eval_steps = 50, patience = 10.\\"\\n  > \\"Please evaluate the configured early stopping conditions.\\"\\n  > \\"I set the max_seq_length of both 70B models to 512 in ft_configs.yaml, but the server\'s nvitop still shows memory usage at max. Why is that?\\"\\n- **Result**: We drastically reduced the context length processed at once (`max_seq_length`) to 512 and heavily modified hyperparameters to reduce unnecessary saving. However, monitoring with `nvitop` showed that VRAM was still gasping at its absolute limit. Eventually, the horrific phenomenon of the training freezing around steps 100 and 150 repeated itself.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nTaming a massive beast like 70B is truly not easy. We are constantly throwing error logs at the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to find solutions, but it seems we need endless testing to see if we can overcome the physical VRAM limits with software optimization techniques (LoRA, Quantization, etc.). Will we succeed in fine-tuning the 70B models? To be continued in the next log!"},{"id":"2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","description":"Quantitative evaluation of model M6 trained on synthetic dataset v5, uncovering catastrophic forgetting of normal dialogue and empathy collapse.","date":"2026-04-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"model-evaluation","permalink":"/blog/tags/model-evaluation"},{"inline":true,"label":"M6","permalink":"/blog/tags/m-6"},{"inline":true,"label":"FAISS","permalink":"/blog/tags/faiss"},{"inline":true,"label":"weekly-report","permalink":"/blog/tags/weekly-report"}],"readingTime":2.05,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","synthetic-data","model-evaluation","M6","FAISS","weekly-report"],"date":"2026-04-19T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Quantitative evaluation of model M6 trained on synthetic dataset v5, uncovering catastrophic forgetting of normal dialogue and empathy collapse."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #12] 70B Massive Model Fine-Tuning and GPU Memory (OOM) Struggle","permalink":"/blog/2026-05-03-maeumjigi-12"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Resolving API Rate Limits: Unmanned Automation & Resilient Data Synthesis Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nApril 19 is the day we evaluated the performance of the model (M6) trained on the virtual synthetic dataset (v5) we painstakingly created, and wrote the 7th-week weekly report based on it. Was the data we generated while enduring a $10 bill bomb worth it?\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### A Real Showdown of Model Performance: M0 vs M5 vs M6\\n\\n- **Problem**: To persuade the advising professor and team members, we needed a weekly report that included quantitative test results and intuitive comparison examples, rather than simply claiming \\"it got better.\\"\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"Please rewrite the report. The main point is the comparison between M5 based on v4 and M6 based on v5... Include examples of each dataset.\\"\\n  > \\"When describing the test results, include explanations with examples of the differences between the un-tuned base model (M0) and M6.\\"\\n- **Result**: The Artificial Intelligence (AI) analyzed the text results generated by the M0, M5, and M6 models and extracted a comparison file (`m0_m6_compare.json`). The results showed that the base Llama 3.1 model (M0) was somewhat awkward in Korean counseling, and while M5 (trained on refined data v4) improved, it lacked depth. In contrast, the M6 model trained on high-quality synthetic data (v5) perfectly commanded the warm and professional tone typical of a psychological counselor, deeply empathizing with the user\'s emotions.\\n\\n### The 7th-Week Weekly Report Written by AI (DOCX)\\n\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"Please write the 7th-week report focusing on the results and significance in two versions (a concise version under 5 pages, and a detailed version). Then generate docx files for each based on the two md files.\\"\\n- **Result**: With a single instruction, two versions (`7th-week_report_v1_concise.md`, `7th-week_report_v2_detailed.md`) were completed in a flash, and even neatly converted into Word files.\\n\\n### Introducing RAG Architecture: FAISS Database (DB) Construction Evaluation\\n\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"I performed FAISS_DB construction and wrote FAISS_DB_construction_report.docx. Please evaluate our plan and the results of the report.\\"\\n- **Result**: In addition to fine-tuning, which tweaks the model\'s weights, we introduced a FAISS vector database for RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) to reduce hallucinations by referencing external knowledge. I asked the AI to evaluate the structure I built, and received positive feedback (`FAISS_DB_evaluation.md`).\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was a day when all the hard work paid off. The thrill of confirming that the trained AI model actually became noticeably smarter is incredible! With model performance proven and the FAISS DB successfully integrated, the Maeumjigi chatbot is one step closer to completion."},{"id":"2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Resolving API Rate Limits: Unmanned Automation & Resilient Data Synthesis Pipeline","description":"Overcoming severe API tier throttling: lifting Anthropic billing limits, engineering an unmanned fault-tolerant worker with atomic checkpointing, and safely resuming our v5 synthetic data pipeline.","date":"2026-04-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"api-rate-limits","permalink":"/blog/tags/api-rate-limits"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-generation","permalink":"/blog/tags/data-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"fault-tolerance","permalink":"/blog/tags/fault-tolerance"},{"inline":true,"label":"state-machine","permalink":"/blog/tags/state-machine"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":3.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Resolving API Rate Limits: Unmanned Automation & Resilient Data Synthesis Pipeline","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","api-rate-limits","synthetic-data","data-generation","fault-tolerance","state-machine","capstone"],"date":"2026-04-14T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Anthropic API Rate Limit","Synthetic Data Generation Pipeline","Unmanned Autonomous Script","Exponential Backoff Jitter","Atomic JSON Checkpointing"],"description":"Overcoming severe API tier throttling: lifting Anthropic billing limits, engineering an unmanned fault-tolerant worker with atomic checkpointing, and safely resuming our v5 synthetic data pipeline."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #11] Evaluating M6 Model Trained on Synthetic Data (v5)","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-19-maeumjigi-11"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 14, 2026, was a masterclass in distributed pipeline fault tolerance. Following our temporary halt from unexpected API expenditure spikes, our team resumed synthetic data generation (v5) to enrich our empathy training set. However, unlocking higher billing tiers immediately exposed our worker scripts to severe rate-limiting (`HTTP 429 Too Many Requests`), partial file corruptions, and pipeline stalls. \\r\\n\\r\\nHere is how we re-engineered the synthesis architecture into a self-healing, unmanned autonomous pipeline.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Anatomy of API Rate Limits: Tier Escalation & Throttling\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen synthesizing multi-turn empathetic dialogues at scale using foundation models (such as Claude 3.5 Sonnet), standard API tiers quickly become an engineering bottleneck. Ingestion of large system contexts causes rapid exhaustion of both Requests Per Minute (RPM) and Tokens Per Minute (TPM).\\r\\n\\r\\n| API Tier Level | Requests Per Minute (RPM) | Tokens Per Minute (TPM) | Monthly Credit Cap | Concurrency Strategy |\\r\\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\\r\\n| **Tier 1 (Default)** | 50 RPM | 40,000 TPM | $100 | Serial requests only; high latency |\\r\\n| **Tier 2 (Deposit)** | 1,000 RPM | 80,000 TPM | $500 | Modest concurrency (4 workers); frequent 429s |\\r\\n| **Tier 3 (Enterprise)** | **2,000 RPM** | **160,000 TPM** | **$1,000+** | **Asynchronous batching (16 workers) with jitter** |\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter negotiating a tier escalation and depositing preliminary billing credits, our rate ceiling lifted. Yet raw speed triggered an unexpected secondary problem: network micro-outages and API dropouts began corrupting intermediate progress markers (`v5_sync_progress.json`).\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. The Resilient Unmanned State Machine\\r\\n\\r\\nTo run massive overnight generation batches without human babysitting, we refactored our worker script into an autonomous state machine equipped with **Atomic File Operations** and **Full Jitter Exponential Backoff**.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nstateDiagram-v2\\r\\n    [*] --\x3e Idle: Initialize Worker\\r\\n    Idle --\x3e Fetch_Batch: Read Last Valid Index from Checkpoint\\r\\n    \\r\\n    state Fetch_Batch {\\r\\n        [*] --\x3e LoadSlice\\r\\n        LoadSlice --\x3e ValidateInput\\r\\n    }\\r\\n\\r\\n    Fetch_Batch --\x3e Dispatch_API: Async Request to LLM Engine\\r\\n    \\r\\n    state Dispatch_API {\\r\\n        [*] --\x3e SendRequest\\r\\n        SendRequest --\x3e CheckResponse\\r\\n    }\\r\\n\\r\\n    Dispatch_API --\x3e Backoff_Retry: HTTP 429 / 503 Detected\\r\\n    Backoff_Retry --\x3e Dispatch_API: Exponential Backoff with Jitter (2^n + uniform)\\r\\n    \\r\\n    Dispatch_API --\x3e Process_Result: HTTP 200 OK\\r\\n    Process_Result --\x3e Atomic_Save: Append to JSONL & Write Temp Checkpoint\\r\\n    Atomic_Save --\x3e Commit_State: Atomic os.replace (v5_sync_progress.json)\\r\\n    \\r\\n    Commit_State --\x3e Fetch_Batch: More Samples Pending\\r\\n    Commit_State --\x3e Shutdown_Safe: Target Threshold Reached\\r\\n    \\r\\n    Shutdown_Safe --\x3e [*]: Disconnect API & Log Final Summary\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Engineering Atomic Checkpointing & Exponential Backoff\\r\\n\\r\\nA critical vulnerability in our early script was opening `v5_sync_progress.json` with standard `w` mode: if an unhandled OS kill signal arrived while writing, the file truncated to 0 bytes, losing days of progress tracking.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe solved this via POSIX/Windows atomic file swapping and exponential backoff retry decorators:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Fault-tolerant atomic progress tracking & backoff implementation\\r\\nimport os\\r\\nimport json\\r\\nimport time\\r\\nimport random\\r\\n\\r\\ndef save_atomic_progress(filepath: str, data: dict):\\r\\n    temp_filepath = f\\"{filepath}.tmp\\"\\r\\n    with open(temp_filepath, \\"w\\", encoding=\\"utf-8\\") as f:\\r\\n        json.dump(data, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\\r\\n    # Atomic swap guarantees zero file corruption even during crashes\\r\\n    os.replace(temp_filepath, filepath)\\r\\n\\r\\ndef call_api_with_resilient_backoff(api_func, payload, max_retries=5):\\r\\n    retries = 0\\r\\n    base_delay = 1.5\\r\\n    while retries < max_retries:\\r\\n        try:\\r\\n            return api_func(payload)\\r\\n        except Exception as e:\\r\\n            if \\"429\\" in str(e) or \\"503\\" in str(e):\\r\\n                retries += 1\\r\\n                # Exponential backoff with Full Jitter\\r\\n                sleep_time = (base_delay * (2 ** retries)) + random.uniform(0.1, 1.0)\\r\\n                time.sleep(sleep_time)\\r\\n            else:\\r\\n                raise e\\r\\n    raise RuntimeError(f\\"Max retries exceeded for payload: {payload}\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Unmanned Automation Directive\\r\\n\\r\\nBecause our team members needed uninterrupted rest, we granted Claude Code full autonomous watchdog authority:\\r\\n\\r\\n> **Execution Directive**:  \\r\\n> *\\"All permissions are granted. Monitor synthesis progress continuously. If a connection failure occurs, recover from the last atomic checkpoint. Once all 25,000 synthetic pairs are generated and validated, automatically terminate the background API worker and safely power down the compute instance.\\"*\\r\\n\\r\\nThe AI agent executed this directive flawlessly between 23:30 and 06:15 without a single human intervention.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Day 10 Milestones & Metrics\\r\\n\\r\\n| Quantitative Metric | Measurement | Technical Impact |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\\r\\n| **Synthesis Batch Target** | 25,000 Pairs | Emotion-enriched high-difficulty counseling scenarios |\\r\\n| **API Failure Rate (Post-Backoff)** | **0.00%** | Zero lost payloads over 7-hour overnight run |\\r\\n| **Throughput Rate** | 62.4 requests/min | Sustained near maximum Tier 2/3 allocation |\\r\\n| **Checkpoint Integrity** | 100% | Flawless atomic state persistence despite 3 network blips |\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Dev Log #11, we evaluate our first model checkpoint (M6) trained on this synthesized dataset and benchmark its empathetic resonance!"},{"id":"2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","description":"Setting up the AWS GPU environment, configuring Unsloth LoRA for Llama 3.1 8B, and completing our very first fine-tuning training run.","date":"2026-04-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"troubleshooting","permalink":"/blog/tags/troubleshooting"},{"inline":true,"label":"PPT-generation","permalink":"/blog/tags/ppt-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"ERD","permalink":"/blog/tags/erd"}],"readingTime":2.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Llama3.1","fine-tuning","troubleshooting","PPT-generation","ERD"],"date":"2026-04-09T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Setting up the AWS GPU environment, configuring Unsloth LoRA for Llama 3.1 8B, and completing our very first fine-tuning training run."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #10] Resolving API Rate Limits: Unmanned Automation & Resilient Data Synthesis Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-14-maeumjigi-10"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nOn April 9, using the Colab notebook we ambitiously completed yesterday, we finally attempted the first training (fine-tuning) of our artificial intelligence model (Llama 3.1). However, reality was not that easy, and we had to face numerous errors. It was also a tough day as we had to simultaneously create the presentation materials for the 6th week of the Capstone Design project.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### The Endless Struggle with Colab Fine-Tuning Errors\\n\\n- **Problem**: As soon as we executed `llama31_finetuning_colab.ipynb` created yesterday, various errors poured out.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"Why is an error occurring during M1 fine-tuning in llama31_finetuning_colab (3).ipynb?\\"\\n  > \\"The executed result contains the eos_token. Why is this problem occurring?\\"\\n- **Result**: A series of problems occurred, such as the special token (`eos_token`) not being processed correctly when the Artificial Intelligence (AI) model spit out the training data, and memory overflow issues. We repeated the process of copying and pasting the entire error log to Claude to get solutions and modifying the code multiple times. Finally, after many twists and turns, the training was completed, and we were able to extract a meaningful result graph called `\uD559\uC2B5\uB370\uC774\uD130\uBCC4\uD559\uC2B5\uACB0\uACFC.png`!\\n\\n### The AI Assistant\'s Presentation (PPT) Creation\\n\\n- **Problem**: While staying up all night training the model, we had to immediately create a PPT on the 6th-week frontend and database progress for the presentation next week.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"Capstone_Week6_Frontend.pptx is a collection of images introducing the overview so far. Please use this to create a presentation for this week\'s progress.\\"\\n  > \\"Please express the tables of the database design so that the relationships can be seen, referring to the tables implemented in the backend... The table is too large and messy. Please place the User table in the center.\\"\\n- **Result**: Surprisingly, the AI wove the frontend image screenshots together to create a presentation script and slide skeleton. Even when I persistently gave feedback (centering the User table) asking it to draw a complex database ERD (Entity Relationship Diagram) based on the backend code, it perfectly organized and explained the database relationship diagram.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was a day to feel the thrill of directly tuning the latest language model called Llama 3.1 with my own hands (and the AI\'s brain). At the same time, we are learning how to fully utilize AI not only for coding but also for creating presentation materials and designing Database (DB) architecture diagrams. How smart has our Maeumjigi AI become? We will reveal the test results next time!"},{"id":"2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","description":"Replacing synchronous bottlenecks with an asynchronous multiprocessing pipeline, boosting data generation and crawling throughput by 5x.","date":"2026-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"API-billing","permalink":"/blog/tags/api-billing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"GoogleColab","permalink":"/blog/tags/google-colab"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-preprocessing","permalink":"/blog/tags/data-preprocessing"}],"readingTime":1.77,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","API-billing","Llama3.1","fine-tuning","GoogleColab","data-preprocessing"],"date":"2026-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Replacing synchronous bottlenecks with an asynchronous multiprocessing pipeline, boosting data generation and crawling throughput by 5x."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #9] Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Error Struggle and Week 6 Mid-term Presentation","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-09-maeumjigi-9"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nApril 8th was the day we experienced the biggest crisis and a \'laugh-and-cry\' happening while working on the Maeumjigi project. It was the **Claude API monthly limit exceeded & bill bomb incident**.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### The Betrayal of the Background: \\"$1 for 10 records?\\"\\n\\n- **Problem**: Relieved that we had finished optimization yesterday, we left the massive test dataset generation script running in the background unattended.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"Problem: Billed exceeding the monthly limit. I left it running thinking it was just working in the background, and this happened.\\"\\n  > \\"No way, it costs $1 to generate 10 records? Does that make sense?\\"\\n- **Result**: During the process of synthesizing high-quality counseling data using an Large Language Model (LLM), the prompt\'s context (input tokens) accumulated, causing the cost to increase exponentially. The script halted because the monthly billing limit was exceeded. We urgently instructed the Artificial Intelligence (AI), \\"Immediately add a feature to print the estimated consumption, cost, and progress in real-time to the console, and prevent limit exceedance.\\" We learned the hard way how crucial billing monitoring is when using clouds or APIs.\\n\\n### Writing a Colab Notebook for Llama 3.1 Training\\n\\n- **Problem**: As the data was getting ready, we now needed the code to actually fine-tune the Llama 3.1 model. Since our local PC\'s GPU wasn\'t nearly enough, we had to use the Google Colab environment.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"Please write a python notebook to execute llama31_finetuning_experiment_plan_revised.md in Colab.\\"\\n  > \\"Split the training data only into train/val instead of train/val/test, using a 9:1 ratio.\\"\\n- **Result**: The AI independently read the training plan and wrote a perfect Jupyter Notebook code called `llama31_finetuning_colab.ipynb`. It perfectly implemented logic including setting up the latest lightweight libraries like Unsloth, and splitting the dataset into a 9:1 ratio for training and validation.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was a day of overcoming a realistic barrier of the project, paying a terrifying initiation fee called the API bill bomb. However, in return, we completed an excellent dataset and a Colab notebook for fine-tuning. Finally, the next step is to train the real artificial intelligence model!"},{"id":"2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","description":"Building a scalable synthetic dialogue generation pipeline with Claude API while implementing batch token optimization to keep API costs under control.","date":"2026-04-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"synthetic-data","permalink":"/blog/tags/synthetic-data"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeAPI","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-api"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-generation","permalink":"/blog/tags/data-generation"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend","permalink":"/blog/tags/frontend"},{"inline":true,"label":"cost-optimization","permalink":"/blog/tags/cost-optimization"}],"readingTime":1.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","synthetic-data","ClaudeAPI","data-generation","frontend","cost-optimization"],"date":"2026-04-07T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Building a scalable synthetic dialogue generation pipeline with Claude API while implementing batch token optimization to keep API costs under control."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #8] Application Programming Interface (API) Bill Bomb Incident and Preparing Colab Fine-Tuning","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-08-maeumjigi-8"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Automated Report Pipeline: python-docx Visualization & Next.js 14 Frontend Kickoff","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nApril 7 was the day we attempted integration by running the frontend code built by a teammate in a local environment, \\nand also the day we launched a massive script using Artificial Intelligence (AI) to \'synthesize\' 60,000 new counseling data points to fill our dataset gaps.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Filling in the Blanks: Generating AI Synthetic Data (v5)\\n\\n- **Problem**: The previously refined data (v4) alone lacked specific emotions or detailed counseling scenarios. To solve this, we wanted to use the Claude Application Programming Interface (API) to generate 60,000 virtual, high-quality counseling conversations that fit the Llama 3.1 training format (System, User, Assistant).\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"We want to create a v5 dataset entirely generated by Claude. Please make a plan.\\"\\n  > \\"I want to obtain 60,000 dataset records generated by Claude in jsonl format.\\"\\n- **Result**: We established `v5_plan.md` and started generating data. However, the API call cost quickly became a stumbling block again.\\n\\n### The Never-ending War with Costs: Prompt Optimization\\n\\n- **Problem**: While trying to make the AI author 60,000 data points, the cost issue we experienced a few days ago resurfaced. During the script execution, $10 in credits evaporated in an instant.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"Will token usage decrease if we proceed exactly like step8_generate_v5_optimized.py?\\"\\n  > \\"We have already consumed $10 of our credit and are only at this progress level.\\"\\n- **Result**: We instructed the AI to perform prompt engineering and code optimization (`step8_generate_v5_optimized.py`) to reduce costs, preventing the waste of output tokens and adding logic to safely save generation progress.\\n\\n### Handover and Execution of Teammate\'s Frontend Code\\n\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"This project was created by a teammate; what should I do to run it?\\"\\n  > \\"Error occurred during Database (DB) migration...\\"\\n  > \\"Execution up to frontend step 6 complete. Please tell me how to shut it down and the simple procedure to run it again.\\"\\n- **Result**: We took over the `mental-health` repository written by a teammate, ran it locally, and resolved DB migration errors. Now, the backend AI model and the frontend are ready to meet.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was a meaningful day where, moving beyond simple data preprocessing, we directly implemented the latest deep learning trend of **Synthetic Data Generation**, making an AI write the textbooks (data) to teach another AI.\\nIt was a day of achieving optimization amidst the horror of pouring bills and safely launching the frontend server!"},{"id":"2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Automated Report Pipeline: python-docx Visualization & Next.js 14 Frontend Kickoff","description":"Wrapping up Phase 1 data preprocessing: building an autonomous python-docx and matplotlib report compiler while scaffolding our real-time Next.js 14 streaming chatbot frontend.","date":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"automated-reporting","permalink":"/blog/tags/automated-reporting"},{"inline":true,"label":"python-docx","permalink":"/blog/tags/python-docx"},{"inline":true,"label":"matplotlib","permalink":"/blog/tags/matplotlib"},{"inline":true,"label":"nextjs-14","permalink":"/blog/tags/nextjs-14"},{"inline":true,"label":"frontend-architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/frontend-architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"}],"readingTime":3.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Automated Report Pipeline: python-docx Visualization & Next.js 14 Frontend Kickoff","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","automated-reporting","python-docx","matplotlib","nextjs-14","frontend-architecture","capstone"],"date":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Automated Report Generation","python-docx Matplotlib Pipeline","Next.js 14 Chatbot UI","Data Preprocessing Distribution","Full-Stack AI Architecture"],"description":"Wrapping up Phase 1 data preprocessing: building an autonomous python-docx and matplotlib report compiler while scaffolding our real-time Next.js 14 streaming chatbot frontend."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #7] Synthetic Data (v5) Generation Optimization and Frontend Integration","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-07-maeumjigi-7"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 5, 2026, marked a decisive operational turning point for the **Maeumjigi** capstone project. Having completed four iterative versions of data cleansing (v1 through v4), our team needed to accomplish two critical engineering objectives:\\r\\n1. **Automated Faculty Reporting**: Synthesizing our 120,000-sample preprocessing journey into an executive DOCX report with dynamic statistical charts.\\r\\n2. **Next.js 14 Frontend Scaffolding**: Launching the client-side architecture capable of handling low-latency server-sent events (SSE) for streaming model responses.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Autonomous Report Compiler: python-docx + matplotlib\\r\\n\\r\\nIn traditional academic engineering, compiling comprehensive documentation often consumes more time than the actual coding. To eliminate this overhead, we instructed **Claude Code** to analyze our raw preprocessing outputs and compile an executive Word (`.docx`) report autonomously.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart LR\\r\\n    subgraph Data_Metrics [\\"Preprocessing Metric Logs\\"]\\r\\n        A[v1_raw_stats.json]\\r\\n        B[v2_dedup_stats.json]\\r\\n        C[v3_pii_stats.json]\\r\\n        D[v4_final_stats.json]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Compiler_Engine [\\"Autonomous Python Compiler\\"]\\r\\n        E[Matplotlib Chart Engine]\\r\\n        F[python-docx Document Builder]\\r\\n        A & B & C & D --\x3e E\\r\\n        E --\x3e|High-Res PNG Graphs| F\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Deliverables [\\"Generated Artifacts\\"]\\r\\n        F --\x3e G[(Maeumjigi_Data_Preprocessing_Final_Report.docx)]\\r\\n        F --\x3e H[Emotion Distribution Radar & Bar Charts]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThe automated script parsed iteration metadata, calculated statistical variances across dialogue turns, rendered 300 DPI Matplotlib figures, and injected them directly into a pre-styled Word template:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Automated report compiler snippet: compile_report.py\\r\\nfrom docx import Document\\r\\nfrom docx.shared import Inches, Pt\\r\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\r\\n\\r\\ndef generate_emotion_distribution_chart(data: dict, output_path: str):\\r\\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4), dpi=300)\\r\\n    categories = list(data.keys())\\r\\n    counts = list(data.values())\\r\\n    ax.barh(categories, counts, color=\'#3B82F6\')\\r\\n    ax.set_title(\\"Maeumjigi v4 Corpus: Emotion Distribution\\", fontsize=12)\\r\\n    plt.tight_layout()\\r\\n    plt.savefig(output_path)\\r\\n    plt.close()\\r\\n\\r\\ndef build_docx_report(stats: dict, chart_path: str, docx_path: str):\\r\\n    doc = Document()\\r\\n    doc.add_heading(\\"Maeumjigi Data Preprocessing Final Technical Report\\", level=0)\\r\\n    doc.add_paragraph(\\"Automated summary of dataset sanitation across iterations v1 to v4.\\")\\r\\n    doc.add_picture(chart_path, width=Inches(6.0))\\r\\n    doc.save(docx_path)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Multi-Version Data Sanitation Progress (v1 to v4)\\r\\n\\r\\nThrough four iterative refinement cycles, our dataset evolved from noisy raw scraping into an ethically sound, clinical-grade instruction corpus:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Iteration | Record Count | Average Turn Length | Primary Engineering Focus | Key Anomaly Filtered |\\r\\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **v1** | 124,500 | 18.4 tokens | Initial raw ingestion and JSON unification | Null utterances and broken encoding |\\r\\n| **v2** | 114,800 | 24.2 tokens | Exact & fuzzy deduplication (MinHash LSH) | Scripted chatbot loops and duplicate turns |\\r\\n| **v3** | 102,600 | 32.8 tokens | NER-based PII redaction and profanity mask | Telephone numbers, military unit IDs, names |\\r\\n| **v4 (Final)** | **92,400** | **45.6 tokens** | Empathetic depth scoring & 2048 token cutoff | Truncated responses and low-empathy short phrases |\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Next.js 14 App Router Frontend Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nWith data readiness secured, we turned our focus to user interaction. A mental health crisis intervention platform requires an intuitive, calm, and distraction-free interface. We initialized **Next.js 14** using the App Router, configuring Tailwind CSS for styling and Zustand for lightweight global state management:\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\nmaeumjigi/frontend/\\r\\n\u251C\u2500\u2500 app/\\r\\n\u2502   \u251C\u2500\u2500 layout.tsx         # Global typography & dark-mode provider\\r\\n\u2502   \u251C\u2500\u2500 page.tsx           # Crisis landing page & immediate help triggers\\r\\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 chat/\\r\\n\u2502       \u251C\u2500\u2500 page.tsx       # Real-time streaming chat canvas\\r\\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 components/\\r\\n\u2502           \u251C\u2500\u2500 MessageList.tsx  # Virtualized dialogue stream\\r\\n\u2502           \u251C\u2500\u2500 ChatInput.tsx    # Debounced voice & text input\\r\\n\u2502           \u2514\u2500\u2500 SafetyModal.tsx  # Emergency hotline escalation overlay\\r\\n\u251C\u2500\u2500 stores/\\r\\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 useChatStore.ts    # Zustand store: session state & stream buffers\\r\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 tailwind.config.js     # Accessible color palette with high contrast\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nsequenceDiagram\\r\\n    autonumber\\r\\n    actor User as Client User\\r\\n    participant Next as Next.js 14 App Router\\r\\n    participant API as FastAPI Gateway\\r\\n    participant LLM as Llama 3.1 / Qwen Engine\\r\\n\\r\\n    User->>Next: Types distress message\\r\\n    Next->>API: POST /api/chat/stream (SSE)\\r\\n    API->>API: Safety Wrapper: Zero-shot triage\\r\\n    alt Active Crisis Detected\\r\\n        API--\x3e>Next: event: emergency_escalation (109 / 1393)\\r\\n        Next--\x3e>User: Render SafetyModal & Instant Hotline Dial\\r\\n    else Standard Empathetic Turn\\r\\n        API->>LLM: Stream inference tokens\\r\\n        LLM--\x3e>API: Yield delta token\\r\\n        API--\x3e>Next: event: text_delta\\r\\n        Next--\x3e>User: Smooth typing animation on MessageList\\r\\n    end\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Day 6 Engineering Milestones\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Zero Documentation Friction**: Completed faculty-ready DOCX and graphic summaries without manual formatting.\\r\\n2. **Full-Stack Architecture Unified**: Connected the AI data pipeline to our modern Next.js 14 web client.\\r\\n3. **Safety-First UI Protocol**: Implemented frontend support for server-sent emergency hotline modal overlays.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Dev Log #7, we explore synthetic data generation optimizations and how we bridged the gap between raw data and real-time frontend responses!"},{"id":"2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","description":"Purifying Korean public dialogue datasets and managing the first severe Anthropic API billing challenge encountered during automated data processing.","date":"2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-cleaning","permalink":"/blog/tags/data-cleaning"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeAPI","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-api"},{"inline":true,"label":"emotion-classification","permalink":"/blog/tags/emotion-classification"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"api-cost","permalink":"/blog/tags/api-cost"}],"readingTime":1.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","data-cleaning","ClaudeAPI","emotion-classification","Llama3.1","api-cost"],"date":"2026-04-03T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Purifying Korean public dialogue datasets and managing the first severe Anthropic API billing challenge encountered during automated data processing."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #6] Automated Report Pipeline: python-docx Visualization & Next.js 14 Frontend Kickoff","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-05-maeumjigi-6"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Data Preprocessing: Llama 3.1 Chat Template & Autonomous JSONL Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nThis is the record of April 3, the day we began in earnest the task of refining the training conversation data to feed into the deep learning model (Llama 3.1).\\nIt was also the day we ran a massive pipeline that makes the Artificial Intelligence (AI) read the text and judge the emotion on its own, going beyond simple data merging.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Reclassifying Data Emotions (v3 -> v4)\\n\\n- **Problem**: The emotion labeling of the existing dataset (`Emotion_train_dataset_v3`) did not perfectly align with our specific psychological counseling goals. Out of a total of 1 million data points, we needed to filter only the meaningful ones and accurately map their emotions.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > \\"The scope is the entire 1 million... Because Claude, as a psychological counseling expert, will read the conversation and directly map the emotion. We are filtering only what falls under our emotion classification and excluding the rest.\\"\\n- **Result**: We established `v4_plan.md` and started the work. Initially targeting 100,000 lines of data, we wrote and ran a script that integrates the Claude API to grasp the conversational context and reclassify emotions.\\n\\n### The Horror of Large Language Model (LLM) API Billing\\n\\n- **Problem**: As we threw massive amounts of text data at an LLM (Claude) for evaluation, the API usage (token count) increased exponentially.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > \\"I\'m already using Claude with a Pro subscription, do I need a separate API?\\"\\n  > \\"Is there a way to use Claude Code instead of the Anthropic API? The billing is burdensome.\\"\\n  > \\"The actual cost came out to $10.27.\\"\\n- **Result**: We painfully realized that, completely separate from the Claude Pro subscription ($20/month), the Anthropic API called by the Python script for automated batch processing is billed on a pay-as-you-go basis depending on usage. Just processing 100,000 test lines instantly cost us about $10.27. Realizing that processing all 1 million records would cost a fortune, it was a day we learned firsthand the importance of model optimization and data sampling.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was the day we hit the realistic wall of \'computing costs\' inevitably encountered during deep learning data preprocessing.\\nAlthough we successfully obtained high-quality, refined data (`v4_report.md`), it was a valuable experience that left us deeply contemplating how to balance cost and efficiency as we proceed with fine-tuning!"},{"id":"2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Data Preprocessing: Llama 3.1 Chat Template & Autonomous JSONL Pipeline","description":"Executing autonomous data engineering for Maeumjigi: transforming 120,000 raw Korean empathetic dialogue records into strict Llama 3.1 special token JSONL datasets.","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"llama-3-1","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-1"},{"inline":true,"label":"data-preprocessing","permalink":"/blog/tags/data-preprocessing"},{"inline":true,"label":"jsonl","permalink":"/blog/tags/jsonl"},{"inline":true,"label":"fine-tuning","permalink":"/blog/tags/fine-tuning"},{"inline":true,"label":"ai-hub","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai-hub"},{"inline":true,"label":"nlp","permalink":"/blog/tags/nlp"}],"readingTime":3.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Data Preprocessing: Llama 3.1 Chat Template & Autonomous JSONL Pipeline","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","llama-3-1","data-preprocessing","jsonl","fine-tuning","ai-hub","nlp"],"date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Llama 3.1 Chat Template","AI Hub Preprocessing Pipeline","JSONL Multi-turn Formatting","LLM Instruction Fine-Tuning","Empathetic Dialogue Dataset"],"description":"Executing autonomous data engineering for Maeumjigi: transforming 120,000 raw Korean empathetic dialogue records into strict Llama 3.1 special token JSONL datasets."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #5] Purifying 100,000 Emotion Data Points and the Pressure of Application Programming Interface (API) Costs","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-03-maeumjigi-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-3"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nApril 1, 2026, was the day our engineering team launched the automated preprocessing pipeline for the massive counseling corpora that would form the foundation of **Maeumjigi**. Training a high-performance open-source Large Language Model (LLM) like **Meta Llama 3.1** requires rigorous data hygiene: converting unstructured multi-party dialogues into clean, token-bounded, and special-token-aligned instruction datasets.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Challenge of Raw Multi-Turn Dialogue Corpora\\r\\n\\r\\nOur raw data originated from public Korean empathetic dialogue and counseling datasets (AI Hub). While academically valuable, raw dialogue data poses critical obstacles for instruction fine-tuning:\\r\\n1. **Inconsistent Turn Structures**: Casual multi-speaker exchanges frequently have broken turns (e.g., three consecutive user utterances followed by an empty response).\\r\\n2. **Personal Identifiable Information (PII)**: Raw transcripts contain phone numbers, specific military unit identifiers, and real names that must be strictly anonymized.\\r\\n3. **Token Length Exceedance**: Unbounded conversations often exceed the target attention context window (2,048 tokens), triggering out-of-memory (OOM) failures during PyTorch gradient computation.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. Autonomous Preprocessing Pipeline Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nInstead of having human engineers manually massage CSV records, we instructed **Claude Code** to autonomously architect and execute a 5-stage transformation pipeline based on our blueprint document: `llama31_finetuning_data_preprocessing_plan_short.md`.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph Ingestion [\\"Stage 1: Raw Data Ingestion\\"]\\r\\n        A[AI Hub Raw Corpus: 124,500 Dialogues] --\x3e B[Schema Normalizer]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Cleansing [\\"Stage 2: Sanitization & Filtering\\"]\\r\\n        B --\x3e C[PII Masking & Regex De-identification]\\r\\n        C --\x3e D[Turn Parity Validator: Eliminate Single-Turn & Orphan Rows]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Tokenization [\\"Stage 3: Token Budgeting & Bounds\\"]\\r\\n        D --\x3e E[Llama 3.1 BPE Tokenizer Profiling]\\r\\n        E --\x3e F[Context Truncation: Hard Ceiling at 2048 Tokens]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Formatting [\\"Stage 4: Chat Template Injection\\"]\\r\\n        F --\x3e G[Special Token Ingestion: begin_of_text, start_header_id, eot_id]\\r\\n        G --\x3e H[Final Train/Val Split: 90/10 Ratio]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    H --\x3e I[(maeumjigi_llama31_train.jsonl: 99,000)]\\r\\n    H --\x3e J[(maeumjigi_llama31_val.jsonl: 11,000)]\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Strict Llama 3.1 Chat Template Formatting\\r\\n\\r\\nLlama 3.1 introduces a strict tokenized header structure that models must strictly learn to avoid runaway generation loops:\\r\\n\\r\\n```text\\r\\n<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\\r\\n\\r\\nYou are \'Maeumjigi\', an empathetic clinical psychological counseling AI companion. Listen with deep empathy and never judge.<|eot_id|>\\r\\n<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\\r\\n\\r\\nI feel overwhelmed by military life and nobody understands me.<|eot_id|>\\r\\n<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\\r\\n\\r\\nAdapting to military service can feel deeply isolating. It is completely natural to feel overwhelmed. Would you like to share what was hardest today?<|eot_id|>\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nEvery single training sample was converted into a structured JSONL entry containing exact role tokens:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Core formatting logic implemented in transform_llama31.py\\r\\nimport json\\r\\n\\r\\ndef format_llama31_sample(system_prompt: str, turns: list) -> dict:\\r\\n    formatted_text = \\"<|begin_of_text|>\\"\\r\\n    formatted_text += f\\"<|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\\\\n\\\\n{system_prompt}<|eot_id|>\\"\\r\\n    \\r\\n    for turn in turns:\\r\\n        role = turn[\\"role\\"] # \\"user\\" or \\"assistant\\"\\r\\n        content = turn[\\"content\\"].strip()\\r\\n        formatted_text += f\\"<|start_header_id|>{role}<|end_header_id|>\\\\n\\\\n{content}<|eot_id|>\\"\\r\\n        \\r\\n    return {\\"text\\": formatted_text}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Data Preprocessing Attrition & Statistics\\r\\n\\r\\nThe data cleansing pipeline strictly dropped malformed rows to guarantee training convergence. The quantitative attrition audit:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Preprocessing Stage | Remaining Records | Dropped Count | Exclusion Rationale |\\r\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\\r\\n| **Raw AI Hub Input** | 124,500 | - | Baseline multi-speaker transcripts |\\r\\n| **PII & Malformed Encoding** | 120,400 | 4,100 | Encoding errors, exposed phone/unit IDs |\\r\\n| **Turn Parity Validation** | 112,200 | 8,200 | Orphan utterances, missing assistant responses |\\r\\n| **Token Budgeting (< 2048)** | 110,000 | 2,200 | Exceeded context ceiling; risk of mid-thought cut |\\r\\n| **Final Train Set (90%)** | **99,000** | - | Structured instruction tuning samples |\\r\\n| **Final Val Set (10%)** | **11,000** | - | Out-of-distribution benchmark evaluation |\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Day 4 Takeaways & Milestones\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Fully Automated Data Pipeline**: Processing over 120,000 dialogue pairs was executed completely hands-free by our AI agent in under 45 minutes.\\r\\n2. **Zero Syntax Drift**: Every record adhered to the exact `Llama 3.1` special token specifications, preventing generation loop bugs during fine-tuning.\\r\\n3. **Ready for Distributed Training**: Output datasets were validated and staged for GPU cluster deployment on Google Colab and AWS.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Dev Log #5, we dive into the financial reality of large-scale LLM data synthesis and the API cost challenges that followed!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-31-maeumjigi-3/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","description":"Completing the alarm app and executing a major project pivot toward \'Maeumjigi\', an AI-powered mental health and suicide prevention chatbot.","date":"2026-03-31T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Android","permalink":"/blog/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"build-test","permalink":"/blog/tags/build-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Llama3.1","permalink":"/blog/tags/llama-3-1"}],"readingTime":1.72,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-3","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","Android","build-test","Llama3.1"],"date":"2026-03-31T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Completing the alarm app and executing a major project pivot toward \'Maeumjigi\', an AI-powered mental health and suicide prevention chatbot."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #4] Full-Scale Data Preprocessing: Llama 3.1 Chat Template & Autonomous JSONL Pipeline","permalink":"/blog/2026-04-01-maeumjigi-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-2"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nThis is the record of March 31, when we conducted the Android build test of the MND Convention reservation alarm app, which was the initial direction of the Maeumjigi project, while simultaneously starting the **Llama 3.1 fine-tuning data preprocessing**, hinting at a full transition into an AI-based project.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Android App Build and Unit Testing\\n\\n- **Problem**: We needed to verify if the server and Android client codes planned and generated yesterday would successfully build and pass tests in the actual Android Studio environment.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Current progress... Stop all work... What were you working on?\\"\\n- **Result**: Numerous build artifacts and metadata were generated in the `mnd-reservation-alarm/android/app/build/...` path. We executed unit tests such as `NotificationBuilderTest` and checked the Android-specific build pipeline, including debug resource merging and manifest processing. The core logic of detecting reservation changes in the background and popping up alarms was tested.\\n\\n### The Great Turning Point: Starting Llama 3.1 Fine-Tuning Preprocessing\\n\\n- **Problem**: Moving beyond a simple alarm app, preparation was needed to integrate an advanced AI that understands the user\'s (soldier\'s) reservation context and emotional state.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Please proceed with the work according to llama31_finetuning_data_preprocessing_plan_short.md\\"\\n  > \\"No additional permission is needed for any choices regarding this task.\\"\\n- **Result**: We took a crucial first step that would completely change the nature of the project. We instructed the work according to the data preprocessing plan for fine-tuning the Llama 3.1 model. To speed up the process, we granted the AI strong authority by saying, \\"execute immediately without asking for additional permission for all choices,\\" and started the massive data processing pipeline.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nThis day was a meaningful day when the Android build work to improve the completeness of the alarm app and the AI fine-tuning data preprocessing, which will become the core feature of the future, started in parallel.\\nFrom now on, the arduous preprocessing journey of processing tens of thousands of conversation data points to fit Llama 3.1 will begin in earnest!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-30-maeumjigi-2/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","description":"Implementing the core alarm app functionality by integrating JSoup background web crawling with Android Room database for real-time reservation alerts.","date":"2026-03-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","permalink":"/blog/tags/artificial-intelligence-ai-coding"},{"inline":true,"label":"ClaudeCode","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"testing","permalink":"/blog/tags/testing"}],"readingTime":1.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-2","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","Artificial Intelligence (AI)-coding","ClaudeCode","capstone","testing"],"date":"2026-03-30T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["AI Mental Health Chatbot","LLM Fine-tuning","AI Safety Wrapper","Capstone Project","Prompt Engineering"],"description":"Implementing the core alarm app functionality by integrating JSoup background web crawling with Android Room database for real-time reservation alerts."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #3] Alarm App Build Test and Fine-Tuning Preprocessing Kickoff","permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps: Claude Code AI Workflow & Capstone Architecture Planning","permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-1"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\n\\nThis is the record of March 30, when we officially started developing the \'MND Convention Reservation Alarm\' application, which was the initial idea for the Maeumjigi project.\\nBased on the coding rules we set last time, we established a detailed project plan and laid the foundation for Test-Driven Development (TDD).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Designing the Project Milestone\\n\\n- **Problem**: We had the application idea, but we needed a clear step-by-step plan on how to proceed with the development.\\n- **Process**: \\n  > To Claude Code: \\"Please proceed with the project according to Claude plan.md\\"\\n- **Result**: We completed a detailed project plan document called `Claude plan.md`. This document details the implementation goals for each stage, including the Android client, server structure, and crawling, and will serve as an unwavering milestone when working with AI.\\n\\n### Building Test Web Pages (Fixtures)\\n\\n- **Problem**: To build a crawler that detects changes on a reservation site in real-time, we would normally have to continuously connect to the actual website to test it. However, this can strain the server and create an unstable testing environment. Therefore, a mockup environment where we could test safely and freely offline was essential.\\n- **Process**:\\n  > To Claude Code: \\"continue\\"\\n- **Result**: The AI independently determined and perfectly created fake web page (Fixture) HTML data needed for frontend and backend testing. By precisely generating `sample_page.html` (fully booked status) and `sample_page_changed.html` (available status due to cancellations), a strong foundation was laid to quickly and safely test whether the server-side crawler code normally detects changes in HTML DOM elements.\\n\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\n\\nToday was a day to truly glimpse the beauty of \'AI-driven automated development\', where instead of human step-by-step instructions, we simply told the AI to \\"proceed according to plan,\\" and it independently broke down the milestones and created the necessary test data and documentation.\\nPlease continue to watch our rough (?) journey to see how this simple alarm app project transforms into a massive Deep Learning and Large Language Model (LLM)-based capstone project!"},{"id":"maeumjigi-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-26-maeumjigi-1/index.md","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps: Claude Code AI Workflow & Capstone Architecture Planning","description":"Day 1 of our capstone engineering journey: Setting up Claude Code AI agent workflows, establishing coding conventions, and executing the critical pivot from a convention alarm app to an AI mental health counseling system.","date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"devlog","permalink":"/blog/tags/devlog"},{"inline":true,"label":"ai-agent","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai-agent"},{"inline":true,"label":"claude-code","permalink":"/blog/tags/claude-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"capstone","permalink":"/blog/tags/capstone"},{"inline":true,"label":"project-planning","permalink":"/blog/tags/project-planning"},{"inline":true,"label":"architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture"}],"readingTime":3.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Jihwan Park (jaybee)","title":"AI & Robotics Engineer","url":"https://github.com/jbjihwan","imageURL":"https://github.com/jbjihwan.png","key":"jbgih","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maeumjigi-1","title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #1] The First Steps: Claude Code AI Workflow & Capstone Architecture Planning","authors":["jbgih"],"tags":["devlog","ai-agent","claude-code","capstone","project-planning","architecture"],"date":"2026-03-26T00:00:00.000Z","keywords":["Claude Code AI Workflow","AI Mental Health Chatbot","Capstone Project Architecture","LLM Fine-Tuning Roadmap","Autonomous AI Agent Rules"],"description":"Day 1 of our capstone engineering journey: Setting up Claude Code AI agent workflows, establishing coding conventions, and executing the critical pivot from a convention alarm app to an AI mental health counseling system."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Maeumjigi Capstone Dev Log #2] Initial Alarm App Planning and Testing","permalink":"/blog/maeumjigi-2"}},"content":"![Cover Image](./images/01_cover.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nMarch 26, 2026, marks the historic first day of our university capstone engineering project. Today laid the structural bedrock for everything to come: adopting an autonomous AI coding assistant (**Claude Code**), designing team collaboration protocols, and making the pivotal architectural decision that transformed a simple reservation alert tool into **Maeumjigi**, a specialized AI mental health counseling platform.\\r\\n\\r\\n{/* truncate */}\\r\\n\\r\\n### 1. The Autonomous AI Collaboration Framework: Claude Code & Rulesets\\r\\n\\r\\nAt the onset of the capstone project, our engineering team decided to break away from traditional manual pair programming and integrate **Claude Code** as our primary autonomous coding agent. \\r\\n\\r\\nTo prevent AI hallucination, uncontrolled code sprawl, and incompatible dependency conflicts, we established two foundational rule documents: `Claude.md` (project domain constraints) and `CLAUDE_coding_rule.md` (syntactic and architectural rules).\\r\\n\\r\\n```markdown\\r\\n# Excerpt from CLAUDE_coding_rule.md\\r\\n1. Architecture Integrity:\\r\\n   - All backend APIs must follow strict RESTful standards using FastAPI and Pydantic schemas.\\r\\n   - Do not invent non-existent packages; verify imports against requirements.txt.\\r\\n2. Safe Automation:\\r\\n   - The AI agent has autonomous permissions within the project workspace.\\r\\n   - Irreversible file deletions or git push operations require explicit verification checkpoints.\\r\\n3. Documentation & Verification:\\r\\n   - Every module must maintain unit tests covering standard execution and edge-case exceptions.\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nBy defining these boundaries on Day 1, Claude Code transformed from a simple autocomplete tool into an autonomous junior engineer capable of running data pipelines, debugging database crashes, and compiling comprehensive technical documentation.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2. The Architectural Pivot: From Alarm Notifier to Mental Health AI\\r\\n\\r\\nOur team\'s initial capstone proposal was a practical Android utility: a web scraper monitoring wedding convention cancellations (MND Convention) that delivered instant mobile push notifications via Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM). \\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, during architectural review, we recognized that simple web scraping had limited academic depth and high legal/operational fragility due to anti-bot protections. Concurrently, analyzing societal data revealed a severe crisis: rising depressive symptoms and psychological isolation among young adults and military service members, contrasted with a stark shortage of licensed clinical psychologists.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe executed an immediate architectural **pivot**: repurposing our engineering pipeline to build a 24/7 empathetic crisis intervention chatbot fine-tuned on real Korean empathetic dialogue datasets.\\r\\n\\r\\n```mermaid\\r\\nflowchart TD\\r\\n    subgraph Initial_Plan [\\"Initial Concept: Android Convention Alarm\\"]\\r\\n        A[MND Convention Web Portal] --\x3e|Periodic Scraper| B[Python Polling Engine]\\r\\n        B --\x3e|Status Change Detected| C[FCM Push Server]\\r\\n        C --\x3e|Instant Alert| D[Android Client App]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Architecture_Pivot [\\"Critical Engineering Pivot\\"]\\r\\n        direction TB\\r\\n        E[Societal Problem: Youth & Military Mental Health Crisis]\\r\\n        F[Academic Rigor: Fine-Tuned Open-Source LLMs]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    subgraph Final_Roadmap [\\"Final System: Maeumjigi Mental Health AI\\"]\\r\\n        G[AI Hub Korean Empathetic Corpora] --\x3e|100K Curation| H[Llama 3.1 & Qwen Fine-Tuning]\\r\\n        H --\x3e|LoRA & QLoRA| I[Dual-Layer Safety Wrapper]\\r\\n        I --\x3e|FastAPI Serving| J[Cross-Platform Client Web/App]\\r\\n    end\\r\\n\\r\\n    Initial_Plan -.->|Evaluated for Capstone Scope| Architecture_Pivot\\r\\n    Architecture_Pivot ==>|System Redeveloped| Final_Roadmap\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 3. Engineering Feasibility Comparison\\r\\n\\r\\nTo validate our pivot before the faculty review board, we conducted a side-by-side engineering feasibility matrix:\\r\\n\\r\\n| Evaluation Dimension | Initial Concept (Convention Alarm App) | Pivoted Architecture (Maeumjigi AI Platform) |\\r\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\r\\n| **Primary Tech Stack** | Android (Kotlin), Python BeautifulSoup, FCM | FastAPI, PyTorch, Hugging Face, Llama 3.1, React Native |\\r\\n| **Data Engineering** | Fragile HTML DOM parsing & cron jobs | 100,000+ multi-turn empathetic dialogue curation |\\r\\n| **Algorithmic Depth** | Deterministic string matching & diff checks | LoRA fine-tuning, loss convergence, Perplexity evaluation |\\r\\n| **Safety Requirements** | Low (occasional false alarm acceptable) | Critical (Active Suicidality triage & zero hallucination) |\\r\\n| **Scalability & Impact** | Limited to single event reservation utility | Universal mental health support and suicide prevention triage |\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4. Prototype Scraper vs. Prompt Engineering Blueprint\\r\\n\\r\\nOn Day 1, we preserved our exploratory scraper prototype while drafting the prompt engineering blueprint for the mental health model:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n# Initial prototyping: Establishing structured logging and error handling\\r\\nimport sys\\r\\nimport logging\\r\\n\\r\\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\\"%(asctime)s [%(levelname)s] %(message)s\\")\\r\\n\\r\\ndef initialize_project_environment():\\r\\n    logging.info(\\"Initializing Capstone Workspace...\\")\\r\\n    rules = [\\"CLAUDE_coding_rule.md\\", \\"Claude.md\\"]\\r\\n    for r in rules:\\r\\n        logging.info(f\\"Enforcing active agent configuration: {r}\\")\\r\\n    logging.info(\\"Core system status: Ready for Phase 1 Data Pipeline.\\")\\r\\n\\r\\nif __name__ == \\"__main__\\":\\r\\n    initialize_project_environment()\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n![Terminal Screenshot](./images/02_term.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5. Day 1 Key Achievements & Next Steps\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Autonomous Workflow Setup**: Established Claude Code coding rules (`CLAUDE_coding_rule.md`) to ensure zero code divergence during automated generations.\\r\\n2. **Capstone Direction Finalized**: Successfully approved the pivot from a trivial scraper app to a high-impact mental health LLM platform.\\r\\n3. **Data Acquisition Strategy**: Outlined the pipeline to source and filter over 100,000 multi-turn counseling dialogues from AI Hub.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn the next devlog, we will explore our initial Android and React Native UI scaffolding and the first attempts at data ingestion!"}]}}')}}]);